 290: cannot ship a behavioural change.
 291: 
 292: ---
 293: 
 294: ### B5. ROWS ASSESSED LATE IN THE SESSION -- ready, and none of them blocked
 295: 
 296: **`lcaisalane` (med) -- SIX items, not the three filed.**  codex was given NO list and warned not to
 297: manufacture three; it found four behavioural issues, an initialisation gap and a format mismatch,
 298: and said *"the filed 'three' overstates the initialisation claim."*
 299: *** THE TRAP: the guard is `if (ofs > len)` -- equality passes -- but the rejected arm is `exit(1)`,
 300: so changing only `>` to `>=` EXPANDS the termination. ***  Boundary and disposition are one design
 301: or neither.  It bounded the claim too: *"this is NOT a demonstrated buffer overrun."*
 302: **AND IT FOUND A DOUBLE-COUNT:** `lcabadval`'s scope explicitly EXCLUDES range-guard analysis while
 303: its later note records this exact termination site -- *"extract or reassign that site explicitly
 304: rather than counting it twice."*  **That is an open obligation on both rows.**
 305: *No in-tree regression drives this shim* -- `lca_irqstub_probe.py` reaches the downstream ISA
 306: mapping directly -- so every item is a documented source candidate.
 307: 
 308: **`fbfiqsilent` -- both arms are member 4 ACCEPT, in separate rounds.**  The IRQ arm reads
 309: `odata = d->irq_enable; fatal(...); exit(1);` -- **it computes the correct value and then ends the
 310: host**, the same shape this project already found at that register's neighbour.  The FIQ twin
 311: returns a silent zero, which *"is correct when disabled, but not for every configured state"*.
 312: **This row is NOT blocked on a manual**: `_scratchpad/dc21285.txt` is present, which is why it was
 313: chosen over the three that are.
 314: 
 315: **`sgirebufuninit` (med) -- enumerated, and it is REACHED IN-TREE.**  18 of 64 combinations send
 316: uninitialised bytes to guest memory on PUT, 30 of 64 consume them on GET -- **and the tiled MTE
 317: already constructs CI/2 and CI/8, two of the leaking cells.**  Fix is `uint8_t buf[8] = {0}`, with
 318: no currently-defined path changed.  *** The oracle needs MemorySanitizer, and MSan is INCOMPATIBLE
 319: with ASan -- so that round owes a THIRD sanitizer configuration, not a reuse of `build-asan/`. ***
 320: 
 321: ### B6. *** A CARRIER-LEVEL FACT ESTABLISHED TONIGHT, AND I HAD IT WRONG TWICE BEFORE GETTING IT
 322: ### RIGHT: OPTION-SET MEMBER 5 (FAULT) IS AVAILABLE ON **MIPS AND M88K ONLY**. ***
 323: 
 324: `src/cpus/memory_rw.c`'s device-failure path has exactly two arms, `#ifdef MEM_MIPS` and
 325: `#ifdef MEM_M88K`.  **No ARM, no Alpha, no PPC, no SH** -- every other architecture reaches
 326: `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` with no exception delivered.
 327: 
 328: **Why this belongs in an adjudication packet:** the option set is what every de-fatalisation round
 329: chooses from, and **member 5 is a phantom on most of this tree.**  A round that reaches for FAULT on
 330: an ARM, SH, PPC or Alpha device is reaching for a round that does not exist yet -- the prerequisite
 331: is building delivery, which a seat priced as *"a new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation."*
 332: 
 333: *My own error trail, recorded because it shows how easily this spreads: I first wrote "FAULT is
 334: unavailable on Alpha" onto three `dev_lca.c` rows -- too narrow.  Then, assessing an ARM row, I told
 335: a seat that "member 5 may genuinely be available here, `memory_rw` has an ARM arm."  It does not.
 336: **I was one brief away from spreading the false version across the ARM queue, having just corrected
 337: the Alpha version of the same mistake.**  All three rows now carry the general form.*
 338: 
