 471: 
 472: ## Y. THE LCA CLUSTER -- six rows, one chipset, and a hardware manual that arrived today
 473: 
 474: **This section did not exist when the packet was written.** The Alpha 21066 Hardware Reference
 475: Manual was fetched at a codex citation, read locally, and it changed three rows. The cluster is
 476: `lcabadval` (high), `lcaconfbus1kill` (high), `lcairqstubkill` (high), `lcaconfwidth` (med),
 477: `lcaisalane` (med), `lcashiftub` (low) -- all in `dev_lca.c`, all reachable from
 478: `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, which is the only machine that installs the device.
 479: 
 480: ### What is now established, with its evidence
 481: 
 482: | claim | evidence |
 483: |---|---|
 484: | `IOC_W_MASK` has **13** documented-legal values; the fork accepts **one** per register | Table 6-6, read locally; confirmed by 5 of 6 seats |
 485: | the guards test the **whole 64-bit operand**, while the hardware defines a **12-bit field** and ignores the rest | deepseek raised it, codex settled it from the RES/type-X definition |
 486: | the manual **requires initialising both base registers to zero**, and `W_BASE1` rejects that | codex, S6.4.14 |
 487: | `IOC_TB_ENA` is **not** a functional defect -- both TEN states are accepted | codex |
 488: | `lca_interrupt_assert` is **guest-reachable** via the unconditionally-installed UART on ISA IRQ 4 | codex, path named end to end |
 489: | forwarding **cannot** complete interrupt delivery -- `cd.alpha.irq_asserted` has **no reader**, and `cpu_dyntrans.c` has no Alpha arm | codex, verified by my own census |
 490: | absent-device reads are **not** all-ones on real hardware -- master abort raises an instruction exception | S6.2.18, read locally |
 491: 
 492: ### QUESTION Y1 (scope) -- one round or six?
 493: 
 494: Six rows, one file, one machine, and several share a disposition question (*what should a chipset
 495: register do with a value it does not model?*). **The one-site rule says a round takes ONE site and a
 496: second joins only if it is witnessed, in the same semantic class, and covered by the same detector
 497: with no new oracle.** `lcabadval`'s six arms are arguably one site by that test; the interrupt stubs
 498: are plainly not. **Is `lcabadval` one round, and if so does it take all six arms or only the two
 499: mask arms where the manual gives an enumerated legal set?**
 500: 
 501: ### QUESTION Y2 (a partial repair) -- does `lcairqstubkill` ship?
 502: 
 503: It is HIGH and guest-reachable. But the honest fix forwards an interrupt to a flag **nothing reads**,
 504: because Alpha has no dispatch arm in the execution loop. So the repair converts *"the emulator
 505: terminates"* into *"the interrupt is silently dropped"*.
 506: 
 507: **Is that an improvement worth shipping, or does it launder a missing feature into a silent one?**
 508: The option set has a member for this -- a bounded, latched announcement that the interrupt was
 509: dropped -- which preserves the diagnostic while removing the termination. **I lean that way and I
 510: want it challenged**, because it is also the disposition that lets a round claim to have fixed
 511: something while the machine still cannot take an interrupt.
 512: 
 513: ### QUESTION Y3 (the addition licence) -- the ASM/ASN plumbing
 514: 
 515: `alphatbinegsel`'s coarse repair is right *because* the translation cache carries no ASM tag
 516: (`ALPHA_PTE_ASM` has zero consumers). The manual documents what the tag is for. **Is adding that
 517: plumbing a queue item at all, or is it a feature request wearing a defect's clothes?** The addition
 518: licence says additions need a measured delta or a transferred warrant, never "it cannot hurt".
 519: 
 520: ### QUESTION Y4 -- am I over-fitting to a manual I got this morning?
 521: 
 522: Three rows changed disposition within hours of a PDF landing. **That is either the correct use of a
 523: primary source or an anchor**, and I cannot tell from inside it. The specific worry: I have been
 524: treating *"the manual documents behaviour X"* as evidence that *"a guest will do X"*, and those are
 525: different claims -- the same conflation the `alphatbinegsel` panel caught me making about
 526: *"during ordinary operation"*. **Which of the seven table rows above is doing work it has not
 527: earned?**



