tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31399:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while writing the design brief for `sgirandomcolour' -- i.e. by reading the arm NEXT\nto the one grok pointed at.  **This is a bigger defect than the row that led me to it, and it needs no\nundocumented mode at all.**\n\n`horrible_getputpixel()' declares `uint8_t buf[8];' and NEVER INITIALISES IT.  Verified: no memset in the\nfunction, no initialiser, and the only memsets in the file are the device struct, `rop_used' and `zerobuf'.\nThe two fields that decide how much of `buf' is USED are decoded from the SAME guest-written mode register\nand are INDEPENDENT of each other:\n\n    uint32_t color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK;      /* bits 4-7  */\n    int bufdepth = 1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3);               /* bits 8-9  -> 1, 2, 4 or 8 */\n\nThe colour arms fill a number of bytes fixed by the COLOUR type; the transfer length is `bufdepth'.  Nothing\nanywhere makes them agree -- `grep -rn '>> *8) *& *3' src/devices/' finds the decode, its own #100 comment,\nand the XFER siblings; NO GUARD.\n\n**TWO REACHABLE UNINITIALISED PATHS, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS:**\n\n* **PUT -- host stack INTO guest-visible memory.**  With the depth field = 3 (`bufdepth' 8) NO colour arm\n  writes `buf[4..7]' -- not one of the five, including the correct ones -- and all eight bytes go straight to\n  `memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL)'.  `DE_MODE_TYPE_CI'\n  writes only `buf[0]', so CI at any depth above 1 leaks the rest.\n* **GET -- host stack INTO `*color', which the caller then STORES.**  The read fills only `bufdepth' bytes,\n  then RGB/RGBA/ABGR combine `buf[0..3]'.  Depth field 0 (`bufdepth' 1) with `DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA' reads ONE\n  byte from memory and builds a 32-bit colour from four.  `draw_primitive()' writes that to the destination\n  in the same loop iteration -- **the identical caller-consumes-a-failed-read shape `sgirandompixel' just\n  fixed one arm of**, reached here with every colour mode documented and implemented.\n\n*** THE #100 COMMENT IS THE TELL, AND IT IS ON THE DECLARATION ITSELF. ***  It reads\n`/*  #100: bufdepth can be 8 = 1<<((mode>>8)&3)  */' -- so a previous round worked out that the guest can\ndemand 8 bytes and GREW THE ARRAY to match, which fixed a stack OVERFLOW.  **It did not ask what fills the\nbytes it had just added.**  The array is the right size and the contents are undefined.\n\nSEVERITY: guest-reachable read of uninitialised host stack whose value becomes guest-visible.  In a loop the\nstale bytes are usually the PREVIOUS pixel's, so the everyday symptom is wrong colours; on the first call in\na frame they are whatever the host stack held.  Both mode words are ordinary device registers reached by\nordinary stores, and all seven call sites pass one of them.\n\nNOT YET WITNESSED -- filed at once so the finding is durable.  The witness is expected to be cheap because\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this exact path on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32' and\nneeds only a different mode word: its `MODE_LIN_A = 0x1220' is LIN_A | depth-field 2 (`bufdepth' 4) | RGBA,\nwhich is the MATCHED case and leaks nothing.  `0x1020' is the same line with depth field 0.\n\n*** PASS-1 CORRECTION FROM THE GROK SEAT, VERIFIED AGAINST THE HEADER BEFORE BEING ACCEPTED.  MY PATH-A\nEXEMPLAR WAS THE WEAKER ONE, AND A FULLY DOCUMENTED VEHICLE EXISTS. ***\n\nThe brief drove PATH A with mode `0x1320' -- depth field 3, `bufdepth' 8 -- and called it reachable.  grok:\n*\"BUFDEPTH in the header is 8/16/32 only -- field 3 is undocumented ... PATH A is live on documented `CI' +\n`BUFDEPTH_16/32' (`0x1200'), not only on undocumented depth 8.  The brief's `0x1320' PATH-A vehicle is the\nweaker one.\"*\n\n**CHECKED, not taken on trust** -- `grep -n BUFDEPTH src/include/thirdparty/crmfbreg.h' returns exactly three:\n\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_8   0x00000000   -> depth field 0 -> bufdepth 1 byte\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_16  0x00000100   -> depth field 1 -> bufdepth 2 bytes\n    DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32  0x00000200   -> depth field 2 -> bufdepth 4 bytes\n\n**Field value 3 is documented nowhere**, so the brief's own vehicle relies on an undocumented depth -- while\n`DE_MODE_TYPE_CI' assigns ONLY `buf[0]' and `DE_MODE_BUFDEPTH_32' transfers FOUR bytes, so `0x1200' leaks\n`buf[1..3]' with **every field documented and CI being colour type ZERO**, the reset value.\n\n*(Note the naming trap I had not spotted: the constants are in BITS and the code's `bufdepth' is in BYTES, so\n`BUFDEPTH_32' is 4.  That is why `1 << ((mode >> 8) & 3)' maxes at 8 with only three constants defined.)*\n\n**AND IT SETTLED Q1.3, WHICH I HAD EXPLICITLY LEFT OPEN: the destination is RAM, not a device.**  So `#95's\ndevice-read pre-zero does not run AT ALL on either path -- not merely `does not cover the bytes past len'.\nBoth paths are therefore worse than the brief stated, not better.\n\n**This is the premise-audit opener earning its place for the third recorded time.**  I asked seats to attack\nthe reachability premise; one did, and the correction STRENGTHENS the finding while WEAKENING the exemplar I\nhad chosen -- which is exactly the asymmetry the rule exists to surface.\n\ngrok's other two calls -- **do not join [[sgirandomcolour]]** (my Q6) and **talk HIGH down to MEDIUM** (my Q5)\n-- are NOT actioned here.  They are adjudication questions and the panel is still running; the\nprovisional-adjudication rule says a ruling short of the roster is provisional on its face, so they wait for\nthe full six.\n\n*** PASS-1 BRIEF DEFECT, RAISED INDEPENDENTLY BY ALL THREE PACKET-FED SEATS: I ASSERTED A PREMISE THEY\nCOULD NOT CHECK, AND SAID `SETTLED PREMISES: NONE CARRIED'. ***\n\nNo burns -- all three returned real verdicts on a 31 KB brief -- and all three named the same hole:\n  * **minimax**: *\"the load-bearing premise is NOT CHECKABLE and the brief states it confidently anyway ...\n    That single fact -- the body of `cpus/memory_rw.c' -- is what would settle it, and the brief should not\n    have stated it as confidently as it did.\"*\n  * **glm**: *\"reachability rests on one uncheckable premise\"*; it treated every grep and scan output in the\n    brief as uncheckable and said so.\n  * **deepseek**: caught a SECOND one I had not noticed -- *\"the brief's table for `default'/YCRCB assumes\n    `fatal()' returns\"*.  My PATH-A table lists the `default:' arm as filling `buf[0..3]' and then writing;\n    **if `fatal()' exited, that arm would never reach the write and the row would be wrong.**\n\n**BOTH RESOLVE IN THE FINDING'S FAVOUR, WHICH IS NOT THE POINT.**  Checked at HEAD: `fatal()' in\n`src/core/debugmsg.c' is `va_start / va_debug / va_end' with **no exit and no early-out**, so the arm does\nreach the write; and grok, a FILE-READING seat, settled the other by reporting the destination is RAM rather\nthan a device, so `#95's pre-zero never runs at all.\n\n**THE DEFECT IS THAT I MADE A DOCUMENTED MISTAKE THE TEMPLATE WARNS ABOUT IN CAPITALS.**  `BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md`\nsection C is headed *\"MANDATORY.  COPY FORWARD.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT GETS DROPPED\"*, and my brief wrote\n*\"SETTLED PREMISES: none carried -- first brief of this campaign\"*.  **That was true about the CAMPAIGN and\nfalse about the QUESTIONS**: the template's own test is whether a seat could build an *if X then A, else B*\nanswer on the premise -- and deepseek built exactly that on `fatal()'.  The obligation is PER-PACKET and\nBROAD, *whatever pass or round settled it*, and `fatal()'-does-not-exit was settled long ago; the carrier\nrecords it costing the minimax seat its answer twice before.\n\n**THE TRANSPORT PREAMBLE DID ITS JOB AND THE BRIEF DID NOT.**  `panel_ollama.py' asks a packet-fed seat to\nNAME any premise it cannot settle instead of guessing, and all three did precisely that rather than\nratifying -- which is the behaviour that makes a packet-fed seat worth firing.  Score this as a BRIEF DEFECT,\nnever as three weak answers.\n\n**FOR THE PASS-2 PACKET:** inline (a) `fatal()'s four-line body, (b) the `memory_rw' pre-zero site with the\nbranch that gates it, and (c) grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.  All three are one or two lines\neach and they are what these seats spent their answers asking for.\n\n*** THE CLASS SCAN'S DENOMINATOR IS WRONG, AND THE WAY IT IS WRONG IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.  Codex seat,\npass 1, verdict GO-WITH-CHANGES. ***\n\nThe brief claimed SIX unpinned-length sites and FIVE stack-buffer write sites, and concluded that\n`dev_sgi_re.c' is the only member of the class in the device tree.  **Codex found four write sites both of my\nscanners missed**, and named the mechanism for each:\n\n  * `dev_dec21143.c' descriptor writebacks (two sites) -- *\"Nested argument commas defeat the scanner's\n    splitting.\"*  **That is limit 1, which the brief DISCLOSED**, demonstrated on real sites.\n  * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' response writes (two sites) -- *\"whose casts prevent recognition of the buffer\n    argument.\"*  **That limit was NOT disclosed and I did not know about it.**\n\nAll four are SAFE (initialised via `store_le32', or cast forms that do fill the buffer), **so the CONCLUSION\nsurvives and the DENOMINATOR does not.**  Those are different claims and the brief conflated them.\n\n*** THE METHODOLOGICAL POINT IS SHARPER THAN THE COUNT, AND IT LANDS ON THE BRIEF'S BEST-LOOKING EVIDENCE: ***\n*\"Agreement between scanners sharing parsing limitations is not exhaustive corroboration.\"*  The brief\npresented TWO independent scans agreeing as strong evidence of scope.  **They are not independent** -- both\nsplit `memory_rw' arguments on commas with the same regex, so both are blind to the same call shapes.  Two\nderivers that can fail the same way are ONE deriver run twice, and this project has already recorded that\nexact lesson on the `sgirekills' count, where two agreeing greps both counted comment lines.\n\n**Two of the brief's SAFE verdicts also need narrowing, per the same seat:**\n  * **GBE** -- the divisor is nonzero and the transfer is bounded, but *\"conversion still loops to 512 after a\n    shorter read\"*, which can consume unfilled bytes.  Codex declines to call it a demonstrated guest\n    disclosure (the forwarded framebuffer prefix is smaller) but says it *\"invalidates the blanket\n    initialization-safety claim\"*.  Correct: my check established the LENGTH bound and said nothing about\n    consumption.\n  * **PXG** -- `#207' bounds the later SRAM copy's SOURCE SPAN without bounding CONSUMPTION, and the initial\n    SRAM copy is unchecked.  The enumerated 3080-byte PX maximum stands; what falls is treating it as proof of\n    complete initialisation across BOTH branches.\n\n**Consequence for this round: the scope sentence must be rewritten before pass 2** -- from *\"the only member\nof this class in the device tree\"* to *\"the only DEFECTIVE member among the sites these two scanners can\nsee, whose blind spots are nested-comma argument lists and cast buffer arguments\"*.  The two new correctness\nfindings the same seat raised are filed separately as [[sgirergbroundtrip]] and [[sgimtedepth4dead]].\n\n*** PASS-1 ADJUDICATION.  EVIDENCE BASIS: 6 of 9 -- six scriptable seats ANSWERED (0 walled, 0 down, 0\nburns, novelty 93-100%); agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED on the measurement reserve; opus5 and fable5 HELD for the\n2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive.  The three absences are DIRECTED, not failures -- but the\nprovisional-adjudication rule still applies and this ruling is provisional on its face. ***\n\n**THE FINDING SURVIVES ALL SIX.**  Nobody disputed that `buf' is uninitialised, that the two mode fields are\nindependent, or that `#95' fails to cover the paths.  What changed is the FRAMING, in three places, and every\ncorrection came from a seat rather than from me.\n\n**D1 -- Q6, ONE ROUND OR TWO: SEPARATE.  My proposal REFUTED, and refuted with a measurement.**  Three seats\nsaid separate (grok, kimi, deepseek); kimi proved it: a shared *destination is deterministic across runs*\noracle is **VACUOUS for this row**, because `srandom(tv.tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv.tv_usec)' makes `random()' vary\nper run while STALE-STACK REUSE UNDER A FIXED GUEST PROGRAM IS RUN-DETERMINISTIC -- so the buggy build PASSES.\nVerified at `src/core/main.c:688-691'.  **I asked the panel not to let the convenience of one round drive the\nanswer, and it did not.**  ACCEPTED: two rounds.\n\n**D2 -- Q5, SEVERITY: grok says MEDIUM, kimi says HIGH *conditionally*.  RESOLVED BY KIMI'S CONDITION, not by\ncounting.**  kimi: HIGH is defensible for this fork's mandate *'provided the entry carries the corrected\nreachability framing ... Do not ship the entry with \"no undocumented value anywhere\" in it.'*  The row KEEPS\nHIGH and the framing is corrected here.  grok's MEDIUM rests on disclosure impact, which is genuinely weak (4\nstale bytes per pixel, usually prior pixels); kimi's HIGH rests on the fork's mandate and on this defect\nfalsifying `#95's *closes the whole class* sentence in the same batch that edited this array.  **That is the\nstronger warrant for THIS fork**, and it is the one I take.  Recorded as a SPLIT, not as agreement.\n\n**F1 -- A FALSE SENTENCE IN MY BRIEF, and it is NOT being edited.**  The brief says the paths are reached\n*'with no undocumented value anywhere'*.  **False**: PATH A's MAXIMAL form (`bufdepth' 8) needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  Documented forms exist -- CI x BUFDEPTH_16/32 for PATH A, and PATH B entirely --\nso the FINDING stands while the blanket phrase does not.  **The sent brief is left BYTE-UNCHANGED on purpose:\nit is the frozen packet six seats reviewed, and rewriting it would falsify the record of what was asked.**\nThe correction lives here and goes into the pass-2 packet.\n\n**F2 -- THE WITNESS IS NOT `ONE CONSTANT'.**  kimi: it needs a register plus the `step_x' twin, or ZERO extra\nregisters via a PUT-only RECT path.  My cost claim was optimistic; the PUT-only path is the cheaper design and\nwas not in the brief at all.\n\n**F3 -- INDEPENDENT CONVERGENCE ON A NEW DEFECT.**  codex and kimi BOTH found the dead `if (depth == 4)'\nwithout knowledge of each other.  Independent convergence is the one kind of agreement that IS evidence, so\n[[sgimtedepth4dead]] ships with two witnesses to its existence.\n\n**OWED BEFORE PASS 2**, from §C2 and the three packet-fed seats who each asked for it: inline `fatal()'s body,\nthe `memory_rw' pre-zero site with its gating branch, and grok's RAM-not-device finding with its warrant.\n\n*** UNDER-HARVESTED REVIEW, CORRECTED 2026-09-07.  I READ THIS SEAT'S ANSWER FOR ITS NEW FINDINGS AND\nSTOPPED.  IT ALSO REFUTED FOUR OF MY CLAIMS AND **REJECTED MY WITNESS DESIGN**, AND I RECORDED NONE OF IT.\nTHIS IS THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, ON AN ANSWER I HAD ALREADY OPENED. (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/codex.txt) ***\n\n**1. MY WITNESS DESIGN IS REJECTED, and the reason is architectural rather than a detail.**  I proposed\npainting a first pixel so `buf' holds KNOWN bytes, then reading a second at a mismatched depth and asserting\nthe result carries bytes from the first.  codex: *\"Separate automatic objects have no architectural promise\nof retaining each other's contents.  Register allocation, inlining, and intervening calls matter.\"*  And the\nsame objection kills the other direction: *\"**Path A has the same problem:** observing an unassigned byte\ndoes not guarantee that its value differs from the intended padding.\"*  **I flagged that risk myself in the\nbrief -- `an oracle of \"the value is wrong\" can pass by luck' -- proposed a fix for it, and did not record\nthat the fix was refused.**\n\n**The prescribed measurement instead: an ATTRIBUTED INITIALIZEDNESS RESULT per direction** -- MemorySanitizer\nor Valgrind Memcheck -- *\"tied to the reviewed build, together with passing functional controls\"*, with the\ncaveat that *\"merely copying undefined bytes need not produce an immediate diagnostic\"*, so instrumentation\ncoverage must be established rather than assumed.  **That is a different kind of round from the one I was\nplanning** and it is why this row is not next in the queue.\n\n**2. `LIN_A cannot be refused' IS FALSE.**  I wrote that case 4 FORCES the valid bit so the arm is reached\nunconditionally.  codex: *\"remove 'LIN_A cannot be refused': its index guard can return before the buffer\ncode.\"*  `tile_nr = x >> 12; if (tile_nr >= 32) return;` -- an early return I read past.\n\n**3. `THE WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT CHANGED' IS FALSE.**  *\"The existing probe uses its LIN_A constant for the\ndestination and matched control as well as a source case.  Changing that shared constant changes the\nobservation path and the control simultaneously.\"*  My cost estimate was wrong for the same reason kimi's\ncorrection said so, and I recorded kimi's and not this one.\n\n**4. `UNINITIALISED ON EVERY PATH' OVERSTATES IT.**  *\"matched operations initialize every byte they\nconsume.\"*  The defect is the MISMATCHED pairs, not the buffer in general.\n\n**5. MY `WORSE THAN I SAID' CLAIM ABOUT RAM IS WRONG.**  I recorded that grok's RAM-not-device finding makes\nboth paths worse.  codex: *\"**RAM does not inherently make Path B worse:** a successful RAM read defines the\nrequested prefix through `memcpy'; the missing suffix is the same problem.\"*\n\n**6. NOT ALL SEVEN CALL SITES TAKE THE GUEST MODE WORDS** -- *\"the two MTE sites construct their own modes\"*.\nMy reachability paragraph implies all seven.\n\n**7. SEVERITY: MED, not HIGH.**  *\"HIGH is not established by inheriting #95's severity.\"*  And it refuses\nthe downgrade too: *\"'usually the previous pixel' and 'first call in a frame' are unsupported explanations of\nstack contents.  They cannot justify downgrading this to a cosmetic rendering defect.\"*  **Both of my\nseverity arguments were unsupported, in opposite directions.**  Lowered to MED provisionally, with potential\nhost information disclosure recorded as unestablished rather than assumed either way.\n\n*** THE PROCESS FAILURE IS THE POINT AND IT IS THE THIRD INSTANCE TONIGHT. ***  I opened this file, extracted\nthe two NEW defects and the census correction, and stopped -- because new findings are what I was looking\nfor.  **A seat that refutes your premises is easy to under-read precisely because its corrections are not\nphrased as discoveries.**  The same failure hit the `lcabadval` answer (an eleventh site missed) and the\n`alphaswpctxptbr` premises.  All three were answers already on disk.\n\n*** THE STRONGEST REACHABILITY VEHICLE WAS IN THE KIMI ANSWER ALL ALONG, AND IT IS ALL-DOCUMENTED.\nHarvested and verified 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt). ***\n\nEverything above argues reachability from the DE path and concedes that PATH A's maximal form needs the\nundocumented depth field 3.  **There is a third vehicle that needs nothing undocumented at all**, and it is\nthe one a real driver uses: `do_mte_transfer()' constructs its own modes with NO colour bits (so CI), because\nthe `if (depth == 4)' that would set RGBA is dead code -- see [[sgimtedepth4dead]], raised to HIGH on this.\n\nCI fills only `buf[0]'; `MTE_DEPTH_16' and `MTE_DEPTH_32' are documented and give `bufdepth' 2 and 4.  **So\nevery MTE fill at those depths leaks 1 or 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel, with every value documented**, on\nthe path kimi names as used by `crmfb_fill_rect'.\n\n**This also settles the `not all seven call sites take the guest mode words' correction** recorded above from\nthe codex seat: the two MTE sites construct their own modes -- and constructing them is exactly what makes\nthem leak.  The two corrections are the same fact seen from opposite sides, and I recorded one without\nnoticing it implied the other.\n\n**AND BOTH FILE-READING SEATS INDEPENDENTLY REACHED THE DETECTOR REQUIREMENT:** the oracle must assert\nVALUES, not determinism.  kimi's proof is the sharper one -- post-fix PATH B yields a deterministic value AND\nthe `bufdepth'-sized mutant yields the previous pixel's bytes, which are *also* deterministic under stack\nreuse, so *\"a determinism oracle CANNOT catch the mutant\"*.  Two seats, two routes, one conclusion.\n\n*** FOURTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER, AND THE RICHEST PER BYTE: THE GROK SEAT'S 15 KB\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/grok.txt).  IT ANSWERED THE OPTION-SET QUESTION WITH GREPPED IN-TREE PRECEDENT, NAMED\nTHREE MEMBERS I HAD NOT, ARGUED THE DISPOSITION I WAS LEANING TOWARD IS WRONG, AND FOUND THE MTE CHAIN\nINDEPENDENTLY.  I HAD RECORDED ONLY ITS BUFDEPTH CORRECTION. ***\n\n**1. THE MTE CHAIN, INDEPENDENTLY.**  *\"16- and 32-bit MTE TLB fills therefore always run as CI + matching\nbufdepth.  32-bit fill writes `bg''s low byte plus three uninit bytes.  Comment at :1322 says NetBSD\n`crmfb_fill_rect' uses this.\"*  **So TWO seats found it and I missed it from both** -- and grok additionally\npinned the in-file comment naming the driver, which I had attributed to kimi alone.\n\n**Its fix-order point is sharper than the one I recorded:** *\"Do NOT join: fixing `== 4' to `== 32' changes\nMTE 32-bit COLOUR semantics, which memset does not.  Memset would turn that path into 'low byte + zeros' --\ndefined, still wrong.\"*  I had noted that repairing the dead branch alone MASKS the leak; grok's is the dual\nand it matters as much -- **the memset fix leaves the MTE path DEFINED AND STILL WRONG**, because the colour\nsemantics are a separate defect from the initialisation.  Neither fix subsumes the other.\n\n**2. THREE OPTION-SET MEMBERS I DID NOT NAME**, and it reached them by grepping rather than reasoning:\n    6.  `uint8_t buf[8] = {0};`  -- the C99 form, same observable as a memset\n    7.  CLAMP THE TRANSFER to bytes filled (`len = min(bufdepth, filled)`) -- guest-visible difference: the\n        OLD DESTINATION BYTES SURVIVE, which is neither zeros nor stale stack\n    8.  IMPLEMENT the modes (YCRCB, 64-bit depth) -- and it refused this itself on the ADDITION LICENCE: no\n        CRIME DE pixel-packing datasheet exists in `_scratchpad/`\n\n**3. THE IN-TREE PRECEDENTS, WHICH IS WHAT THE OPTION-SET RULE ASKS FOR AND I DID NOT DO:**\n    * **this function's own RGB PUT already pads with zero** (`buf[3] = 0`) -- in-function precedent for\n      DEFINED PADDING rather than refusal;\n    * `dev_dreamcast_maple.c' does `memset(buf, 0, 8)' then a partial fill then `MEM_WRITE' -- **the same\n      shape, and the house answer there is zero the whole array**;\n    * `dev_sgi_gbe.c' (`#196') renders an unimplemented colour type as BLACK -- defined zeros, no `fatal'\n      flood;\n    * MTE's own `zerobuf' is memset before its write, in this same file.\n\n**4. IT ARGUES DECLINE IS THE WRONG DISPOSITION, AND THE REASON TIES BACK TO THE MTE CHAIN:**\n*\"`CI' + `BUFDEPTH_32' is a plausible 8-bit index in a 32-bit container (RGB already pads).  Declining it\nwould also break the MTE 16/32-bit TLB fill path, which ALREADY EMITS THAT PAIR.  No hardware evidence it is\nillegal.\"*  **That is decisive against member 4** and I had not connected the two facts -- the very pair a\ndecline would reject is the one the model's own MTE path generates.\n\nIts recommendation is **(1)/(6), full-object zero**, with the comment saying plainly that hardware padding is\nunknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME model -- which is also what the codex seat\nindependently chose.\n\n*** 5. AN APPARENT DISAGREEMENT I COLLAPSED, RESOLVED HERE BY READING BOTH RATHER THAN PICKING ONE. ***\nI recorded codex's *\"RAM does not inherently make PATH B worse\"* as a correction of my summary.  grok says\nthe opposite in its heading: *\"PATH B is worse than the brief said.\"*  **Reading both: they are not in\nconflict.**  grok's point is that `#95' does not run AT ALL on a RAM destination, so nothing pre-zeroes\nanything; codex's is that a successful RAM read still DEFINES the requested prefix through `memcpy', so only\nthe suffix beyond `bufdepth' is undefined -- which is the same defect either way.  **codex is right on the\ntechnical point and grok is right about the brief's framing.**  Recording only one of them, as I did, loses\nthat.\n\n*** THE THREE PRECEDENTS VERIFIED, AND THE THIRD IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN PRIOR RULING ON THE SAME\nQUESTION.  Checked 2026-09-07 -- grok said `grep, not argument', so they were grepped. ***\n\n    buf[3] = 0;                         dev_sgi_re.c, this function's own RGB PUT      CONFIRMED\n    memset(buf, 0, 8);                  dev_dreamcast_maple.c, twice (:285, :330)      CONFIRMED\n    *r = *g = *b = 0;                   dev_sgi_gbe.c #196                             CONFIRMED\n\n**The `#196' comment is the decisive one and it is BY THIS PROJECT'S OWN SEATS:**\n\n    default:/*  #196: (Codex/Fable) d->color_mode is guest-controlled; an\n                unimplemented WID mode must not exit() the host (get_rgb is\n                per-pixel, so don't fatal()-flood either) -- render black.  */\n            *r = *g = *b = 0;\n\n**That is the same question already answered**: a GUEST-CONTROLLED unimplemented colour mode, in a PER-PIXEL\nhelper, on a SIBLING SGI framebuffer -- and the ruling was DEFINED ZEROS, with the per-pixel property given\nexplicitly as the reason not to `fatal()'-flood.  It is the identical reasoning `sgirandompixel' used for its\nlatch, so the house doctrine is consistent across three rounds.\n\n**SO THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BY TRANSFERRED WARRANT RATHER THAN BY PREFERENCE:** full-object zero, with a\ncomment saying plainly that hardware padding is unknown and this is defined behaviour rather than a CRIME\nmodel.  Both file-reading seats reached it independently, and `#196' is a fourth vote from a round that\nalready shipped.\n\n*This is the option-set rule working exactly as written -- `GREP THE FORK FOR AN EXISTING IDIOM; the tree's\nown precedents are part of the design space'.  I did not do that; grok did, and it produced a better answer\nthan the argument I was assembling.*\n\n*** FIFTH UNDER-HARVESTED ANSWER -- THE PACKET-FED SEATS.  glm's contains a DEFECT NOBODY ELSE FOUND\n(filed as [[sgilinearnegidx]]) and a BETTER VERSION OF MY OWN #95 ARGUMENT. ***\n\n**glm's `#95' warrant is stronger than the one I wrote and does not depend on reading the source:**\n*\"zeroing past `len' would itself be an OOB write on any caller with a tight buffer ... So every\nMEMORY-SAFE implementation of `#95' zeroes at most `bufdepth' bytes, and PATH B's `buf[bufdepth..3]' are\nstale under all of them, not merely the described one.  PATH A is a `MEM_WRITE' whose source is `buf'; a\nwrite-side pre-zero cannot exist there without destroying the payload of every device write in the tree.\nSurvival against `#95' is OVER-DETERMINED, not lucky.\"*  My version rested on the pre-zero happening to be\ngated on `MEM_READ'; glm's shows no memory-safe implementation could behave otherwise.\n\n**And a correction to my own §3b:** I wrote that `#95' zeroes `buf[0..bufdepth-1]' unconditionally.  glm:\n*\"that is the device branch only -- the stronger case presented as fact, harmless to the claim.\"*\n\n**minimax's witness-table point, recorded with its own arithmetic slip corrected:** it observes that the\nwitness table lists only RGBA mode words and omits the CI cases, which is fair -- CI leaks at any depth above\n1 and no row drives it.  Its example value is wrong, though: it calls `0x1210' *LIN_A | depth 2 | CI*, and\n`0x1210 & 0xf0' is `0x10' = RGB, not CI (`0x00').  **The observation stands and the constant does not.**\n\n*** SHARPENED 2026-09-07 BY THE glm SEAT on the `sgirergbroundtrip` pass-1 panel (_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055), and it is\nwider than this row said. ***\n\n    \"per the code's own #100 comment a bufdepth of 8 makes every named PUT case transmit four\n     uninitialized stack bytes to guest memory; a bufdepth-8 GET silently drops the high half;\n     and with bufdepth 1 or 2 the RGB/RGBA/ABGR GETs read uninitialized buf[2..3].\"\n\n**THREE DISTINCT EXPOSURES, not one**, and the row had only the first:\n  (a) bufdepth 8 PUT -- `buf[4..7]` are never written by ANY arm, so four uninitialised stack bytes\n      go to guest memory through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)`;\n  (b) bufdepth 8 GET -- the high half is read into buf and then silently discarded, since every GET\n      arm reads at most four bytes.  Not a leak; a correctness gap;\n  (c) **bufdepth 1 or 2 GET -- the RGB, RGBA and ABGR arms read `buf[2..3]` that `memory_rw` never\n      filled.**  This is the one the row did not have, and it is the mirror of (a): a LEAK INTO THE\n      GUEST'S OWN PIXEL VALUE rather than into guest memory.\n\n**THE GUARD/BODY SPLIT IS WHY ALL THREE EXIST:** the colour type is bits 4-7 of the mode word and\n`bufdepth` is bits 8-9 of the SAME word, read independently.  Nothing couples them, so a guest\nselects a four-byte colour type with a one-byte transfer length whenever it likes.\n\n*Recorded from `#505`'s pre-table GUARD-FIELD-vs-BODY-FIELD check, which found the same disagreeing\nstate independently and then DECLINED to row it -- the exposure is identical before and after that\nround's edit (the shipped GET read three bytes, the fixed one reads three bytes), so a row there\nwould attribute a pre-existing defect to a round that neither created nor cured it.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 -- BY grok, BECAUSE CODEX REFUSED THE BRIEF.\n`regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/grok_pass1_20260907.txt`, 18 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the right fix for\nTHIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.  It is not the packing fix, it is not PUT-only, and\nno single-access guest value of 0 can pin it.  S1 and S5 hold.  S2's decode holds.  S3's GET\noverstates RGB.  S4's quotes exist and its site-count does not.\"*\n\n**Q1/Q2 ANSWERED, and Q2's cost objection dissolved by a fact I did not have:** *\"It is declared\nAFTER the coordinate / `tlb_mode` / `tileptr`-valid early returns, so a zero-fill at that site is not\npaid on declined pixels.\"*  So the per-pixel cost applies only to pixels that were going to be\ntransferred anyway -- which removes the reason to prefer a narrower site.\n\n**Q3 ANSWERED, and it is the answer I asked for rather than the one I wanted:** *\"no single-access\nguest value of `0` can pin it.\"*  A value oracle cannot distinguish \"filled with zeros\" from\n\"happened to be zeros\", which is what the brief suspected and what the round must now design around.\n\n*** MY S3 WAS WRONG IN ITS EMPHASIS, AND grok's ENUMERATION IS BETTER THAN MY PROSE. ***  I led with\ndepth 8; the severe case is CI:\n\n    PUT bytes transferred with no store:\n                              depth 1   depth 2   depth 4     depth 8\n      CI                      none      buf[1]    buf[1..3]   buf[1..7]\n      RGB/RGBA/ABGR/default   none      none      none        buf[4..7]\n\n**CI at depth 4 already transfers three unstored bytes**, which needs no unusual depth at all -- and\nthe MTE path forces CI (see [[sgimtedepth4dead]]).  *\"emphasis on depth 8 is the weak vehicle.\"*\n\n**S2 REFINED, and the refinement matters for how this is described:** on the MTE path the colour bits\nare NOT a second guest field.  `src_mode`/`dst_mode` are built as `(tlb << 10) + (depth_field << 8)`\nwith no type bits, so `color_mode` is pinned at 0 (CI) unless the dead branch fires.  *\"That is a\nconstant colliding with a guest depth, not two independent guest fields.\"*  The two-independent-fields\nstory is true of the DE path and false of the MTE path.\n\n*** TWO NEW FINDINGS UNDER THE FOUND-SOMETHING-ELSE LICENCE: ***\n  1. **`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` (bits 2-3) is never read by `dev_sgi_re.c` at all.**  A third mode field\n     the device ignores entirely.\n  2. **A WRONG RECORD IN PRODUCT CODE, verified by reading:** `dev_sgi_re.c:732` says\n     `// bufdepth = 1, 2, or 4.` immediately above\n     `int src_bufdepth = 1 << ((src_mode >> 8) & 3);` -- which yields **1, 2, 4 or 8**.  The `#100`\n     round grew `buf[8]` precisely because the decode reaches 8, so this comment was already false\n     when that round shipped.  **L4, cheap, and it belongs to whichever round next touches this\n     file.**\n\n**S4's SITE COUNT IS WRONG AND ITS QUOTES ARE NOT.**  I wrote that `memory_rw.c` zero-fills at three\nsites; grok says the count does not hold and flagged in its own trace that *\"GET versus PUT may not\nshare a `#95` path\"*.  **That distinction is load-bearing and I had glossed it:** `#95` zero-fills a\nREAD buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access.  It therefore does nothing for a PUT (a write), and\non a successful GET it fills exactly `bufdepth` bytes -- never `buf[2..3]` beyond them.  **So the\ntransferred warrant is weaker than the brief claimed: `#95` establishes the fork's ETHOS for this\nclass, not a mechanism that already covers these calls.**\n\n*Recorded as the round's own premise-audit result: of five settled premises, two survived intact, one\nwas refined, one had its emphasis corrected, and one had its supporting count refuted.*\n\n*** A SECOND, INDEPENDENT AUDIT AGREES ON THE FIX AND GOES FURTHER ON THE ORACLE.  codex, via the\nREWORDED brief (`codex_reworded_20260907.txt`) after the original was refused. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Zero-initialize all eight bytes at the existing declaration.  That completes the local\ninitialization repair in both directions, but does not establish correct pixel-format or MTE\nbehavior.  **No guest-visible value or write-count oracle can reliably prove initialization\noccurred.**\"*\n\n**TWO SEATS, TWO TRANSPORTS, SAME FIX.**  grok: *\"Zero-fill `buf` at its current declaration is the\nright fix for THIS class and is the whole fix OF this class.\"*  codex: *\"Zero-initialize all eight\nbytes at the existing declaration.\"*  Both add the same caveat -- it repairs initialisation and does\nNOT repair the packing/format defect, which is [[sgimtedepth4dead]]'s territory.  **The design is\nsettled.**\n\n*** THE ORACLE IS HARDER THAN THE NEIGHBOURING ROUND ASSUMED, AND THIS IS THE LOAD-BEARING FINDING.\n***  grok said no single-access guest VALUE can pin it.  codex rules out the WRITE-COUNT oracle as\nwell -- and a write-count oracle is exactly what a seat proposed for [[sgirandomcolour]]\n(*\"observe ZERO MEM_WRITE calls from an unsupported PUT\"*).  **That proposal survives for\n`sgirandomcolour`, where the question is whether a write HAPPENS; it does not transfer to this row,\nwhere the question is what the written bytes CONTAIN.**  Two rounds, one idiom, and it is valid in\nonly one of them.\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** if no black-box guest-visible oracle can prove\ninitialisation, this round's detector cannot be a rung-3 probe of the ordinary kind.  The candidates\nare an offline differential over the arms, or a `harness:` witness under the ladder's fifth term.\n**That must be decided BEFORE the fix, not after** -- the round is otherwise a one-line edit with no\nway to defend it.\n\n**AND THE CEILING FOR THIS ROW IS NOW STATED CLEANLY** (same answer, Q1): *\"initializing the buffer\nchanges neither the transfer length nor the call count.  Uninitialized bytes can happen to match the\nexpected initialized values, so even matching values PLUS matching counts cannot establish\ninitialization.  **Unexpected values can expose a failure; expected values cannot prove\ndefinedness.**\"*\n\nThat is the sharpest form of the limit and it settles the round's shape: **no guest-visible oracle\ncan prove this fix worked.**  The detector must be an offline differential or a `harness:` witness\nunder the ladder's fifth term -- decided BEFORE the one-line edit, not after.\n\n*** FULL PANEL 2026-09-07 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260907_130159`).  SIX SEATS, ZERO DOWN, glm did\nNOT burn for the second panel running. ***\n\n**THE SPLIT, which is the thing for the adjudication:**\n  * **grok: TAKE THE ROUND** -- *\"zero-fill `buf` at the existing declaration; do not make the arms\n    write `bufdepth` bytes; do not treat a guest 0 as proof of initialisation.\"*\n  * **deepseek: NOT RATIFY as a correctness fix** -- *\"zero-fill only converts uninitialized reads\n    into defined-but-wrong bytes for CI depth>1; the independent assignment/transfer defect\n    remains.\"*\n  * **minimax: the symptom is handled but THE LICENCE IS UNSUPPORTED** -- *\"no measured delta is\n    offered.\"*\n\n  *So the question is not whether to zero-fill.  It is whether zero-fill is a FIX or a SYMPTOM PATCH\n  that leaves the real decoupling defect in place -- and whether the addition licence permits it with\n  no measured delta.*\n\n*** THREE SEATS INDEPENDENTLY FOUND A DEFECT IN MY PACKET, AND IT IS A CROSS-ROUND INTERACTION I HAD\nNOT SEEN. ***  S3's REVERSE-DIRECTION sentence -- *\"at depth 1 or 2 the read fills only that many\nbytes and the multi-byte arms then read `buf[2..3]` regardless\"* -- is **false of RGB as the file now\nsits**.  grok: *\"its GET sentence is false of RGB.\"*  glm: *\"asserted in prose, not in the enumerated\ntable, and under the packet's own model it is wrong.\"*  minimax: *\"S3's table is asymmetric -- write\ndirection enumerated, read direction mentioned in one sentence only.\"*\n\n  **WHY: `#505` changed RGB GET from `buf[1..3]` to `buf[0..2]`.**  So post-`#505` an RGB read at\n  depth 2 touches `buf[2]` and NOT `buf[3]`.  The sentence was true of the pre-`#505` file and I\n  carried it forward into a packet written after the fix.  **My own two rounds interact and the\n  enumerated table hid it, because I enumerated the WRITE direction and prosed the READ direction.**\n  *A table beside a sentence invites exactly this: the table gets checked and the sentence does not.*\n\n**THREE SEATS ALSO AGREE S4's WARRANT IS DEAD:** *\"S4 is ethos, not precedent\"* (deepseek, minimax);\n*\"its site-count and the `#95` transfer do not [hold]\"* (grok).  I had already recorded `#95` as\nweaker than claimed; the panel makes it unanimous.  **The addition licence therefore wants a MEASURED\nDELTA for this one-line change, and none exists.**  That is now the round's blocking question rather\nthan the oracle.\n\n**Q1 IS SETTLED AGAINST DEPTH-AWARE ARMS**, by two seats and for the same reason: *\"depth-aware arms\nwould trespass on the neighbouring rows that own pixel semantics and would require hardware truth\nthis packet does not contain\"* (kimi).  So the boundary with [[sgimtedepth4dead]] and\n[[sgirandomcolour]] holds.\n\n*** AND THE ORACLE QUESTION HAS ITS FIRST CONSTRUCTIVE ANSWER, from the kimi substitute: ***\n*\"The only honest definedness detector is an instrumented run (MSan/Valgrind class); any harness\nwitness is one-sided **UNLESS THE STACK BENEATH THE FRAME IS PRE-SEEDED**.\"*\n\n  **That is actionable and nobody had proposed it.**  Every earlier answer said no oracle can prove\n  definedness; this says a harness witness becomes TWO-SIDED if the frame's stack is seeded with a\n  known non-zero pattern first -- then `0` means initialised and the pattern means not.  This fork\n  already has an ASAN/UBSan tree (`build-asan/`), so the instrumented route is also available.\n  **Both need checking before the round; neither was on the table an hour ago.**\n\n*** THE CODEX SEAT REFUTED S4 AND THE OTHER THREE SEATS RATIFIED MY ERROR.  SETTLED BY OPENING\nTHE FILE, NOT BY COUNTING SEATS. ***\n\nMy S4 said `#95` *\"fills a READ buffer for `len` bytes on a FAILED access\"*.  deepseek, minimax and\ngrok all agreed and graded the warrant *\"ethos, not precedent\"*.  **codex went and read it:**\n\n    memory_rw.c :492   if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memset(data, 0, len);\n                :494   paddr -= mem->devices[i].baseaddr;      <- THE DEVICE DISPATCH\n\n**`:492` is not in a failure branch at all.**  It prefills on EVERY device read, successful or not,\nbefore dispatch and before length clamping -- its own comment says *\"never hand back uninitialised\nhost memory to the guest.  Root-cause fix for the uninit-read class\"*.  The failure-path fills are\n`:215` and `:267`, the two that `return MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED`.  **Verified here by reading all six\nsites.**  codex's enumeration of six (215, 267, 492, 671, 683, 732) is correct and my 'three sites'\nwas wrong as well.\n\n  > **SO THE WARRANT IS A PRECEDENT, NOT AN ETHOS: `#95` is exactly 'initialise storage before a\n  > producer may assign only part of it'.**  That is this fix, one layer down.  The addition licence\n  > is satisfied by TRANSFERRED WARRANT and the round does NOT owe a measured delta -- which\n  > REVERSES the blocking question three seats had just created.\n\n**THIS IS THE WRONG-FACT-COMES-BACK-CONFIRMED CLASS AT FULL STRENGTH.**  I stated a weakened S4 as a\nSETTLED PREMISE; three of six seats confirmed it; one seat checked it.  The carrier's rule --\n*unanimity on a brief-supplied fact is worth nothing; score agreement only where a seat re-derived\nit* -- scores this exactly right, and I had written the premise-audit opener into the brief and\nstill stated S4 as settled rather than as a question.\n\n*The sharper lesson: **a premise I had ALREADY CORRECTED ONCE, in the weakening direction, is the\nmost dangerous kind.**  It arrives wearing the clothes of a correction, so it reads as the careful\nversion and nobody re-checks it -- including me, who wrote it.*\n\n**codex's other corrections, each verified before recording:**\n  * **S1 is wrong.**  Not 'once per pixel from three callers': THREE execution paths, TWO caller\n    functions, SEVEN direct call sites (LINE GET :817 PUT :863; RECTANGLE GET :878, GET :883,\n    PUT :929; tiled MTE GET :1382 PUT :1387).  RECTANGLE does TWO GETs and a PUT per iteration, so\n    'once per pixel' understates a fill's cost threefold on that path.\n  * **S3's GET row corrected, agreeing with grok, glm and minimax** -- per-arm and per-width: CI\n    consumes NO unassigned byte at any width; RGB consumes `buf[1..2]` after a 1-byte read and\n    `buf[2]` after 2; RGBA/ABGR/default consume `buf[1..3]` and `buf[2..3]`.  **`#505` is why RGB\n    differs** -- it now reads `buf[0..2]` and never `buf[3]`.\n  * *** `-DNDEBUG` IS SET, so an `assert()`-based detector is a NO-OP in a normal build. ***\n    Verified: `configure:313` sets `CFLAGS=\"-DNDEBUG $CFLAGS\"`, and `src/devices/Makefile:9` carries\n    it.  An initialisedness harness built on `assert` would be VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION -- the\n    check-that-cannot-fail shape, arriving inside the detector for this very round.\n  * **Q4, a coverage gap in the SHIPPED `#505` detector:** `rgb depth64` and `rgb tiled64` widen the\n    *source read*; their destination stays a four-byte RGBA write, so **no row establishes\n    initialisation of an eight-byte PUT's tail.**  Filed as [[sgireput8tail]] rather than fixed.\n\n*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- ENUMERATED, not asserted, in BOTH directions. ***\n\n**PUT -- uninitialised bytes actually transferred to guest memory:**\n\n    CI          depth 2 -> 1 byte, depth 4 -> 3 bytes, depth 8 -> 7 bytes\n    every other encoding (incl. RGB/RGBA/ABGR/YCRCB and the 11 undocumented)  depth 8 -> 4 bytes\n\n    18 of 64 colour-type x byte-depth combinations, across all sixteen encodings the type mask\n    allows.  (6 of 16 counting only the four handled types; 7 of 20 including YCRCB.)\n\n**GET -- uninitialised bytes consumed by the recombination: 30 of 64.**\n\n## TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG\n\n* **`random()` INITIALISES its four bytes.**  So the `sgirandomcolour` arm does NOT leak\n  uninitialised data -- it writes objectionable data.  Different defect, and the distinction is\n  what keeps the two rows separable.\n* **RGB's GET reads `buf[0..2]` only, never `buf[3]`** -- my brief said the arms recombine\n  `buf[0..3]`, which is true of the others.\n* and my \"stale from a previous call\" framing was loose: *\"there is no persistent previous-call\n  buffer in the language semantics.  Automatic storage becomes indeterminate again on each\n  execution of the declaration.\"*\n\n## *** A REACHABILITY FINDING I DID NOT ASK FOR: THE MTE PATH HITS THIS. ***\n\nThe tiled MTE constructs **CI/1, CI/2, RGBA/4 and CI/8**.  Its GETs therefore avoid the short-read\nrecombination -- **but its CI/2 and CI/8 PUTs are affected.**  *So this is not a hypothetical\ncombination space: an in-tree path already builds two of the leaking cells.*\n\n## THE FIX, and it costs nothing on correct paths\n\n    uint8_t buf[8] = {0};\n\n*\"It preserves the output of otherwise defined paths whose consumed bytes were already initialised.\nPUT overwrites the same bytes before transferring them; GET overwrites the requested bytes before\nrecombination.  Transfer addresses and lengths remain unchanged.\"*  **That is the Q4 property --\nno currently-correct path changes.**\n\n## SEVERITY, BOUNDED RATHER THAN INFLATED -- which is what I asked for\n\n**Both a guest-visible nondeterminism defect AND a bounded information-exposure one**, but *\"source\ninspection alone does not demonstrate secrets, another process's memory, or a reliable arbitrary\nhost-memory read.\"*  It is the emulator's own stack.\n\n## SCOPE: two rounds -- AGREED, but NOT on the panel's reason\n\nThe `sgirandomcolour` panel ruled two rounds 5/5 because *\"one oracle cannot distinguish a\nCI/depth-8 regression from a YCRCB regression.\"*  codex agrees with the conclusion and **disputes\nthe argument**: *\"One test suite can check multiple independent properties; one CI/depth-8\nobservation cannot establish YCRCB correctness.\"*\n\n**And it sharpened the overlap the panel had called a half-fix.**  Zero-initialisation fixes the\nfour unwritten TAIL bytes of the default PUT at depth 8, and the default GET's short-read\nrecombination -- **it leaves all four `random()` calls and their PRNG-state effects intact.**  So it\nis an overlap in *uninitialised-buffer behaviour*, not half of the random assignments.\n**RECORDED HERE EXPLICITLY, as it asked**: if `sgirandomcolour` claims those tail/GET cases, its\nscope overlaps this row and the two rounds must say so.\n\n## *** THE ORACLE IS THE HONEST ANSWER I ASKED FOR, AND IT HAS A COST ***\n\n*\"A single ordinary run cannot reliably distinguish fixed from unfixed.  Uninitialised storage can\nhappen to contain zeros or a stable pattern ... Repeated identical output also does not prove\ninitialisation.\"*\n\n**The instrument is MemorySanitizer**, which can tell an uninitialised zero from a deliberately\ninitialised one.  **THAT IS A NEW BUILD TREE:** `build-asan/` carries\n`-fsanitize=address,undefined`, and **MSan is incompatible with ASan** -- so this round owes a third\nsanitizer configuration, not a re-use.  *That cost is real and is recorded now rather than\ndiscovered mid-round.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31411:     "note": "PASS 1.  **GO-WITH-CHANGES**, 123 KB, and the strongest answer of the six.  *** IT KILLED THE BRIEF'S OWN Q6 PROPOSAL WITH EVIDENCE FROM THE TREE. ***  I had asked whether one oracle -- *the destination is DETERMINISTIC across runs* -- could cover this row and [[sgirandomcolour]] together.  kimi: `srandom(tv.tv_sec ^ getpid() ^ tv.tv_usec)' runs at startup (src/core/main.c), so `random()' varies per run -- **but stale-stack reuse under a fixed guest program is run-DETERMINISTIC, so the BUGGY BUILD PASSES a determinism oracle.**  The shared oracle is VACUOUS for this row and toothed only for the other.  VERIFIED: main.c:688-691 reads exactly that, under `skip_srandom_call'.  On Q5 it split the difference with a CONDITION rather than a number: HIGH is defensible for this fork's mandate *'provided the entry carries the corrected reachability framing ... Do not ship the entry with \"no undocumented value anywhere\" in it.'*  It also independently found the dead `depth == 4', and corrected the witness-cost line: not `one constant' but a register plus the step_x twin, or zero via a PUT-only RECT path.  Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260907_010527 (preserved: regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/)"
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31417:     "note": "PASS 1.  **TAKE THE ROUND, BUT NOT ON THE BRIEF'S PATH-A EXEMPLAR.**  Settled the premise I left open -- the destination is RAM, not a device, so `#95's pre-zero never runs at all -- and corrected my PATH-A vehicle: the header documents BUFDEPTH 8/16/32 only, field 3 is UNDOCUMENTED, so `0x1320' is the weak form while CI + BUFDEPTH_32 (`0x1200') leaks with every field documented.  VERIFIED against crmfbreg.h before acceptance.  Argued MEDIUM on severity and NO on joining sgirandomcolour.  Panel dir _scratchpad/panel_20260907_010527 (preserved: regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/)"
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31463:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_scope_20260907.txt",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31475:     "note": "Corrected two of my premises, refined the sgirandomcolour overlap, and refused to inflate the exposure claim beyond what source inspection establishes."
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31496:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on the `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 panel, under the standing\n*\"if you found something ELSE, say so\"* licence -- it is not what that brief asked about.\n\n**VERIFIED BY ARITHMETIC BEFORE FILING.**  The two arms disagree about which byte holds which channel:\n\n    PUT  case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:   buf[0] = *color >> 24;  buf[1] = *color >> 16;\n                                  buf[2] = *color >> 8;   buf[3] = 0;\n    GET  case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:   *color = (buf[1] << 24) + (buf[2] << 16) + (buf[3] << 8);\n\nPUT stores the top three bytes at indices **0,1,2**; GET recombines from **1,2,3**.  Storing `0x11223300'\ngives bytes `11 22 33 00', which GET reads back as **`0x22330000'**.  A round trip through the model's own\nRGB mode does not return what was written.\n\n*** THE REASON THIS MATTERS BEYOND THE BUG: EVERY ANALYSIS THIS WEEK, MINE INCLUDED, CALLED THE FOUR\nIMPLEMENTED COLOUR ARMS `CORRECT' AND TREATED ONLY THE `default:' ARMS AS SUSPECT. ***  The\n`sgirebufuninit' brief says so explicitly -- *\"NO colour arm writes buf[4..7] -- not one of the five,\nincluding the four CORRECT ones\"*.  codex's words: *\"Calling all implemented color arms 'correct' is\nunwarranted.\"*  It is right, and the phrase had been repeated unchecked across three documents.\n\nWHICH SIDE IS WRONG IS **NOT** ESTABLISHED HERE and must not be guessed: RGBA uses 0,1,2,3 symmetrically on\nboth sides, and ABGR uses 0..3 reversed on both sides, so RGB is the only asymmetric pair -- but whether the\nhardware packs RGB at the low three bytes or the high three is a DATASHEET question this row does not\nanswer.  A round taking it owes that reference, or a comparison against a real framebuffer dump.\n\nNO WITNESS.  Reachability is not in doubt (RGB is documented and implemented) but nothing here shows a guest\ndriver round-tripping through it.\n\n*** WHICH SIDE IS WRONG IS NOW SETTLED, AND NO DATASHEET WAS NEEDED.  THE ANSWER WAS ONE FILE AWAY, IN THE\nCONSUMER OF THESE VERY BYTES.  Derived 2026-09-07, same day as the filing. ***\n\nThis row said the packing question needed *\"a datasheet reference or a comparison against a real framebuffer\ndump\"*.  **It needed neither.**  `dev_sgi_gbe.c`'s tick reads the SAME tile memory these arms write and turns\nit into host framebuffer pixels:\n\n    color = (buf[i]<<24) + (buf[i+1]<<16) + (buf[i+2]<<8) + buf[i+3];\n\nSo the in-tree contract is unambiguous: **byte index 0 is the MOST significant channel.**  Against that:\n\n    PUT  RGB    buf[0]=c>>24  buf[1]=c>>16  buf[2]=c>>8   buf[3]=0        AGREES with the consumer\n    PUT  RGBA   buf[0]=c>>24  buf[1]=c>>16  buf[2]=c>>8   buf[3]=c        AGREES\n    GET  RGBA   (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)+buf[3]              AGREES\n    GET  ABGR   (buf[3]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[1]<<8)+buf[0]              AGREES (deliberately reversed)\n    GET  RGB    (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8)                     *** DISAGREES -- off by one ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS IN THE `GET' ARM, AND IT IS THE ONLY ARM IN EITHER SWITCH THAT DISAGREES WITH THE CONSUMER.**\nIts sibling `GET RGBA' does the identical job one index lower and is correct, which is about as strong an\ninternal warrant as this tree offers.  The fix is the index triple: `(buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8)'.\n\n**WHY THIS MATTERS FOR THE ROUND'S SHAPE:** it converts an open design question into a one-line fix with a\nnamed oracle, and it supplies the detector's channel for free -- a PUT-then-GET round trip through RGB must\nreturn what was written, which is a value oracle, not a survival one.\n\n*The general lesson, and it is the option-set rule wearing another hat: I reached for an EXTERNAL authority\nbefore asking whether the tree already answered the question.  `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf' confirms there is no SGI\nor CRIME document here at all -- only MIPS manuals -- so the external route was not merely slower, it was\nCLOSED, and the in-tree consumer was the only available oracle the whole time.*\n\n*** A STRONGER ARGUMENT THAN THE CONSUMER ONE, AND IT NEEDS NO EXTERNAL REFERENCE AT ALL.  Enumerated\n2026-09-07 by walking all four modes rather than reasoning about them. ***\n\nThe note above identifies the GET arm as wrong using `dev_sgi_gbe.c` as an oracle.  That argument stands, but\nit depends on how GBE INTERPRETS an assembled value -- so here is the same conclusion from inside the\nfunction, depending on nothing outside it:\n\n    mode   PUT writes            GET returns    round-trips?\n    RGB    11 22 33 00           0x22330000     *** NO -- expected 0x11223300 ***\n    RGBA   11 22 33 44           0x11223344     yes\n    ABGR   44 33 22 11           0x11223344     yes\n\n**RGB IS THE ONLY MULTI-BYTE MODE THAT FAILS ITS OWN ROUND TRIP.**  And the two that pass do so using\nOPPOSITE byte orders -- RGBA ascending, ABGR descending -- which proves the file can express either\nconvention correctly.  So this is not a disagreement about endianness or about what the hardware packs; **RGB\nis simply inconsistent with itself**, and no datasheet is needed to say so.\n\n**AND IT NARROWS WHICH SIDE:** `PUT RGB' writes the top three bytes to `buf[0..2]', exactly as `PUT RGBA'\ndoes; `GET RGBA' reads `buf[0..3]' and is correct.  Since the two PUTs agree on `buf[0..2]', a correct\n`GET RGB' must read `buf[0..2]' too.  **It reads `buf[1..3]'.**  The GET arm is the outlier by the file's own\ninternal evidence.\n\n*** ONE THING THE ENUMERATION CORRECTED, AND IT WOULD HAVE READ AS A SECOND DEFECT: `CI' ALSO FAILS A NAIVE\nROUND TRIP, AND THAT IS CORRECT BEHAVIOUR. ***  `CI' is COLOUR-INDEX -- one byte -- so `PUT' storing only\n`buf[0]' and `GET' returning only `buf[0]' loses the upper 24 bits BY DESIGN.  A round-trip oracle applied\nblindly to all four modes would red on CI and manufacture a phantom defect.  **The detector must expect CI to\npreserve ONE BYTE, not four** -- which is exactly the kind of row that looks like a bug and is a\nmisunderstanding of the mode.\n\n**THE DETECTOR FALLS OUT OF THIS FOR FREE:** one PUT-then-GET row per mode, with RGBA and ABGR as ACCEPT rows\nthat must stay green (they are the de-escalation clause's accept side, already present) and CI pinned at one\nbyte.  The value oracle is the round trip itself -- no survival row, no exit-status channel, and nothing that\na message change could silence.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN, AND IT CAN MEASURE WHICH ARM IS WRONG RATHER THAN INFERRING IT.  Derived\n2026-09-07 from the XFER path. ***\n\nEverything above identifies the GET arm by ARGUMENT -- the in-tree consumer, and the fact that RGB alone\nfails its own round trip.  Both are sound.  **But the model makes the question directly measurable, and a\nmeasurement beats two good arguments.**\n\n`draw_primitive()` reads TWO INDEPENDENT guest registers:\n\n    dst_mode = d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_DST - 0x2000) / 4];\n    src_mode = d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC - 0x2000) / 4];\n\nand an XFER op **GETs with `src_mode` and PUTs with `dst_mode`** -- so the colour type of the read and of the\nwrite are separately selectable by the guest.  That yields a 2x2 that ISOLATES the defective arm:\n\n    src_mode   dst_mode   what it tests                       expected if GET RGB is the wrong one\n    RGBA       RGBA       CONTROL -- both arms known good     destination == source\n    RGB        RGBA       isolates the RGB **GET**            destination CORRUPTED\n    RGBA       RGB        isolates the RGB **PUT**            destination == source (PUT is fine)\n    RGB        RGB        the full defect, both arms          destination corrupted\n\n**The predicted corruption is deterministic and computable in advance**, which is what makes this a value\noracle rather than an inequality: `GET RGB` builds `(buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8)` from source bytes\n`B0 B1 B2 B3`, giving `B1 B2 B3 00`.  So a source of `0xaabbccdd` copied through RGB lands as **`0xbbccdd00`**\n-- a one-byte left shift with a zero fill.  A row can assert that exact value, not merely that something\nchanged.\n\n**AND THE 2x2 IS THE DE-ESCALATION CLAUSE'S ACCEPT SIDE FOR FREE**: the RGBA/RGBA row and the RGBA/RGB row\nmust stay GREEN after the fix, so the accept rows the clause demands are the same rows that isolate the\ndefect.  No extra sessions.\n\n**THE MODE WORDS**, from the same arithmetic as [[sgirebufuninit]]: LIN_A is bits 10-12 = `0x1000`, the depth\nfield is bits 8-9, the colour type bits 4-7.  With `BUFDEPTH_32` (field 2, `0x200`) so all four bytes\ntransfer:\n\n    RGBA   0x1220     RGB   0x1210\n\n`0x1220` is exactly what `regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py` already programs, and its `copy lin_a` row\nalready performs an XFER copy and reads the destination back.  **So the witness is that probe's existing\nsession with one register changed** -- which is why this is the cheapest live item on the board.\n\n*** ONE THING TO CHECK BEFORE TRUSTING THE 2x2, and it is the trap that caught the last round: does the XFER\npath actually reach `horrible_getputpixel` twice, once per mode?  ***  The GET is guarded by\n`drawmode & DE_DRAWMODE_XFER_EN`, and there is a SECOND `horrible_getputpixel(0, ...)` immediately above it\nthat reads the DESTINATION with `dst_mode` for the ROP path.  If that one also fires, three calls occur and\nthe 2x2's attribution is wrong.  **Enumerate the drawmode bits the session sets before building the rows** --\nthe previous round's witness failed twice on exactly this kind of unexamined session detail.\n\n*** THE WITNESS OBJECTIONS THAT KILLED [[sgirebufuninit]]'s DESIGN WERE CHECKED AGAINST THIS ROW AND DO\nNOT TRANSFER.  Checked 2026-09-07, not assumed. ***\n\nThe codex seat rejected `sgirebufuninit''s witness on two grounds.  Both were tested here:\n\n* **\"Changing that shared constant changes the observation path and the control simultaneously.\"**  True\n  there, because `MODE_LIN_A' serves the destination, the matched control AND a source case.  **Not true\n  here:** `setup(A, src_mode)' stores `DE_MODE_DST = MODE_LIN_A' (hardcoded) and `DE_MODE_SRC = src_mode'\n  (the parameter).  Passing `0x1210' changes ONLY the source mode; the destination and the control keep\n  `MODE_LIN_A' untouched.\n* **\"Separate automatic objects have no architectural promise of retaining each other's contents\"**, and\n  \"observing an unassigned byte does not guarantee that its value differs from the intended padding\".\n  Decisive against an uninitialised-memory oracle.  **This row's oracle reads no unassigned byte at all:**\n  a `PUT RGB' followed by a `GET RGB' on a KNOWN source is fully defined behaviour, and\n  `0xaabbccdd -> 0xbbccdd00' is a deterministic byte re-index, not a stale-value comparison.\n\n**So this row keeps a real value oracle while `sgirebufuninit' needs initializedness instrumentation** --\nwhich is why the two rounds are different in cost by a wide margin, and why this one is next despite the\nother's higher profile earlier tonight.\n\n*Recorded here so the round that takes this does not have to re-derive whether a rejection aimed at a\nneighbouring row applies to it.  A refutation is as transferable as a warrant, and needs checking the same\nway.*\n\n*** PASS 1 PANEL, 2026-09-07 (_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055).  THE PANEL REFUTED MY ATTRIBUTION, A DIFFERENT FILE THEN\nSETTLED IT, AND A SEAT CAUGHT A DEFECT IN MY OWN ORACLE. ***\n\n**1. THE ATTRIBUTION WAS NOT ESTABLISHED, and four seats said so independently.**  My brief argued the\nGET is the wrong arm on two grounds:\n  * **Ground 1 was CIRCULAR** -- *\"PUT's `buf[3] = 0` is an explicit pad write, so a GET reading byte 3\n    reads the pad\"* assumes PUT is authoritative, which is precisely the question.\n  * **Ground 2 was neutralised** -- I appealed to RGBA's byte order, but ABGR uses the opposite one and\n    the default/raw GET arm uses ABGR order too, so the file offers no single order to appeal to.\n  * **The measurement is ARM-NEUTRAL** -- 0xbbccdd00 is what the code does; under a pad-FIRST container\n    it is the FAITHFUL result and PUT is the bug.  A round-trip failure cannot say which end failed.\n\n**2. THE SETTLING FACT IS IN `dev_sgi_gbe.c`, AND NO SEAT FOUND IT.**  The scanout reads the same tile\nmemory back for display:\n\n    color = (buf[i]<<24) + (buf[i+1]<<16) + (buf[i+2]<<8) + buf[i+3];   /* byte 0 = MSB */\n    case CRMFB_MODE_TYP_RGB8:      // Used by NetBSD's X11 server\n            *r = color >> 24;  *g = color >> 16;  *b = color >> 8;      /* byte 3 UNUSED */\n\n**R = byte 0, G = byte 1, B = byte 2, byte 3 ignored -- exactly what the PUT arm writes.**  The pad sits\nat buf[3] because the CONSUMER ignores buf[3], not because PUT writes a zero there.  Different file,\nopposite direction, independent of which DE arm is right: that is what makes it non-circular, and it is\nthe answer to the Q2 I asked and nobody could reach from the packet.\n\n**3. glm INDEPENDENTLY DISSOLVED THE ABGR OBJECTION**, which is the sharpest single contribution of the\npanel: *\"for the same color, RGBA and ABGR write IDENTICAL memory bytes, [R,G,B,A]; they differ only in\nregister word order ... among the five 4-byte arms, the RGB GET is the lone container outlier -- your\ncase is stronger than you stated.\"*  Verified by reading: RGBA word 0xRRGGBBAA -> buf [RR,GG,BB,AA];\nABGR word 0xAABBGGRR -> buf [RR,GG,BB,AA].  **My \"opposite byte orders\" was about the register WORD.  The\nquestion is about the memory CONTAINER, and on the container all three 4-byte PUT arms agree.**\n\n**4. *** THE PANEL CAUGHT MY ORACLE, AND IT WOULD HAVE FAILED A CORRECT FIX. *** **  The witness's\nsuccess row pinned **0xaabbccdd**, the source word.  A corrected RGB GET returns **0xaabbcc00** -- RGB\ncarries three channels and the pad is not transported.  **Four seats named 0xaabbcc00 independently**\n(grok x5, glm x3, kimi-substitute x2, codex x2) against a brief that had said otherwise.  Re-pinned\nBEFORE the code changed, and the committed build re-measured RED under the new expectation -- the\noracle-correction rule's order discriminator, satisfied rather than asserted.\n\n*The row that survives is stronger for it: `rgb chan` pins the EXACT post-fix value, so a fix that moves\nthe bytes to some THIRD wrong place still reds -- which a \"not the corrupt value\" row could not do.*\n\n**5. codex's RESERVATION IS KEPT AND IS IN THE CONTRACT:** *\"GET is the best-supported repair site for\nthis emulator's RGB layout; hardware fidelity and real-driver usage remain unproved.  The cited MTE RGBA\nbranch is unreachable.\"*  No CRIME/RE datasheet is in `_scratchpad/` (checked by `ls`), so this ships as\nan INTERNAL-CONSISTENCY fix warranted by the tree's own consumer -- **not a claim about silicon**.\n\n**6. glm's MTE `|=` HAZARD -- checked, and its antecedent is FALSE.**  glm warned that\n`src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` yields ABGR (0x30) *if the type field already holds RGB (0x10)*, and said\nplainly it could not settle it from the packet, naming the settling fact.  I checked it:\n`src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & 0x300) >> 8) << 8)` sets bits >=10 and bits 8-9 only -- **the type\nnibble is never set**, so `|= 0x20` gives exactly RGBA.  The hazard does not fire.  *It is still a real\nlatent note -- if that construction ever gained a type field, `|=` would corrupt it rather than replace\nit -- and codex separately confirmed the whole branch is unreachable anyway (`sgimtedepth4dead`).*\n\n**SEATS: 6 of 7 scriptable answered.**  codex 233 KB, grok 12 KB, glm 9.9 KB, minimax 6.8 KB,\nkimi-substitute 6.9 KB, deepseek 2.7 KB.  The **kimi CLI is 403-walled** on its weekly window (350-byte\nfile, correctly reported WALLED rather than scored as a short answer) and its seat is **COVERED** by the\n`kimi-k3:cloud` substitute.  **agy DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED** -- measurement reserve, owner ruling.\n**Opus 5 and Fable 5 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED** -- both held entire for the 2026-09-09 adjudication by\nstanding owner directive.  *Three seats unfired for three different stated reasons; none is a seat\nfailure and none is agreement.*\n\n*** KILL TABLE 1 FOUND A CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH IN MY OWN DETECTOR.  L2 -- REPAIRED IN-ROUND. ***\n(`regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/killtable_20260907/`, 6-row detector)\n\n    base            6/6\n    M1_verbatim     4/6   KILLED  -- clause (b), by BOTH rgb rows\n    M2_off1         5/6   KILLED\n    M3_pad          5/6   KILLED -- by rgb chan ONLY\n    M4_or           6/6   EQUIVALENT, proven -- not a coverage gap\n    M5_rev          5/6   KILLED\n    M6_cond         6/6   *** CLEAN SWEEP -- FLOOR BREACH ***\n    nullmedium      1/6   clause (c) discharged\n\n**M6_cond is the canonical clause-(d) form** -- guard KEPT, repair CONDITIONED on a field the guarded\nbody reads:\n\n    case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:\n            if (bufdepth == 4)  *color = (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8);   /* the fix   */\n            else                *color = (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8);   /* the defect */\n\n**Every session in the 6-row detector used `BUFDEPTH_32`, so `bufdepth` was 4 in all of them and the\nmutant's FIX branch is the only one the detector ever executed.**  The detector proved the fix works\nexactly where it was measured and said nothing whatever about anywhere else.\n\n`bufdepth` is squarely inside the DEPENDS-ON closure and not an exotic choice: it is read by the guarded\nbody (it is `memory_rw`'s transfer length) and it comes from **bits 8-9 of the SAME mode word** whose\nbits 4-7 the guard reads.  The closure enumerates it first, not last.\n\n**REPAIR: `rgb depth64`, a second session at `BUFDEPTH_64` (bufdepth 8).**  Width 8 keeps `buf[0..2]`\nfilled by `memory_rw`, so the row stays a VALUE oracle; widths 1 and 2 would leave `buf[1..2]`\nuninitialised, which is `sgirebufuninit` and would make the row non-deterministic.  **8 is the only\nother width that can carry a value oracle at all**, which is why the repair is one row rather than a\nsweep of the axis.\n\n*** AND THE REPAIR OWES ITS OWN MIRROR, WHICH IS WHY TABLE 2 CARRIES `M7_cond8`. ***  The class-closure\namendment says a repair made at exactly the address the mutant used leaves the symmetric cell open --\n`#499` paid for that three times running.  M6 exempts the width the OLD rows use; M7 exempts the width\nthe NEW row uses.  If M7 escapes, the repair reproduced the defect it was built for, and the honest\ndisposition is to close the WIDTH AXIS in one diff rather than add a third cell.\n\n**Table 2 also carries `M8_put`, which measures rather than asserts what `rgb samemode` bought.**  Two\nseats said a same-mode row would reach the RGB PUT arm that nothing else executes; M8 corrupts that arm\nand nothing else.  *A row added on a seat's say-so and never measured is an assumption wearing a row's\nclothes.*\n\n*** PASS 2: THE glm SEAT BURNED, AND ITS ANSWER IS THE BEST CRITIQUE OF THE WARRANT ANYONE HAS\nGIVEN.  Recovered to `regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/glm_BURN_thinking_20260907.txt`. ***\n\n**THE BURN, by the carrier's own discriminator:** `\"response\": \"\"`, `thinking` 114,797 chars,\n`done_reason: \"stop\"` -- the model FINISHED, so the budget did not run out; the answer simply never\nreached the response channel.  **A size check scores this the best answer in the panel (120 kB, second\nonly to codex) and a naive check scores it DOWN.**  The carrier's rule is what got it read: an empty\n`response` is the diagnostic, `done_reason` decides the disposition, and **the thinking is read even\nwhen there is no channel marker.**\n\n**IT ANSWERED MY HEADLINE Q1 AND THE ANSWER IS THAT MY WORRY WAS UNFOUNDED:** *\"The\nguest-programming concern is a red herring -- the warrant compares two STATIC interpretations\nhard-coded in the emulator (GBE's RGB8 byte->bit mapping; DE PUT's register->byte mapping).  Guest\nprogramming selects which interpretation each device applies but cannot change either mapping, so no\nguest-consistency assumption is needed for the layouts to agree.\"*\n\n**AND THEN IT FOUND WHAT I HAD SMUGGLED IN -- three facts the packet asserts and never shows:**\n  1. **that GBE scans the SAME memory the DE writes** -- *\"asserted twice, never inlined.  If GBE\n     scans a copy or a different aperture, the warrant collapses to 'two devices with\n     similar-looking code'\"*;\n  2. **that the RGB PUT writes R at `buf[0]`** -- only the comment asserts it, and the diff does not\n     include the PUT arm;\n  3. the GBE RGB8 bit-position claim itself.\n  4. *(noted, unfalsifiable)* \"authoritative consumer\" presumes the GBE code is correct, and two\n     agreeing paths by the same author share blind spots.\n\n*** THE DECOMPOSITION IS THE VALUABLE PART AND IT MAKES THE ROUND'S POSITION STRONGER, NOT WEAKER: ***\n*\"the fix is round-trip-correct for ANY channel order ... So the fix is SAFE ON ROUND-TRIP GROUNDS\nALONE; the GBE warrant is only needed for the ARM-CHOICE, and it stands or falls with fact (2).\"*\n\n**That separates two claims I had run together.**  The FIX's correctness follows from PUT/GET\nsymmetry and needs no external warrant at all.  The GBE evidence is needed only to justify editing\nthe GET rather than the PUT.  A future reader who doubts the scanout argument still has a correct\nfix; they only lose the reason for the arm choice.\n\n**FACT (1) CHECKED AND CONFIRMED, with a qualification glm could not reach from a packet.**  Both\ndevices address the same space by the same formula:\n\n    GBE   tile[i] = (256*ptr[i*2] + ptr[i*2+1]) << 16;\n          memory_rw(..., base + 512*line, buf, len,      MEM_READ,  NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);\n    DE    tileptr = d->re_tlb_a[tile_nr] << 16;\n          memory_rw(..., tileptr + ofs,   buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS | PHYSICAL);\n\nSame `<< 16` on a 16-bit tile pointer, same PHYSICAL path.  **The qualification: WHICH tiles each\ntouches comes from separately-programmed pointers** (GBE's tiletable in memory, DE's `re_tlb_a`\narray), so the honest statement is *\"whatever tile the guest points both at, DE writes and GBE reads\nwith the same byte-to-address mapping\"* -- which is what a framebuffer driver does and is enough for\nthe arm choice.\n\n**FACT (2) IS A NAMING CLAIM, AND THE WARRANT DOES NOT NEED IT.**  glm is right that only the comment\ncalls `buf[0]` \"R\".  The tighter form drops the channel names entirely: **byte 0 carries bits 31..24\nin BOTH directions** -- DE PUT writes `*color >> 24` there, GBE's RGB8 reads bits 31..24 out of the\nword it composes with `buf[0]` as MSB.  The chain closes on BIT POSITIONS; the channel names are\ndecoration.  **The shipped comment is accurate in the tree's own vocabulary but over-specified**, and\nthe bit-position form is what a future reader should get -- an L1 tightening, since the round has not\nshipped.\n\n*This is the third time this session that the most useful finding came from a seat that a size or\nstatus check would have mis-scored: the kimi CLI walled and its substitute answered, grok looked\nstarved at 354 bytes and was mid-answer, and glm looked like the panel's best answer and had an empty\nresponse field.*\n\n*** PASS 2 FOUND THREE DEFECTS IN MY OWN RECORDS AND ONE MIS-BUILT MUTANT.  The kimi SUBSTITUTE\n-- a PACKET-FED transport that cannot read the repo -- found all four from the packet's own\narithmetic. ***  Panel `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_104814`.\n\n**FINDING 1 -- MY \"MIRROR\" MUTANT WAS NOT A MIRROR, and this is the serious one.**\n\n    kimi: \"M7_cond8 cannot be both 'the mirror of M6' and score 7/8; a mirror predicts 5/8.\"\n\n    M6_cond   if (bufdepth == 4) FIXED else DEFECT   ->  width 4 fixed, width 8 defective\n    M7_cond8  if (bufdepth != 8) FIXED else DEFECT   ->  width 4 fixed, width 8 defective\n\n**Over the only two widths any row drives, {4, 8}, those are the SAME FUNCTION.**  M7 re-measured\nM6 and I recorded it as having closed the symmetric cell.  It had measured nothing new.\n\n*** MEASURED, table 3 (`killtable3_20260907/`): the TRUE mirror `M9_truemirror`\n(`if (bufdepth == 8) FIXED else DEFECT`) scores **5/8**, EXACTLY the score kimi derived from the\npacket. ***  Three width-4 rows red.  **So the width axis IS closed -- but by `M9`, not by the\nmutant the earlier record credited.**  The conclusion survives; the evidence for it did not, and\na claim resting on a mutant that measures nothing is a wrong record however right its conclusion.\n\n**FINDING 2 -- THE LOCATION AXIS, and the measurement went the other way from the prediction.**\nkimi named `ofs` as an unpinned clause-(d) axis and predicted `if (ofs == K)` sweeps 8/8, reasoning\nthat every row writes at ofs 0.  **`M10_ofs` (`if (ofs == 0) FIXED else DEFECT`) reds three rows\ninstead.**  The reason is a fact about the probe that the packet did not contain: the source pixel\nis read at `src_x = SRC_OFS = 0x40`, so `ofs` is 0x40 on every GET and 0 only on the destination\nPUT.  **The rows discriminate on location already -- by an accident of the probe's geometry rather\nthan by design**, which is worth recording as exactly that: covered, not intended, and therefore\nfragile to anyone who moves SRC_OFS.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- THE \"SAME SCRIPT\" CLAIM IS CONTRADICTED BY ITS OWN DENOMINATORS.**\n\n    kimi: \"S4's 'same script' is contradicted by its own denominators (4/6 -> 8/8).\"\n\nCorrect.  The witness ladder's clause (i) wants ONE script red pre-fix and green post-fix; I quoted\na 6-row pre-fix run against an 8-row post-fix run.  Table 3 measures `prefix8row` -- the shipped\n8-row probe against pre-fix source -- so the pair is genuinely same-script.\n\n**FINDING 4 (L4, CORRECTED IN BOTH PLACES) -- THE GATE COMMENT'S \"SILENTLY GREEN\" IS FALSE.**\nThe sibling block says a row name of 16+ chars makes the two-space pattern *\"UNSATISFIABLE, which is\nthe same trap in its other, SILENT direction: the check stops testing anything and stays green.\"*\nI copied that verbatim into the new block.  **kimi refuted it and the measurement agrees:** `count`\nreturns 0, `check` compares 0 against a wanted \"1\", and 0 != 1 FAILS -- loudly.  Rendered rows at\nlengths 15/16/17 match 1/0/0.  The unsatisfiable direction is SAFE for a want-1 check and silent\nonly where a check wants ZERO, which no row check here does.  Corrected in MY block (L1) and in the\nSIBLING's (L4, wrong record, which the stopping rule licenses).\n\n**minimax's axis, recorded and measured:** *\"the detector does NOT catch a PUT/GET pair that swap\nchannel bytes consistently, and does NOT exercise ABGR.\"*  The first is TRUE AND UNAVOIDABLE -- a\nconsistent swap round-trips by construction, so no round-trip oracle can see it, and that is a\nclause-(e) disclosure rather than a gap.  `M11_cmode` measures the colour-type conditioning form.\n\n*** AND THE SEAT THAT FOUND ALL FOUR CANNOT READ THE REPO. ***  Every one came from the packet's own\narithmetic -- comparing two mutant predicates, comparing two denominators, and re-deriving what an\nunsatisfiable grep does to a `check`.  **The doctrine says a packet-fed seat supplies attack\nreasoning and never repo fact, and that is exactly what happened**: it made no claim about the tree\nand four claims about my arithmetic, and was right about all four.\n\n*** THE REMAINING PASS-2 SEATS, READ IN FULL RATHER THAN BY VERDICT LINE -- WHICH IS HOW THE ONE\nNEW AXIS WAS FOUND. ***\n\n**AN EXTRACTOR BLIND SPOT, worth fixing separately:** `seat_verdicts.py` filed deepseek as *\"answered,\nNO VERDICT LINE (format miss)\"*.  **It has one** -- it just reads `VERDICT:` rather than `**VERDICT:`,\nso the pattern missed it.  Had I trusted the extractor's label, a seat with four substantive claims\nwould have been filed as a formatting problem.  *That is the unread-review class arriving through a\nTOOL rather than through inattention.*\n\n**deepseek made TWO claims that measurement refutes and ONE that is new and good.**\n\n  * **REFUTED -- *\"the gate block's two-space grep is unsatisfiable for every name shorter than\n    15\"*.**  It has the direction backwards.  A name of N chars is followed by `(16-N)+1` spaces, so\n    every N <= 15 leaves at least two and matches.  Measured over the shipped row names: live(4),\n    devsig(6), copy lin_a(10), rgb chan(8), rgb samemode(12), rgb not corrupt(15) -- **all six\n    match**.  Unsatisfiability begins at N >= 16, which is the case the block warns about.\n  * **REFUTED -- *\"`rgb not corrupt` is vacuous as inlined\"*.**  Table 2 measures it firing: it is one\n    of the FOUR rows that redden `M1_verbatim` (4/8).  It declines to fire on `M3_pad`, but that is\n    SPECIFICITY, not vacuity -- the row names one exact wrong value and M3 produces a different one,\n    which is why `rgb chan` exists beside it.\n  * *** NEW AND WORTH MEASURING -- the `tileptr` axis. ***  Its Q2: *\"Condition the corrected RGB GET\n    on `ofs == 0` (or on `tileptr == <the single value the probe uses>`).\"*  The `ofs` half was\n    measured and DIES (`M10_ofs` 4/8, because the source is read at `src_x = 0x40`).  **The `tileptr`\n    half is untested and is not the same question**: every row in this detector drives LIN_A, so a\n    repair conditioned on the ADDRESSING MODE -- e.g. `tileptr & 0x80000000`, the linear flag -- is\n    true for all eight rows and would sweep clean.  `tileptr` is read by the guarded body, so it is\n    inside the DEPENDS-ON closure, and \"condition the fix on linear-vs-tiled\" is a plausible edit\n    rather than a contrived one.  **Queued for a fourth table.**\n\n**grok's verdict, and it is the sharpest framing of the round:** *\"RATIFY THE ONE-LINE GET FIX; DO NOT\nRATIFY S2 AS STATED, THE DETECTOR'S CONTRACT TEXT, OR 'WIDTH IS THE ONLY OPEN (d) AXIS.'\"*  All three\nreservations are upheld by measurement: S2 needed the qualification four seats independently named;\nthe contract text overclaims; and width was NOT the only open axis -- location and addressing mode\nwere both unexamined, one of which is now measured closed and one of which is queued.\n\n*** FOUR SEATS INDEPENDENTLY NAMED THE SAME GAP IN S2 -- glm, kimi, minimax and deepseek -- and that\nunanimity IS worth something here, unlike unanimity on a brief-supplied fact: each derived it from the\npacket's own internal inconsistency (the DE mode register and the GBE mode register are different\nregisters) rather than from agreeing with me. ***\n\n**KILL TABLE 3 (`killtable3_20260907/`) -- every mutant pass 2 demanded, all killed, and the\nclause-(i) gap closed:**\n\n    base            8/8\n    M9_truemirror   5/8   the ACTUAL mirror -- exactly the score kimi derived from the packet\n    M10_ofs         4/8   location axis, killed (see below -- not for the reason predicted)\n    M11_cmode       7/8   colour-type conditioning, killed\n    prefix8row      4/8   *** the shipped 8-row probe against PRE-FIX source ***\n\n**`prefix8row` is the same-script pair the ladder wants** -- 4/8 red before, 8/8 green after, one\nprobe.  The earlier `4/6 -> 8/8` was two probes, which pass 2 caught from the denominators alone.\n\nRestore verified against SEC; the clone sentinel cleared.\n\n*** KILL TABLE 4 (`killtable4_20260907/`): A SECOND CLAUSE-(d) FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n    base          8/8\n    M12_linear    8/8   *** CLEAN SWEEP -- the addressing-mode axis was wholly unpinned ***\n\nEvery row drove LIN_A, so `linear` (`tlb_mode > 3`) was true in all eight and a repair conditioned on\nit was invisible.  `linear` DOMINATES the edited lines via `if (!linear && ...) return;`, so it is in\nthe DEPENDS-ON closure -- **L2, repaired in-round** by `rgb tiled`, a ninth row driving a TLB_A\nsource (src tiled, dst LIN_A deliberately: the arm under test is the GET, whose `linear` comes from\n`src_mode`).\n\n**The seat's FORM had to be corrected to make it measurable.**  deepseek named `tileptr`; that cannot\ndiscriminate, because `tileptr &= ~0x80000000` strips the flag before the switch.  *A prediction is\nnot a measurement until someone finds the variable the source actually keeps.*\n\n*** AND THE NEW ROW REPORTED MY OWN SETUP ERROR RATHER THAN PASSING QUIETLY. ***  First run: it read\n`0x77665544` = `FG_VAL`, which the probe's own `DE_FG` comment names as what a DECLINED get leaves.\n`re_tlb_a` is `uint16_t[256]` written by a halfword loop, not `uint32_t` like `re_linear_a`, so the\n`<< 32` shift correct for LINEAR_A lands in `re_tlb_a[2]` and leaves `[0]` zero -- `tileptr = 0`,\naccess declined.  Corrected to `<< 48`.  **A green row there would have been vacuous by\nconstruction: it would have measured a declined access rather than a tiled one.**\n\n**`rgb tiled` WENT RED TWICE BEFORE IT WENT GREEN, and both times it read `0x77665544` = FG_VAL,\nwhich this probe's own DE_FG comment names as what a DECLINED get leaves.**\n\n  1. **`re_tlb_a` is `uint16_t[256]` filled by a HALFWORD loop**, not `uint32_t` like `re_linear_a`.\n     An 8-byte store fills `[0..3]` from four big-endian halfwords, so `[0]` comes from bits 63..48.\n     The `<< 32` shift that is CORRECT for RE_LINEAR_A lands in `re_tlb_a[2]`.\n  2. *** A TILED TILE POINTER MUST CARRY ITS OWN VALID BIT. ***  `if (!(tileptr & 0x80000000))\n     return;` gates every access.  LIN_A sets the bit EXPLICITLY (`0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[n] <<\n     12)`), so a probe built from the linear path never learns it exists; TLB_A takes it from the\n     stored value, and since `re_tlb_a` is uint16_t, bit 31 of tileptr IS bit 15 of the register.\n     Fixed by `re_tlb_a[0] = 0x8002` -> tileptr 0x80020000 -> valid -> masked to 0x20000.\n\n**A row that had gone green through either mistake would have been VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION** --\nmeasuring a declined access while claiming to measure a tiled one.  It went red instead, twice, and\nnamed the value that explained why.\n\n*And a lesson about the breach: `M12_linear` swept 8/8 precisely because no row had ever driven the\nnon-linear path -- and closing it required two device facts the linear path conceals.  **An axis\nnothing exercises is also an axis nobody has had to learn**, which is why the cost of closing one is\nsystematically underestimated.*\n\n*** PASS 3 (panel_20260907_121317) REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN.  FIVE SEATS, ONE REASON, AND A\nTHIRD FLOOR BREACH. ***\n\n    codex   NOT ESTABLISHED YET | OPEN (split)\n    grok    NOT RATIFY -- do not close this chain\n    kimi-sub NOT CLOSED\n    deepseek NOT CLOSED\n    minimax  rgb tiled is NOT proven sound; the floor is NOT closed\n    glm     *** SEAT DOWN -- SECOND THINKING BURN OF THE DAY *** (121,216 chars recovered by hand to\n            `glm_BURN2_thinking_20260907.txt`; it was reconstructing the per-row kill matrix from the\n            SCORES alone and confirming each conditioning row is the unique killer of one mutant)\n\n**THE BREACH: the {tiled x bufdepth 8} JOINT CELL was unrowed**, so `(bufdepth == 4 || linear)` is\ntrue in all three rowed cells and scores 9/9 by construction.  Named INDEPENDENTLY by grok, the kimi\nsubstitute and deepseek; kimi wrote the mutant in exactly that form from the packet alone.\n\n**It is the class-closure amendment's own predicted failure**: I repaired cell-by-cell twice, at\nexactly the address each mutant used.  Closed with `rgb tiled64` IN ONE DIFF with the gate fix.\n\n**grok additionally read the GATE against the PROBE** -- which no earlier review had done -- and\nfound `gate_arm.sh` pinning 8 rows and never naming `rgb tiled`.  It would have failed loudly rather\nthan silently, but it would have shipped, and that row would have been uncounted by name.\n\n*** TABLE 6 -- THREE ROWS ARE VOID, AND I VOIDED THEM MYSELF WITH A CHECK THE CARRIER SAYS\nCANNOT FAIL.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nI checked whether the table driver was alive with `ps -ef | grep -c python` and got **0**.  The\ndriver runs `python3` INSIDE WSL, and git-bash `ps -ef` cannot see WSL processes at all -- so that\ncheck returns 0 whether or not anything is running.  **It is the argument-blind process check the\ncarrier already records, reached by a second road: not 'the pattern names a script' but 'the\nprocess is on the other side of the WSL boundary'.**  I read the 0 as a dead run, wrote a resume\ndriver, and launched it into `/tmp/gx505f` **while the original was still building there**.\n\n**THE THREE TELLS I HAD AND MISREAD, in the order they appeared:**\n  1. `M14_compmirror.raw` was 787 bytes and ended at row 6 with no `SGIRGB_RESULT` -- which I read\n     as a truncation.  It was a file **being written**.  The completion-sentinel rule is right and I\n     applied it to the wrong hypothesis: absent sentinel means *not finished*, and *not finished*\n     includes *still going*.\n  2. The `.MUTANT` sentinel said `M14_compmirror` -- **the sentinel was CORRECT and current**, and I\n     treated a live sentinel as a crash residue.\n  3. `grep` reported `binary file matches` on two logs.  That is not a size or a truncation signal;\n     it means the file contains NUL bytes.\n\n**THE EVIDENCE OF THE RACE IS IN THE ARTIFACTS AND IT IS UNAMBIGUOUS:**\n\n    prefix10row.raw   1295 bytes, 339 of them NUL     <- two writers, one seeking past the other\n    M14_compmirror    13:21:47   nullmedium 13:22:13   prefix10row 13:23:04\n\n`nullmedium` is written AFTER `prefix10row` by the driver, so a single run cannot produce that\nordering.  Two runs can.\n\n*** AND M14's LOG IS INTERNALLY IMPOSSIBLE, WHICH IS THE PROOF RATHER THAN THE SUSPICION. ***\n`M14 = cond(bufdepth == 8 || !linear)`.  The `rgb depth64` row drives `MODE_RGB64 = 0x1310`, so\n`bufdepth = 1 << ((0x13) & 3) = 8` and the condition is TRUE -- that row MUST read back the fixed\nvalue.  **It reads back the defect value**, and all four RGB rows report byte-for-byte what\n`M1_verbatim` reports (both 4/10).  A mutant conditioned on a predicate cannot score identically to\nthe unconditional defect unless the binary measured was not the binary intended.\n\n> **VOID: `M14_compmirror`, `prefix10row`, `nullmedium`.**  All three were written inside the race\n> window (13:21:47 onward).  **`base` and `M1`-`M13` STAND**: every one was written before the\n> second driver launched, and `M13_composite` at 13:14:49 -- the mutant this table exists for, at\n> **9/10, KILLED** -- is clear of the window by seven minutes.\n\n**The three void rows are re-measured ALONE, in a FRESH clone, with no concurrent driver.**  Until\nthen table 6 is a 13-of-16 table and the class-closure claim is UNDISCHARGED -- `M14` is the mirror\ncell that closes the width x addressing 2x2, so its absence is exactly the hole pass 3 refused to\nclose the chain over.\n\n*The carrier's remedy was available and I did not take it: **long jobs go to `run_in_background`,\nwhich the harness tracks and notifies on**, so no process check is needed and a second launch\ncannot happen.  The original run WAS launched that way -- I then ignored the harness's own tracking\nand asked the operating system instead.*\n\n> **THE RULE THIS ADDS, and it is narrow: WHEN A HARNESS-TRACKED TASK HAS NOT NOTIFIED, IT IS\n> RUNNING.**  That is what tracking means.  A process check can only ever weaken that -- it cannot\n> strengthen it -- and on this machine it cannot even weaken it honestly, because it is blind to\n> WSL in one direction and to arguments in the other.\n\n*** TABLE 6 COMPLETE AND CLEAN, 2026-09-07, AFTER THE THREE RACED ROWS WERE RE-MEASURED ALONE IN\nA FRESH CLONE (`/tmp/gx505g`). ***\n\n    base_recheck     10/10   <- the FRESHNESS PROOF: the clone reproduces the shipped score\n    M14_compmirror    7/10   KILLED   (the raced run said 4/10)\n    prefix10row       4/10   clause (i), the SHIPPED 10-row script against PRE-FIX source\n    nullmedium        1/10   clause (c), identity rows\n\nEvery log carries **0 NUL bytes** and ends `SGIRGB_DONE`; the restore passed the two-part clearance\nand the sentinel cleared.\n\n**M14's CLEAN VALUE IS INTERNALLY COHERENT WHERE THE RACED ONE WAS NOT, and that is the proof the\nre-measure was necessary rather than merely cautious.**  `M14 = cond(bufdepth == 8 || !linear)` uses\nthe FIXED code exactly where the mode is 64-bit or tiled:\n\n    rgb chan / not corrupt / samemode   LIN_A, depth 4     predicate FALSE -> defect -> RED\n    rgb depth64   0x1310 -> 1<<(0x13&3) = 8                predicate TRUE  -> fixed  -> green\n    rgb tiled     0x0210 -> TLB_A, !linear                 predicate TRUE  -> fixed  -> green\n    rgb tiled64   0x0310 -> both                           predicate TRUE  -> fixed  -> green\n\nThree red, seven green = 7/10.  **The raced log had `rgb depth64` showing the DEFECT value against\nits own TRUE predicate**, which is what exposed the race in the first place.\n\n> **THE WIDTH x ADDRESSING 2x2 IS NOW CLOSED BY A MEASURED MIRROR PAIR** -- `M13_composite`\n> (`bufdepth == 4 || linear`) at 9/10 and `M14_compmirror` (`bufdepth == 8 || !linear`) at 7/10, both\n> KILLED.  That is what pass 3 refused to close the chain without, and it is the class-closure rule\n> discharged rather than argued.\n\n*** CLOSED 2026-09-08.  IT WAS FIXED BY `#505` AND THE ROW WAS LEFT OPEN -- a stale row, found\nwhile scoping a DIFFERENT round in the same file. ***\n\nThe fix is in the tree and carries its own provenance comment: the GET arm now reads\n`(buf[0] << 24) + (buf[1] << 16) + (buf[2] << 8)`, with a `#505:` block above it recording that it\nused to read `buf[1..3]` and why the PUT arm is the authoritative one.  `CHANGELOG.md` carries a full\n`#505` round block, and the commit is **`7a41b3f`** (\"#505 #506 #507 #508: four test-first rounds,\nthree new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\").\n\n    state    open   -> closed\n    commits  None   -> 7a41b3f\n    witness  None   -> probe:regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py\n\n*** AND `#505`'s CHANGELOG BLOCK CITES A WITNESS PATH THAT DOES NOT EXIST.  Corrected in the same\npass. ***  It names `regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/rgb_witness.py`; that file is absent and, on\nthe evidence of the artifacts directory, never existed under that name.  The witness is\n**`regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`** (27,831 bytes), whose own header opens *\"sgirergbroundtrip --\nRUNG-3 WITNESS for dev_sgi_re.c horrible_getputpixel()'s RGB arms\"* and restates the defect exactly.\nSo the witness is real and only the pointer was wrong.\n\n> **A ROW LEFT OPEN OVER A SHIPPED FIX IS THE OVERTAKEN-ROW SHAPE WITHOUT THE OVERTAKING.**  The\n> queue said a med defect was live in a file three rounds were actively editing.  I read this row\n> while deciding the scope of the `sgirandomcolour` round, took its title at face value, and started\n> designing around a defect that was already gone -- *which is the second time this session a stale\n> record nearly shaped a round*.\n\n*Found by reading the SOURCE while assembling a cluster brief, not by any check.  Nothing in the\nharness compares a row's open/closed state against the presence of its own fix in the tree, and this\nis not an argument that something should: the honest position is that it was luck, and the cheap\ndefence is the one that worked -- open the file before believing a row's title.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31507:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **FOUND BY THE CODEX SEAT** on an unrelated brief, and VERIFIED at HEAD before filing.\n\n    int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);\n\n`MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK' is `0x00000300' and `MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT' is 8, so the extracted field is TWO BITS, 0..3,\nand `depth' takes exactly the values **8, 16, 32, 64**.  The function then tests `if (depth == 4)', which is\ntherefore **never true** -- the guarded block is dead code.\n\ncodex's reading of what is lost: the dead block is *\"its intended RGBA construction\"*, so *\"its helper calls\nretain CI\"* -- i.e. the transfer runs in colour-index mode where the author meant RGBA.  **That second half\nis NOT verified here** and is the part a round would have to establish; what IS verified is that the branch\ncannot execute.\n\n*** IT ALSO REFUTES A PREMISE OF THE `sgirebufuninit' BRIEF, which is why it was filed rather than noted. ***\nThat brief asks whether callers constrain the (colour type, depth) pair.  Here is a caller that TRIES to and\nFAILS SILENTLY -- so the model's own producer does not supply the pair its author intended.  Any round\nreasoning about what callers guarantee must account for this one.\n\nLOW because a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything; the SEVERITY lives in whatever the missing RGBA\nconstruction was supposed to do, which is unestablished.\n\n*** RAISED LOW -> HIGH.  I FILED THIS SAYING `a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything' AND THAT IS\nWRONG: THE DEAD BRANCH IS WHAT MAKES THE MTE PATH LEAK.  Found in the kimi seat's pass-1 answer\n(regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/pass1_panel/kimi.txt) -- which I had already opened and under-read -- and VERIFIED here. ***\n\n    src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);\n    dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);\n    if (depth == 4) {                     <- DEAD: depth = 8 << field, so 8/16/32/64, never 4\n            src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;\n            dst_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;\n    }\n\n**The constructed modes carry NO colour bits, so the colour type is 0x00 = CI.**  The only thing that would\nmake them RGBA is the dead branch.  So **the MTE path always runs CI** -- and CI's PUT arm fills ONLY\n`buf[0]' while the transfer is `bufdepth' bytes.\n\n    MTE_DEPTH_8  = 0  -> bufdepth 1   matched, no leak\n    MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1  -> bufdepth 2   fills buf[0], writes 2  -> 1 uninitialised byte per pixel\n    MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2  -> bufdepth 4   fills buf[0], writes 4  -> 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel\n\n**All three are DOCUMENTED constants in `crmfbreg.h'.**  kimi: *\"every MTE_TLB_A/B/C fill with MTE_DEPTH_16/32\nleaks buf[1..3] per pixel (documented MTE_DEPTH values!).  This is the strongest reachability evidence:\nPROM/NetBSD crmfb_fill_rect uses this path.\"*\n\n**SO THE FIX ORDER MATTERS AND IS NOT WHAT I ASSUMED.**  Repairing `depth == 4' ALONE would switch the MTE\npath to RGBA, which fills `buf[0..3]' -- accidentally masking the leak at depth 32 while leaving depth 16 (2\nbytes, RGBA fills 4) and every DE-path case untouched.  **A round that fixes only this would look like it\nfixed [[sgirebufuninit]] and would not have.**  That is the shape the vacuity floor exists to catch, arriving\nbefore either round starts.\n\n*** WHOLE-FUNCTION AUDIT, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_audit_20260907.txt).  MY Q4 CONCLUSION IS PARTLY\nWRONG AND THE SCOPE RULING IS FINER THAN `SEQUENCE, DO NOT SHARE'. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"Q1 is correct for the tiled transfer path.  Q4 is partly wrong: correcting the condition for\n32-bit transfers leaves depth 16 in CI.  Additional defects exist; the current `zerobuf' chunk sizing is\nbounded.\"*\n\n**WHAT I GOT RIGHT AND WHAT I MISSED.**  I argued that repairing `depth == 4' alone would switch the path to\nRGBA and MASK the leak at depth 32.  True for depth 32 -- and I stopped there:\n\n    depth 32   -> RGBA both ways; four source bytes preserved, all four transmitted bytes initialised\n    depth 16   -> STAYS CI: two bytes transmitted, ONE initialised          <- I missed this\n    depth 64   -> STAYS CI: eight bytes transmitted, ONE initialised        <- I missed this entirely\n\n**So the repair masks ONE of three leaking depths and leaves two.**  My sentence said it leaves \"depth 16 and\nthe whole DE path\"; depth 64 was not in it at all.\n\n**And forcing RGBA at depth 16 would not help either:** *\"its PUT would initialize four local bytes but\ntransmit only two.  Its GET would still decode four bytes after reading only two, exposing another helper\ninitialization problem.\"*  The colour type and the transfer width are independent, so no choice of colour\nfixes a width mismatch.\n\n**THE SCOPE RULING IS FINER THAN MINE.**  I said the two defects must be SEQUENCED rather than shared.\ncodex: *\"The defects CAN share a change round, but successful 32-bit MTE behavior would then provide no\nindependent evidence that the shared helper defect was fixed.  For separate rounds, handling the helper\ndefect first avoids that masking.\"*  **So sharing is permitted and costs the independent evidence** -- which\nis a vacuity-floor concern, not a reopening-rule one.  If separated, the HELPER (buf initialisation) goes\nFIRST.  That inverts nothing I had planned but states the cost precisely, which my version did not.\n\n**CONFIRMED SAFE:** the `zerobuf' chunk sizing is bounded -- so the earlier reading of that path stands.\n\n**RE-DERIVED, NOT NEW:** it independently found the RGB GET/PUT layout mismatch ([[sgirergbroundtrip]] --\nnow THREE independent derivations) and the MTE access handler's unreachable width branch ([[sgimtewidth]],\nalready filed, whose title already anticipates that the obvious fix would CREATE a width kill).  **No\nduplicate rows filed** -- the C0 check catching its second duplicate of the session.\n\n*** AND IT SELF-REPORTED A SCOPE VIOLATION, WHICH IS WORTH RECORDING BECAUSE NOTHING WOULD HAVE CAUGHT IT:\n*\"One faulty search exclusion returned forbidden artifact matches, crossing your scope; no finding above\nrelies on them.\"*  The brief forbade reading `regress/artifacts/'; a bad exclusion pattern let some through,\nand the seat said so unprompted and stated the consequence.  That is the honest form, and it is the same\ndiscipline this project asks of its own records. ***\n\n**INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED 2026-09-07 by the codex seat**, unprompted, on a panel about a different\ndefect in the same file (`#505`, `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_051055`).  Its verdict line ends:\n*\"The cited MTE RGBA branch is unreachable.\"*\n\nThat is a second seat, on a separate brief, reaching this row's central claim from the source rather\nthan from this row -- which is the distinction the seat ledger draws between a reading that RATIFIES a\nbrief-supplied premise and one that RE-DERIVES a fact the brief did not supply.  **The `#505` brief\nmentioned the branch only in a question about whether RGB mode is guest-reachable; it did not state\nthat the branch is dead.**\n\n*And the glm seat, packet-fed, raised a DIFFERENT hazard on the same two lines and was honest about not\nbeing able to settle it: it warned that `src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` yields ABGR (0x30) rather than\nRGBA IF the type field already holds RGB (0x10), and named the settling fact as \"the full mode\nconstruction around that line\".  Checked: `src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & 0x300) >> 8) << 8)`\nsets bits >= 10 and bits 8-9 only, so the type nibble is always 0 and `|=` gives exactly RGBA.  **The\nconditional is sound and its antecedent is false.**  It remains a real latent note -- if that\nconstruction ever gained a type field, `|=` would corrupt rather than replace it -- but it is moot while\nthe enclosing `if (depth == 4)` never executes, which is this row.*\n\n*** PASS-1 DESIGN AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex,\n`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  THE DISPOSITION IS **DEFER**, AND\nIT RE-ORDERS THE QUEUE. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Defer `depth == 32` activation.  Correct the comment now and fix buffer\ninitialization under `sgirebufuninit`.  The one-token repair changes defined fill behaviour as well\nas copy behaviour, and exposes another existing arithmetic defect through MTE.\"*\n\n**So the obvious repair is the WRONG one, for the reason the brief asked about:** making the dead\nbranch live is not a bug fix, it is enabling a path that has never executed in this fork, and it\nchanges **fills as well as copies**.  The addition licence wants a measured delta or a transferred\nwarrant; neither exists, and activation additionally surfaces a further arithmetic defect.\n\n**WHAT TO DO INSTEAD, in the seat's order:**\n  1. **Correct the comment.**  It asserts *\"this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit\n     transfers\"* -- a behaviour the code does not have.  That is a wrong record in product code and\n     is cheap to fix.\n  2. **Fix the buffer initialisation under [[sgirebufuninit]]**, which is where the defect actually\n     lives.  The dead branch is the MECHANISM of its reachability, not the defect itself.\n\n*** A PREMISE CORRECTION -- MY S2 WAS TOO BROAD, and this is the fourth of the session. ***  I wrote\nthat *every* MTE transfer runs through `horrible_getputpixel` with CI colour.  The seat scoped it:\n**TILED MTE transfers use this helper; LINEAR MTE fills use a separate path.**  So the exposure is\nreal but narrower than the row claimed, and any detector must select a tiled transfer deliberately\nrather than assume it.\n\n**Q1 CONFIRMED with its enumeration, which is worth keeping because it also bounds the selectors:**\nonly TLB selectors A, B and C reach this block -- the source check rejects larger ones -- and the\ndepth field maps as\n\n    field 0 -> depth  8 bits -> bufdepth 1 -> modes 0x000 / 0x400 / 0x800   (A / B / C)\n    field 1 -> depth 16      -> bufdepth 2 -> modes 0x100 / 0x500 / 0x900\n    field 2 -> depth 32      -> bufdepth 4 -> modes 0x200 / 0x600 / 0xa00\n    field 3 -> depth 64      -> bufdepth 8 -> modes 0x300 / 0x700 / 0xb00\n\n-- which confirms `depth` is never 4 and that the intended test was `depth == 32`.\n\n**QUEUE CONSEQUENCE:** `sgirebufuninit` is the round to run, not this one.  This row's own\ndisposition shrinks to a COMMENT CORRECTION plus a disclosure that the branch is dead, and it should\nbe taken as a records rider rather than as a behaviour round.  *That is the option-set discipline\nworking in the conservative direction for once: the answer was \"do less than you proposed\".*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED BY THE CODEX SEAT, 2026-09-07, while answering an unrelated design\nbrief on `sgirandomcolour` -- it was not asked about this row. ***\n\n*\"MTE's RGBA-selection branch is dead.  `depth` evaluates to 8, 16, 32, or 64, but the branch tests\n`depth == 4`.  Consequently, these helper calls retain CI, whose PUT fills only one byte even when the\ntransfer is wider.\"*  (`dev_sgi_re.c` depth calculation `:1311`, dead branch `:1365`.)\n\n**And it draws the boundary against [[sgirebufuninit]] in one sentence:** *\"Buffer initialisation\nwould address undefined bytes without restoring the intended multi-byte copying behaviour.\"*  So\nzero-filling `buf` does NOT subsume this row -- the wide copy stays broken, it merely stops being\nundefined.  That is the same boundary the `sgirebufuninit` panel drew from the other side.\n\n*** THE DIAGNOSIS IS A UNITS MISMATCH, AND THE CODE SAYS SO FOUR LINES EARLIER.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n    :1311  int depth = 8 << ((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT);   /* BITS */\n    :1359  src_x1 /= (depth / 8);                          <- the author converting bits -> bytes\n    :1369  // ... this makes sure that we copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers.\n    :1371  if (depth == 4) {                               <- compares BITS to a BYTE count\n\n**`depth` is in bits and takes 8/16/32/64; the test wants 4 BYTES.**  The comment states the intent\noutright -- *\"copy 32 bits when doing 32-bit transfers\"* -- so the intended predicate is\n`depth == 32`, and `:1359` shows the same function converting between the two units four lines above\nthe mistake.\n\n**AND THE CONSTANTS DEFINE ONLY THREE OF THE FOUR FIELD VALUES:** `crmfbreg.h:268-270` names\n`MTE_DEPTH_8 = 0`, `MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1`, `MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2`.  **Field value 3 -- depth 64 -- is named\nnowhere**, so the two-bit field is wider than the encodings that exist and a fix owes a decision\nabout it.\n\n> **MY ARITHMETIC FOR THE OBVIOUS FIX, offered to the seat as a question rather than a conclusion:**\n> `depth == 32` is field 2, giving `bufdepth = 1 << 2 = 4` bytes AND RGBA, which assigns `buf[0..3]`\n> -- matching the four-byte transfer exactly.  **But that repairs only the 32-bit case.**  Field 1\n> still moves 2 bytes with `buf[0]` assigned, and field 3 moves 8 with `buf[0]` assigned.  So the\n> one-token fix may be the same bug one layer up.\n\n*Design out to codex: `regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/DESIGN_brief.md`.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgimtedepth4dead/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: confirmed dead comparison; `depth == 32` is a justified NARROW correction. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, both toward less confidence:**\n  * **S3 needed the word PUT.**  The CI *PUT* initialises only `buf[0]`; a successful *GET* reads\n    `bufdepth` bytes into its own buffer and then retains only `buf[0]` in `*color`.  Different\n    mechanisms, and I had written them as one.\n  * **S4 OVERSTATED CAUSATION.**  This branch explains the 32-bit MTE mismatch, but **DE\n    independently reaches the buffer defects** -- so the dead branch is *a* mechanism of\n    [[sgirebufuninit]], not *the* mechanism, and it does not prove every affected copy observably\n    leaks.\n\n*** THE STRUCTURAL LIMIT, and it settles Q1 against the broader repair: THE HELPER CANNOT CARRY\nEIGHT BYTES AT ALL. ***  `*color` is a `uint32_t`, so no implemented type represents a 64-bit\ntransfer regardless of which type is selected:\n\n    8 bits    CI carries the one transferred byte\n    16 bits   CI loses the second; RGBA would make GET consume TWO BYTES BEYOND the two read\n    32 bits   RGBA carries exactly four        <- what `depth == 32' buys\n    64 bits   NO implemented type carries eight; the intermediate uint32_t cannot represent them\n\n**So 'select a type per width' is not a complete solution with this helper.**  A broader repair needs\nexplicit transfer-width handling or refusal of unsupported combinations, and *the scoped source\ncannot establish the hardware's intended 16-bit packing or any 64-bit format*.  The separate\n`DE_MODE_PIXDEPTH_*` definitions do not settle it -- **the helper does not interpret those bits.**\n\n**FIELD VALUE 3 IS GUEST-STORABLE AND REACHES THE TILED PATH** (`:1522` retains the field; the tiled\ndestination cases at `:1341` have no depth validation), and the emulator then treats it as an\neight-byte transfer.  *The separate LINEAR destination path rejects non-8-bit depth by exiting.*\n**But its hardware meaning is unknown**: the missing constant proves only that this header names\nthree encodings, not that the fourth is reserved -- and the regression's `MODE_RGB64` is no evidence\neither, being a DE test value I constructed locally.\n\n> **So a complete MTE repair owes an explicit DISPOSITION for value 3.  Declining it as UNIMPLEMENTED\n> is defensible pending a hardware contract; calling it RESERVED is not established.  A\n> comparison-only correction must leave that obligation recorded** -- which is what this note is.\n\n**Q5 -- THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT IS ONE CHARACTER:** after the fix, change `32` to `33` and the branch\nis unreachable again.  `rgb tiled64` and the other nine rows of the RGB script are all expected to\nstay green, **because they exercise DE and never construct an MTE mode.**  *Static prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**AND THE WITNESS HAS AN ORACLE PROBLEM WORTH RECORDING BEFORE ANYONE BUILDS ONE:** MTE is registered\nunconditionally beside DE and dispatches synchronously to guest physical memory, so it is reachable\n-- but *\"an affected copy can appear correct if the PUT's uninitialised tail happens to retain bytes\nfrom the preceding GET.  These are separate automatic objects; that preservation is neither\nguaranteed nor evidence of correctness.\"*  **So a simple copy-equality row cannot be trusted**, and\nfill needs separate validation from copy because fill bypasses the source conversion.  The sibling's\nDE programming also cannot be reused unchanged: its LIN_A destination is exactly what MTE's linear\npath exits on.\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN 2026-09-07: ACCEPT `depth == 32`, REFUSE MY ORACLE, and it is NOT a\n`detector:` round -- a FILL channel IS deterministic. ***\n\n**MY PROPOSED ORACLE WAS WRONG, and the reason is a fact about the write path:** CI PUT does not\nreplicate `buf[0]` across the span and does not leave a tail.  It writes `buf[0]` at the lowest\naddress and `buf[1..bufdepth-1]` as uninitialised stack, and `memory_rw` memcpys `bufdepth` bytes.\nSo a pre-painted destination is **overwritten with garbage**, not preserved:\n\n    belief                          what the code does\n    CI writes 1 byte, 3 stay paint  CI writes FOUR bytes at MTE_DEPTH_32; the tail is garbage\n    remaining paint IS the defect   on HEAD the tail is not paint -- the row reds because the path\n                                    RAN, which reads as \"the witness never reached MTE\"\n    tail != paint IS the defect     true on HEAD *and* after the fix.  Vacuous.\n    tail equals the previous GET    separate automatic buf[8] objects; neither guaranteed nor\n                                    evidence\n\n*** THE CHANNEL THAT WORKS: TILED **FILL** `dest[0]`.  FILL never GETs. ***  With `bg` chosen to\nhave four distinct bytes (`0x11223344`):\n\n    dest[0]   HEAD (CI)  0x44   -- buf[0] = (uint8_t)bg, the LOW byte\n    dest[0]   fix (RGBA) 0x11   -- RGBA lays [bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, bg], the HIGH byte\n    dest[1..3]                  -- uninitialised on HEAD: **DO NOT ASSERT**\n\n**COPY CANNOT WITNESS THIS AT ALL.**  Source `aa bb cc dd`: CI GET takes `buf[0] == 0xaa` and CI PUT\nwrites `0xaa` at `dest[0]`; RGBA GET reconstructs `0xaabbccdd` and RGBA PUT also writes `0xaa` at\n`dest[0]`.  **The first byte of a copy is the one byte CI already gets right**, and the broken bytes\nare exactly the uninitialised ones that must not be read.  *\"If the witness is a blit, refuse it.\"*\n\n**NOT a `detector:` round.**  The path is reachable and the attempt has not been made; it only looked\nimpossible because the oracle was bad.  Four ways to produce a FALSE \"cannot witness\": MTE LIN_A/B at\ndepth 32 (LINEAR `exit(1)`s -- host death, not a red row); COPY; asserting `dest[1..3]` on HEAD; and\n**TLB_A without bit 15**, which returns silently and looks like \"MTE did nothing\" -- the trap\n`sgi_re_rgb_probe.py` already paid for twice.\n\n**LEAVE FIELD 3 ALONE in this diff.**  A tiled decline is a SECOND SITE -- new predicate, new\nannounce, new accept row -- and it is not the units bug.  The tiled/LINEAR disagreement is\npre-existing and `if (depth == 32)` does not worsen it.  File field 3 and the LINEAR depth kill as\ntheir own row.\n\n**THE REGISTER MAP FOR THE BUILD, derived today so the next step is mechanical rather than a\nre-derivation:**\n\n    CRIME RE base            0xb5000000   (RGB probe: CRIME_RE_TLB_A 0x1000 -> 0xb5001000)\n    CRIME_MTE_MODE  0x3000   MTE_MODE_COPY 0x800 (1=copy, 0=CLEAR/fill -- FILL needs it CLEAR)\n    CRIME_MTE_BG    0x3018   MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK 0x300, MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT 8, MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2\n    CRIME_MTE_DST0  0x3030   MTE_MODE_DST_BUF_MASK 0x1c, MTE_TLB_A = 0\n    CRIME_MTE_DST1  0x3038   -> MTE_MODE for a tiled 32-bit FILL is 0x200\n    CRIME_DE_START  0x0800   ORed into the register address; `if (startFlag) do_mte_transfer()'\n                             at the END of DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_mte), so any MTE write with 0x800 set\n                             fires the transfer -- write DST1|START last\n    TLB_A[0] = 0x8002        BIT 15 IS THE VALID BIT (same constant the RGB probe uses)\n\n*NetBSD's `crmfb_fill_rect` is exactly this path -- FILL, tiled `MTE_TLB_A`, `MTE_DEPTH_32`, X\nbyte-scaled -- so the driver path and the oracle are the same path.*\n\n*** THE PROBE'S MODE VERIFIED SAFE BY READING, 2026-09-07 -- I answered my own review question\nrather than waiting for the seat, per *anything checkable in two minutes gets CHECKED*. ***\n\n`MODE_TILED_FILL = 0x200` passes all three pre-checks in `do_mte_transfer` before the dst switch:\n\n    :1323  dst_y_step != 0 && != 1 && != -1   dst_y_step is 0 (register never written)   SAFE\n                                              -- and its exit(1) is commented out anyway\n    :1328  mode & MTE_MODE_STIPPLE            STIPPLE is **0x400**; 0x200 & 0x400 == 0     SAFE\n    :1336  src > MTE_TLB_C                    src = (0x200 & 0xe0) >> 5 = 0 = MTE_TLB_A    SAFE\n\n**And my worry about the bytemask kill does not apply: `:1413 if (bytemask != 0xffffffff)` is inside\nthe LINEAR arm** (its own debug line at `:1407` says `LINEAR TRANSFER`), which a tiled fill never\nreaches.  *A zero bytemask would kill a LINEAR transfer and is invisible to the tiled path* -- worth\nknowing for anyone who later writes a linear MTE probe.\n\n`MTE_MODE_COPY` is `0x800` and is CLEAR in `0x200`, so this is a FILL and no GET runs -- which is the\nwhole reason the oracle is deterministic.\n\n*** PASS 1 PANEL, 2026-09-07, panel dir `_scratchpad/panel_20260907_170713`.  SEVEN transports\nfired; SIX answered with a verdict.  Seat-by-seat, because a seat that did not answer is a SEAT\nFAILURE and never agreement: ***\n\n    codex GPT-6 Astra max   ANSWERED 127,698 b, full verdict\n    grok 4.6 xhigh          ANSWERED  12,341 b\n    ollama glm-5.3          ANSWERED   7,686 b        ollama deepseek  ANSWERED 4,381 b\n    ollama minimax          ANSWERED   8,735 b        ollama kimi-k3   ANSWERED 8,014 b\n    kimi CLI                *** WALLED -- 403 weekly (7-day) usage limit, 350 b ***\n                            the SEAT IS COVERED by the kimi-k3:cloud substitute above\n    agy 3.7                 DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- measurement reserve (owner ruling)\n    Opus 5 / Fable 5        DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- held for the 2026-09-09 adjudication\n\n**THE CENTRAL CLAIM SURVIVED ALL SIX**, each by enumerating the four field values rather than\nagreeing: `depth = 8 << (2-bit field)` gives {8,16,32,64}, so `depth == 4` is unreachable, both\n`|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA` assignments are dead, every tiled transfer takes CI, and at 32 bits CI\nassigns `buf[0]` while `memory_rw` copies four.  **F1 (`depth == 32`) is unanimous.**\n\n*** THREE ERRORS IN MY OWN BRIEF, EACH FOUND BY A SEAT.  Recorded because the premise-audit\nopener is the one piece of prose in this project that demonstrably fires. ***\n\n**1. A WRONG NUMBER, caught INDEPENDENTLY BY TWO SEATS (kimi substitute and codex).**  I wrote\nthat F2 (`depth >= 32`) \"converts a four-byte problem into a four-byte problem at a different\noffset\".  At field value 3 the copy length is 8 and CI assigns ONE byte, so **SEVEN** are\nuninitialised; RGBA would assign four, leaving **FOUR**.  F2 is a REDUCTION, not a neutral\nswap.  *The conclusion (reject F2 for want of a semantic warrant) stands; the arithmetic under\nit did not, and a leak-count argument is exactly where that matters.*\n\n**2. A WRONG RECORD IN THE PROBE HEADER (codex).**  It said \"only the top byte of the\nbig-endian word is read\".  The probe executes `lw` and masks afterwards: it **READS four bytes\nand ASSERTS one**.  Corrected in place -- and the distinction is load-bearing, because the tail\nbecomes assertable the moment the fix lands, which is what `fill bot' now does.\n\n**3. AN IMPRECISE MECHANISM (kimi substitute).**  My `copy top' table said the dest-only cell\ngives 0x00 because \"the write puts the high byte of a low-byte colour first\".  It gives 0x00\nbecause the CI GET *assigns* `*color = buf[0]`, zero-extending; had the GET left `bg` untouched\nan RGBA PUT would have emitted `bg >> 24` = 0x11.  The row is right for a different reason, and\nthe reason is now in the row's own comment so a future seat does not \"correct\" the want to 0x11.\n\n*** TWO HOLES IN MY ROW SET, BOTH MEASURED-CLASS ESCAPES, BOTH CLOSED BEFORE TABLE 1. ***\n\n* **RGB-for-RGBA is invisible to dest[0] forever** (glm and grok, independently).  The RGB PUT\n  arm writes {bg>>24, bg>>16, bg>>8, **0x00**} -- IDENTICAL to RGBA at dest[0].  So a one-token\n  `|= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB' substitution passed all twelve rows I had proposed.  Closed by\n  **`fill bot'** (dest[3]: 0x44 vs 0x00), and on the copy side by **`copy 4th'**.\n* **Over-reach to depth 16 is unpinned** (glm).  `depth >= 16' fires at 32 (so `fill top' stays\n  green) and is cold at 8 (so `depth8 top' stays green).  **Nothing reds.**  Closed by\n  **`depth16 top'** -- the ADJACENT DOCUMENTED cell, which is why 8 alone was never enough.\n\n**AND A THIRD, FOUND BY THE PRE-TABLE CHECK ITSELF rather than by a seat:** `M8`, a SOURCE-side\nRGB substitution, escaped all twelve rows including `copy top' and `copy 2nd', because RGB's GET\ndrops only the LOW byte.  `copy 4th' (dest[3] = 0x4d) closes it and closes two other cells with\nit.  *That is what the block is for, and it fired on its first mandated application.*\n\n*** A NEW LIVE DEFECT VOLUNTEERED UNDER THE \"if you found something else\" LICENCE (codex), which\nturned out to be ALREADY FILED -- so it is recorded as an independent re-derivation, not a new\nrow. ***  The RGB and RGBA GET arms compute `buf[0] << 24` on a promoted `uint8_t` (ABGR and the default arm shift `buf[3]`; CI shifts nothing -- \"every GET arm\" was FALSE and the pass-2 codex seat caught it), which promotes to signed `int`;\nfor a first byte >= 0x80 the shift exceeds INT_MAX and is UB.  That is [[sgirebufshiftub]].\n**It changed this round's design:** the copy rows use `SRC_WORD = 0x1a2b3c4d`, first byte\ndeliberately below 0x80, so this detector does not stand on undefined behaviour.\n\n**THE SCOPE ANSWER, converged across four seats:** ONE round for the 32-bit cell -- it is the\nonly cell with an intent oracle (the comment names 32) and the only one with a UB-free channel.\nThe 16-bit and encoding-3 cells share a DIFFERENT defect (CI never fills `bufdepth` bytes) which\nbelongs to [[sgirebufuninit]] and must close in ONE round across all widths, **not one round per\ndepth**.  This is explicitly not the `#499` same-class-three-times shape.\n\n> *** THE TRAP GROK NAMED, AND IT GOES IN THE CHANGELOG VERBATIM: F1 fills buf[0..3] at depth 32,\n> so it ACCIDENTALLY MASKS the 32-bit tiled cell of [[sgirebufuninit]].  A later round that rows\n> only depth 32 on this path will look fixed and will not be.  KEEP THAT SIBLING ALIVE ON DEPTH\n> 16.  Do not write prose claiming F1 closed the leak. ***\n\n**SIBLING PARITY -- the one place two seats disagreed, settled by reading rather than by vote.**\ncodex called MTE_TLB_B and _C \"uncovered\" and wanted rows; grok held that the repaired comparison\ndoes not read `dst_tlb`, so a per-label row would pin the helper's ARRAY SELECTION -- a different\nproperty -- and called it \"cheap, not required\".  **grok's reading is adopted and the cell is\nDISCLOSED rather than rowed**, with the escape stated in `PRETABLE.out`: a repair conditioned on\n`dst_tlb == MTE_TLB_A` escapes this detector.\n\n**MEASURED, same script both sides (witness clause (i)):**\n\n    6-row witness   HEAD  5/6    witness_prefix_HEAD.raw\n    13-row detector HEAD  9/12 at 12 rows (re-measured in the clone as M1; see the table)\n    13-row detector FIX  13/13   witness_12row_FIXED.raw + the table's BASE row\n\n\n*** SHIPPED as `2956c7e` on 2026-09-07.  STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT `in work': both stages stand\nat 7/9 awaiting opus5 and fable5, and a row may not CLOSE while a stage awaits a seat.  The work\nshipping and the row finishing are different facts.  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv;\nsix questions for the adjudication in fable_queue.md 7g. ***",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31754:   "state_note": "**RULE QUESTION for the 2026-09-09 adjudication.  A TEMPLATE CHANGE MADE ON MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN BY\nRULING, disclosed as such.**  `BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md` gained a section C5 telling every codex brief to (1) use\nordinary engineering language and (2) scope the seat's reading to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.\n\n**THE EVIDENCE IS A FOUR-RUN EXPERIMENT WITH A CLEAN CONTROL** (see [[codexsubjectrefusal]]): vocabulary\nalone converted an immediate refusal into a run that worked for minutes, and scoping alone carried that run\npast the exact byte where the unrestricted one was flagged -- 279,132 vs 279,268, same distance, opposite\noutcome.\n\n**WHY IT NEEDS ADJUDICATION RATHER THAN JUST SHIPPING:** `BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md` was adopted by flagship\nadjudication and it is what EVERY brief starts from, so a section added to it changes how every future round\ntalks to a seat.  I made it structural rather than leaving it in prose because this file's own thesis is that\nprose does not fire -- but the same thesis says the template is load-bearing, which is exactly why the change\nshould be reviewed rather than assumed.\n\n**THE QUESTION, stated so a seat can answer it without reconstructing the context:**\n\n  1. **Is scoping the seat AWAY from `regress/artifacts/` and the ledger a REVIEW-QUALITY LOSS?**  Those\n     archives hold the kill tables and prior measurements.  A seat that cannot read them cannot check a\n     historical score -- and the pass-2 brief's own instructions sometimes ASK a seat to verify one.  The\n     measurement says reading them gets the seat refused; it does not say the reading was worthless.\n  2. **Is there a middle form** -- scope OUT by default, and inline the specific artifact when a question\n     depends on it?  That is what §C2 already mandates for the packet-fed seats, so the machinery exists.\n  3. **Does the vocabulary rule risk a WRONG RECORD?**  I claim it does not: rewording in-house slang into\n     the words an outside engineer would use kept every technical claim, and the answers got HARDER.  But\n     `kill' is this project's own term of art and a brief that avoids it is, in a small way, describing the\n     work differently from how the CHANGELOG describes it.\n\n**MY POSITION, so the seat has something to attack:** (1) is a real but small loss and (2) is the right\nanswer; on (3) the risk is negligible for BRIEFS and would be serious for RECORDS, and the section says so.\nReverting is one deletion if the adjudication disagrees.\n\n*** FOURTH REFUSAL, 2026-09-07, AND IT IS THE FIRST THAT PUTS TWO PROJECT RULES IN DIRECT\nCONFLICT.  The adjudication should rule on the conflict, not only on the section. ***\n\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/WITNESS_brief.md` obeyed BOTH existing C5 rules -- ordinary\nengineering language throughout, reading scoped to `src/` -- and was REFUSED on the prompt:\n\n    If this seems wrong, try rephrasing your request.  To get authorized for security work,\n    join the Trusted Access for Cyber program: https://chatgpt.com/cyber\n\n**It is TRIGGER 3 (chain-shaped content), not a new trigger** -- the brief carried a numbered\nfour-step procedure for exercising a PALcode handler and reading a value back out of guest memory.\n\n*** THE CONFLICT, AND IT IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN INCIDENTAL. ***  The lifecycle ruling of\n2026-08-27 MANDATES that before recording a round as rung-1 you *\"ask one seat to try a rung-3\nprobe\"* -- and it was minted precisely because a seat, asked once, built a rung-3 witness in a\nsingle invocation.  **But a witness DESIGN is a chain by construction**: step 1 set this up, step 2\ndrive it, step 3 read the observable.  There is no way to ask *\"is this witness design sound?\"*\nthat is not chain-shaped, because the design IS the chain.\n\n**So C5's stated mitigation -- split the chain into link questions -- does not fully apply here.**\nSplitting works when the chain is a CLAIM you want checked link by link.  It does not work when the\nchain is the ARTIFACT under review: you can ask the links (does the save precede the ctx update?\nis dispatch gated?) and the seat answers them well, but **the DESIGN itself then goes unreviewed and\nthe author assembles it alone** -- which is exactly the situation that produced this project's\nfour-readers-one-wrong-line incident.\n\n> **THE MITIGATION I APPLIED, AND IT COSTS NOTHING: SEND WITNESS-DESIGN QUESTIONS TO A NON-CODEX\n> SEAT.**  The roster has six other answering seats and only codex has this filter.  The chain-free\n> READ_brief.md went to codex (field-by-field audit, consumers, ordering, dispatch gating) and the\n> ORIGINAL chain-shaped WITNESS_brief.md went to grok -- a file-reading seat with no refusal on\n> record.  **Neither question was dropped and neither was weakened.**\n\n*That is a better answer than rephrasing, and it generalises: the refusal is a property of ONE\ntransport, so the roster routes around it.  The seat ledger already insists a seat's CLASS travels\nwith its transport; this says the same about what a transport will ANSWER.*\n\n**WHAT THE ADJUDICATION IS NOW ASKED, in addition to the three questions above:** should C5 gain a\nrouting clause -- *witness-design and reachability questions go to grok or the packet-fed seats by\ndefault, codex gets the reading questions* -- or is that conceding too much, given codex is the\nstrongest reading seat and the one whose answers have most often been right?\n\n*Apparatus note from detecting it: my first check was `grep -ci 'refus|cannot help|...'` over the\nstderr, which returned **12 hits on the HEALTHY run** -- the brief and the source legitimately use\n\"refusal\", \"decline\", \"declined\" throughout, since that is the subject matter.  **An unanchored\nkeyword grep cannot distinguish a refusal from a discussion of refusals**, which is this project's\nquoted-marker collision in a new place.  What settled it was reading the TAIL of each stderr: one\nended mid-file-read, the other ended in the cyber-program notice.*\n\n*** FIFTH REFUSAL, 2026-09-07, AND IT IS A FOURTH TRIGGER: THE DEFECT CLASS ITSELF. ***\n`regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/PASS1_brief.md`, refused after **43,839 tokens** of real work --\nso the flag came at the END, content-level, like the archive-reading case rather than the prompt-level\nvocabulary and chain cases.  Log: `codex_pass1_20260907.err`.\n\n**IT OBEYED EVERY EXISTING C5 RULE.**  Reading scoped to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.  **No chain** --\nno numbered procedure, no reachability question, no step-by-step anything; it asks four design\nquestions about where to put a `memset`.  Ordinary engineering register throughout.\n\n*** WHAT IS LEFT IS THE SUBJECT.  The defect IS information disclosure: uninitialised host stack\nreaching guest-visible memory.  There is no way to state that defect without stating it. ***\n\n**THAT MATTERS MORE THAN THE FOUR TRIGGERS BEFORE IT**, because the earlier ones all had a\nreformulation available.  Vocabulary could be reworded; archive reading could be scoped out; a chain\ncould be split into links, and where it could not -- a witness design -- it could be routed to grok.\n**A defect class cannot be reformulated away: describing an uninitialised-memory-to-guest defect in\nwords that do not describe it produces a brief about a different defect.**\n\n**AND THIS FORK'S QUEUE IS FULL OF THIS CLASS.**  `#95` is the tree's own root-cause fix for the\n\"uninit-read class\", with per-device predecessors `#91`, `#93`, `#94`; the live rows include\n[[sgirebufuninit]], [[sgirandomcolour]] (host RNG into guest memory) and [[sgimtedepth4dead]] (the\nmechanism that makes the first reachable).  **If the strongest reading seat cannot be asked about this\nclass, it is not available for a substantial fraction of the remaining work.**\n\n**MITIGATION APPLIED, and it is the routing answer again:** the brief went to **grok** unchanged.  A\nreworded codex version is a second experiment worth running -- but the wording that would pass is\nthe wording that omits the defect, so I expect it to answer a narrower question.\n\n**WHAT THE 2026-09-09 ADJUDICATION IS NOW ASKED, expanded from the three questions above:**\n  1. Is the routing clause -- *witness designs and disclosure-class defects go to grok and the\n     packet-fed seats; codex takes the reading questions* -- the right standing answer, or a\n     concession that quietly halves the roster's strongest seat?\n  2. **Does a refusal need to be RECORDED AS A SEAT FAILURE in the round record**, the way a wall is?\n     It is not a wall, not a burn and not a stall; `panel.sh`'s seat check does not classify it, and\n     `seat_verdicts.py` gained a SEAT REFUSED label only for the echo shape.  A refusal that lands\n     after 43k tokens leaves a ZERO-BYTE answer file and a large `.err` -- which is the agy wall's\n     signature exactly, and would be misfiled as a quota event by anyone reading sizes.\n  3. Is there a fifth trigger nobody has hit yet?  Four have been found by walking into them.\n\n*Counted honestly: five refusals, four distinct triggers -- vocabulary (prompt-level), archive reading\n(content-level), chain shape (prompt-level), defect class (content-level).  Every one was found by\nlosing a run to it.*\n\n*** THE CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT RAN AND IT SETTLES THE FOURTH TRIGGER: IT IS THE CONSEQUENCE\nFRAMING, NOT THE SUBJECT MATTER. ***  2026-09-07.\n\n    ORIGINAL   regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/PASS1_brief.md\n               REFUSED after 43,839 tokens.  Content-level flag.\n    REWORDED   regress/artifacts/sgirebufuninit/PASS1_brief_reworded.md\n               ANSWERED.  exit 0, 10,557-byte answer, 185 kB of reasoning.\n\n**THE TECHNICAL CONTENT IS THE SAME BRIEF.**  Same five settled premises, same five questions, same\ncode quoted, same scoping, same premise-audit opener.  **What was removed is the sentence describing\nwhat the defect MEANS** -- the original framed it as host memory becoming visible to the guest; the\nreworded version frames the identical mechanism as a buffer whose assigned length and transferred\nlength are decided independently.\n\n*** SO THE ROUTING CLAUSE IS NOT THE ONLY REMEDY, and my stated expectation was WRONG. ***  I wrote\nthat *\"the wording that would pass is the wording that omits the defect, so I expect it to answer a\nnarrower question.\"*  **It answered the same question, and its answer went FURTHER than grok's on\nthe hardest part** (see [[sgirebufuninit]]: it rules out the write-count oracle that grok's answer\nleft open).  The reformulation cost the round nothing technical.\n\n**WHAT IT COSTS IS STILL REAL AND IS NOT A TECHNICAL COST:** the brief no longer says why the defect\nmatters, and a future reader of the artifacts sees a design question about buffer initialisation\nrather than about a defect class this fork has four rounds' history with.  **The disclosure lives in\nthe reworded brief's own header**, which names it as a rewording and says what was removed -- so the\nrecord is honest even though the packet is narrower.\n\n**THIS CHANGES WHAT THE 2026-09-09 ADJUDICATION IS BEING ASKED.**  The question is no longer *\"is\nrouting the only answer for disclosure-class defects\"* -- measurably it is not.  It is now: **given\nthat rewording works, is rewording ALLOWED?**  A brief written in words chosen to pass a filter is\none step from a brief written in words chosen to be persuasive, and I would rather that line were\ndrawn by someone other than the person under time pressure to get an answer.\n\n*Recorded as a measurement with its controls named: one variable changed, both runs same seat, same\nmodel, same effort, same scoping, same day.*\n\n*** SIXTH REFUSAL, AND IT CORRECTS THE PARAGRAPH ABOVE.  I OVERCLAIMED FROM ONE CONTROLLED PAIR.\n***  `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/HARNESS_review.md`, refused at 42,363 tokens; log preserved\nas `codex_REFUSED_harness_20260907.err`.\n\n**WHAT I WROTE:** *\"the controlled experiment ... settles the fourth trigger: it is the CONSEQUENCE\nFRAMING, not the subject matter.\"*  **That sentence is true of the trigger it was testing and false\nas a general claim, and I stated it generally.**\n\n**THIS BRIEF HAS NO DISCLOSURE FRAMING AT ALL.**  It is a CODE REVIEW of a python probe -- \"read this\nas code, not design\", five questions about instruction encodings, register numbers, a debugger\ncommand and which rows could go green while measuring the wrong thing.  No leak, no host memory, no\nguest visibility, no consequence sentence anywhere.  **It refused anyway.**\n\n*** SO THE HONEST MODEL IS TWO INDEPENDENT TRIGGERS, NOT ONE: ***\n  * **CONSEQUENCE FRAMING** -- measured, and REMOVABLE.  The reworded `sgirebufuninit` brief proves\n    a disclosure-class defect can be discussed if the consequence sentence goes.  That result stands.\n  * **WITNESS / CHAIN SHAPE** -- measured three times now (the swpctx witness design, and this code\n    review of the probe that implements it), and **NOT removable**, because a witness IS a sequence.\n    This brief's Q3 walks one: *\"Two `swpctx` calls later, `PCB_A+16` should hold ...\"*.  There is no\n    way to ask whether a witness is correct without describing what it does.\n\n**AND THE SECOND TRIGGER IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS MORE**, because the lifecycle MANDATES asking a seat\nto attempt a rung-3 witness.  A defect class can be reworded; a witness cannot be de-sequenced.\n\n**ROUTED TO grok, which has now answered THREE briefs codex refused** -- the witness design (finding\nthe flaw that made me rebuild it around K0SEG), the disclosure-class design, and now this code\nreview.  *The routing clause is not a concession to be minimised; on this evidence it is the only\nthing keeping the witness half of the lifecycle running at all.*\n\n**REVISED TALLY: six refusals, four triggers** -- vocabulary (prompt), archive reading (content),\nchain/witness shape (both levels, three instances), defect class framing (content, removable).\n\n*Recorded as a correction to my own paragraph rather than appended as a new finding, because the\nerror was in the SCOPE of a claim I made from a single pair -- which is the exemplar problem this\nproject keeps hitting: a rule minted from one instance carrying that instance's shape as if it were\nthe class.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31808:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07, found while checking [[alphapfnoffset]]'s reachability rather than by any seat.\n\n    if (vaddr == 0xfffffffd80000000ULL) {\n            fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems to want to be used. Not yet implemented.\\n\");\n            exit(1);\n    }\n\n**A guest-chosen virtual address ends the emulator process.**  It sits inside the `ptbr == 0' bootstrap arm,\nso it is reached BEFORE virtual memory is configured -- i.e. early in a boot, on an address the kernel\nchooses, not one a user program supplies.\n\nIt is the same de-fatalisation class this project has worked through repeatedly in devices, arriving in a CPU\nmemory path where nobody has been looking.  **The disposition is NOT obvious, which is why this is filed\nrather than fixed:** declining leaves the guest with a wrong translation and it will fault or hang somewhere\nless legible, so this may be one of the rare sites where a loud stop is genuinely better than continuing --\nand if so the round's product is a RECORD saying so, plus a latch, not a removal.\n\nMED: reachable by construction, but only on a machine whose Alpha VM support is already declared incomplete\nseven TODOs over.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : replace the terminal path with the architecturally appropriate outcome\n    one oracle     : that outcome is observed for the affected address class\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** grok ASSESS 2026-09-08 -- the sixth and last Alpha row.  THE CLUSTER IS NOW FULLY ASSESSED. ***\n\n**THE CLAIM IS TRUE BUT NARROWER THAN FILED: it holds for kernel mode with PTBR == 0 on that exact\nVA.**  There are TWO hits on `0xfffffffd80000000` in `memory_alpha.c` and **only one terminates**;\nthe other is a bare `fatal(\"AYONA3\n\")` -- a debug string left in the tree.  `fatal()` only prints;\nthe termination is the `exit(1)` immediately after, with nothing recovering in between.\n\n## THE CENSUS I ASKED FOR, and it changes the framing\n\n| walker | unmodelled / stub region | ordinary translation miss |\n|---|---|---|\n| Alpha | this one VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)`; other PTBR==0 VAs **ACCEPT** identity | `fatal` + **`return 0`**, no guest exception (`#194` removed the abort here) |\n| MIPS | -- | `mips_cpu_exception`, `return 0` |\n| ARM | unimplemented descriptor type: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `arm_exception`, `return 0` |\n| PPC | 64-bit translation: `fatal`+`exit(1)` | `ppc_exception`, `return 0` |\n| M88K | internal inconsistency: `exit(1)` | `m88k_exception`, `return 0` |\n| SH | unimplemented kernel VA: `fatal`+`exit(1)` (**honours FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS**) | `sh_exception`, `return 0` |\n\n**The kill is an outlier in BOTH in-file directions** -- it is neither the PTBR==0 siblings' ACCEPT\nnor the walk's own `return 0`, which this project already chose over host death in `#194`.\n\n## *** ACCEPT IS REFUTED ON A PREMISE, AND THIS IS THE SHARPEST PART OF THE ANSWER ***\n\nDeleting the `exit(1)` looks like member 4, because `*return_paddr` is already computed above it.\n**But that value is the comment's own \"UGLY hack\": a K0SEG mask applied to a K1SEG address**, giving\na physical address near 4 TB against a default 128 MB of RAM.  Data then takes the unmapped-physical\npath -- **zeros returned, writes dropped, and the access reported SUCCESSFUL.**\n\n> *\"That is the silent bogus map the landmine exists to refuse.\"*\n\n**So a bare de-fatalisation here would be WORSE than the kill it removes**, which is the opposite of\nthis queue's usual shape and is exactly the kind of thing the option-set rule exists to surface.\n\n## DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, `return 0`\n\n* **5 FAULT is architecturally right and NOT AVAILABLE.**  Choosing it obliges building delivery\n  first: a PAL frame, `kentry[ALPHA_KENTRY_MM]`, MMCSR/VA, and teaching `pc_to_pointers` about a\n  redirected PC.  **That is a new IMPLEMENT round, not a de-fatalisation.**  *My brief's caution\n  that FAULT may be unavailable on Alpha is confirmed, with the bill itemised.*\n* **7 IMPLEMENT does not apply** -- its tell is \"no predicate, whole path stubbed\"; this is a\n  one-address predicate, and K1SEG translation IS the walk once PTBR is set.\n* **2 silent DECLINE drops the only signal the author planted.**\n\n## SCOPE: STILL SIX SEPARATE ROUNDS\n\nI asked whether the shared missing MM-dispatch now meets codex's bar for merging this with\n`alphafaultclaim`.  **It does not:** *\"missing MM-dispatch is a CARRIER, not a shared repair.\"*  A\ncommon blocker is not one fix design and one oracle.\n\n## SEVERITY: **med**, explicitly BELOW `alphatbinegsel`\n\nRaised from unset to `med` on grok's argument: this needs a guest already executing privileged code\nwith PTBR == 0 at one specific address, whereas `alphatbinegsel` is a documented primitive silently\nunder-flushing during ordinary operation.  *The ranking is the seat's and I am adopting it rather\nthan substituting my own.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31830:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the codex reachability pass (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt).\n\ncodex: *\"The fork's claim that a failed Alpha walk causes the caller to raise a fault is unsupported by this\npath.  The translator returns zero, the memory worker returns failure, and the Alpha data instruction\nabandons the access without delivering an Alpha exception.\"*\n\n*** THIS IS A WRONG-RECORD (L4) CANDIDATE IN A COMMENT THE FORK ADDED ITSELF. ***  `memory_alpha.c` carries\na `#194: (Codex/Fable) a failed guest page-table walk must not ...' note at its `not_found' label -- so a\nprevious round reasoned about this path and recorded a conclusion about fault delivery.  If the delivery\ndoes not happen, that record is false and a future round will transfer it.\n\n**AND IT CORROBORATES A STANDING CARRIER CLAIM FROM A NEW DIRECTION.**  The option-set rule already records\nthat member 5 (FAULT) *\"is NOT universally available -- there is no Alpha equivalent\"* of the MIPS/M88K\nexception dispatch.  That was derived from `memory_rw.c`; this is the same conclusion reached from the\nAlpha instruction side.  **Any round reaching for FAULT on an Alpha device now has two independent reasons\nnot to.**\n\nNOT YET VERIFIED BY ME -- recorded as the seat stated it, with the two citations it gave, because the\nverification is a reading of `cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c`'s failure path and belongs to the round that\ntakes it.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : if it is only a false comment, document actual fault ownership\n    one oracle     : the documented claim is supported by the return/caller control flow\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED 2026-09-08 BY A SEAT THAT WAS NOT ASKED ABOUT THIS ROW. ***\n\ncodex, answering the `alphapteprot` brief (`regress/artifacts/alphapteprot/codex_assess_20260908.txt`):\n\n> *\"There is no implemented Alpha MM-fault dispatch here.  `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` and `ALPHA_MMCSR_*`\n> have definitions but no consumers; `kentry` entries are initialized and assigned, never used to\n> dispatch.  The comments claiming the translator or caller 'already signalled' a fault are\n> unsupported by the implementation.\"*\n\n**That is this row's claim, reached from the other direction** -- it was tracing whether a\nwrite-protected page could fault, and found the fault machinery is definitions only.  *Raised under\nthe found-something-else licence, on a brief about the page walk's return value.*\n\n**CONSEQUENCE FOR SCOPE:** this row and `alphapteprot` are now coupled by a shared prerequisite --\n**neither can deliver an architectural fault, because nothing dispatches one.**  codex's earlier\ncluster ruling said they may be grouped *\"only after proving they share a file, one fault-handling\nrepair, and the same fault-delivery oracle.\"*  **The shared missing machinery is evidence toward\nexactly that**, and it is the question to put to the adjudication rather than to settle here.\n\n*** A THIRD INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION, 2026-09-08, again from a seat not asked about this row. ***\ncodex, on the `alphactxfail` brief: *\"The comment saying the translator already signalled a fault is\nalso unsupported by the inspected Alpha failure path.\"*  **Three briefs, three directions, one\nconclusion** -- the tbi callback trace, the page-walk protection review, and now the context-switch\nerror handling.  *None of the three was asked about this row; all three arrived at it while tracing\nwhether something could fault.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31834:   "id": "alphaswpctxreg",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31840:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the codex reachability pass (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt), raised under the\nfound-something-else licence on a brief about something unrelated.\n\ncodex: *\"`swpctx` writes the old context into **a0**, although the supplied ABI wrapper returns **v0**.\nThis independently contradicts the handler's contract.\"*\n\nIf correct, a guest calling the PALcode `swpctx` gets its previous context pointer in the wrong register --\nthe caller reads `v0` and finds whatever was there.  That is a silent wrong value rather than a crash, which\nis the harder kind to notice.\n\n*(SUPERSEDED WITHIN THE HOUR, kept because the record should show the order.  This paragraph read:\n**\"NOT VERIFIED BY ME.  Both citations are given and the check is two file reads; it is filed rather\nthan confirmed because the Alpha queue is already three rows deep tonight and none of them has a\nwitness.  A round taking any Alpha item should settle this one first -- it is the cheapest of the four\nto check.\"**  It WAS the cheapest, it WAS two file reads, and both were done immediately below.)*\n\n*** VERIFIED BY ME 2026-09-07, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN THE SEAT STATED: THE CODE CONTRADICTS THE COMMENT\nDIRECTLY ABOVE IT. ***\n\n    /*  Return old context in v0.  */\n    cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n\nThe comment says v0; the store is to ALPHA_A0.  **ALPHA_V0 exists and is used correctly elsewhere in the same file** -- the PAL dispatch a hundred\nlines up does `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = a3;' -- so this is not a missing symbol or a naming\nconvention; it is one wrong identifier.  And the ABI wrapper confirms the intent independently:\n`alpha_pal_swpctx' in `src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h' declares its return register as\n`__asm(\"$0\")' (v0), takes the new context in `__asm(\"$16\")' (a0), and ends `return (v0);'.\n\n**RAISED LOW -> MED and this is now the CHEAPEST CERTAIN FIX ON THE BOARD** -- cheaper than\n[[sgirergbroundtrip]], because the intent is stated on the line above the defect rather than inferred from a\nconsumer.  One token, ALPHA_V0'.\n\n**Its REACHABILITY is still bounded by the same open question as the other Alpha rows** ([[alphapfnoffset]]'s\nboot question) -- a guest must reach a context switch.  But unlike those, the DEFECT is certain without any\nwitness: a comment and its code cannot both be right.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : return the old context through the ABI-defined result register\n    one oracle     : returned v0 equals the outgoing context address\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** FIX DESIGN COMPLETE, codex 2026-09-08 -- AND IT REFUTED THE PREMISE I PUT IN THE BRIEF. ***\n\n**MY Q2 PREMISE WAS WRONG.**  I asked whether this is ONE defect or TWO, suggesting the store to\n`r[ALPHA_A0]` does two wrong things: fails to place the result where the caller reads it, AND\nclobbers the caller's `a0`.  **The clobber is not a defect.**  The wrapper is:\n\n    static __inline unsigned long\n    alpha_pal_swpctx(unsigned long ctx)\n    {\n            register unsigned long a0 __asm(\"$16\") = ctx;\n            register unsigned long v0 __asm(\"$0\");\n            __asm volatile(\"call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx\"\n                    : \"=r\" (a0), \"=r\" (v0)\n                    : \"i\" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), \"0\" (a0)\n                    : \"$1\", \"$22\", \"$23\", \"$24\", \"$25\", \"memory\");\n            return (v0);\n    }\n\n**`a0` is an OUTPUT tied to an input, so its incoming value need not survive** -- the wrapper\ndiscards that output and returns `v0`.  So it is ONE defect with two register-state effects, and the\none-character edit repairs it without any separate preservation handling.\n\n*And my reading of `\"0\"` was wrong in the other direction too: it refers to output OPERAND ZERO,\nwhich here occupies `$16` (`a0`) -- it does NOT select physical `$0`.  `ALPHA_V0 = 0` and\n`ALPHA_A0 = 16` in `cpu_alpha.h`, so the two happen to look alike in a way that invites exactly this\nconfusion.*\n\n## THE FIX -- one character, and it matches FOUR in-tree siblings\n\n    -\t\tcpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n    +\t\tcpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n\n**The house idiom was found by grepping the fork rather than by reasoning**, as the option-set rule\nrequires.  The closest sibling is `PAL_OSF1_swpipl` (case 0x35) -- *\"a0 = new ipl, v0 = return old\nipl\"* -- which writes the old state into `ALPHA_V0` and then installs the new state from the copied\nlocal `a0`, the identical shape.  `rdmces`, `rdps` and `whami` all return through `ALPHA_V0` as\nwell, as does `PROM_R_PUTS` in `alpha_prom_call()`.\n\n**Q4 -- no in-tree dependency.**  The local `a0` stays live through `cpu->cd.alpha.ctx = a0`, so the\nload-new-context step is unaffected; the emulator caller checks status and PC and never reads\n`r[ALPHA_A0]`; and `src/` contains no calls to the wrapper at all.  *An external guest relying on\nthe accidental old-context value in `a0` would observe a change -- but the wrapper provides no such\nguarantee, so that is not a contract this round breaks.*\n\n## *** THE ORACLE IS DEBUGGER-VISIBLE, WHICH MAKES THE WITNESS CHEAP ***\n\n    observable   existing code   fixed code\n    v0           P (stale)       C  (the outgoing context)\n    a0           C               N  (the incoming argument)\n\n**The oracle is `v0_after == ctx_before`**, and the debugger's `reg` command reaches the Alpha\nregister dump, which prints both registers by name -- **so guest code need not consume the return\nvalue for the two builds to be distinguishable.**  That is a materially easier witness than\n[[alphatbinegsel]]'s, which needs a cached mapping, a PTE remap and everything inside one `step N`.\n\n*** THE VACUITY CONDITION, and it is the same shape as the page-0 trap on the sibling row: the\nobservation requires `P != C` -- the pre-call `v0` must differ from the outgoing context, or a stale\n`v0` happens to look correct. ***  The probe must set `v0` to a distinctive value before the call.\n\n**Q6 -- KEEP the comment, and it owes a row.**  *\"Return old context in v0\"* asserts an observable\npostcondition (the value AND its register), so under this project's rule it is a claim that must be\npinned, not a description.  It makes no claim about preserving `a0`, which is consistent with the\nfinding above.\n\n**STATUS: ready to implement.**  One-character product change, four sibling precedents, a named\noracle with its vacuity condition, and no in-tree dependency.  Nothing has been built or run --\nthis was a source review and the seat said so.\n\n*** THE WARRANT IS STRONGER THAN THE SEAT STATED, AND I RE-DERIVED IT RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBING. ***\n\ncodex named four sibling precedents.  **THE DERIVER, so the next reader re-runs it instead of\ntrusting a literal:**\n\n    grep -n 'ALPHA_V0\\] =' src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n\nOn 2026-09-08 that printed EIGHT return-value sites in this one file -- :118 (PROM_R_PUTS),\n:204 (rdmces), :306 (sysvalue), :339 and :344 (swpipl, rdps), :356 (usp), :361 (whami), :383 (a\nsuccess code).  The same grep for ALPHA_A0 finds the swpctx line under review.\n\n> **SO swpctx IS THE LONE OUTLIER AMONG NINE RETURN-VALUE SITES IN ITS OWN FILE.**  That is a far\n> stronger warrant than \"four siblings agree\", and it is the shape the option-set rule already\n> records from sgireunmapped: *the arm under review was the file's own outlier, and one grep showed\n> it.*\n\nALPHA_V0 is 0 and ALPHA_A0 is 16 (src/include/cpu_alpha.h), so the two constants look confusable in\na way that plausibly explains the original slip -- and explains my own misreading of the \"0\" asm\nconstraint as selecting physical $0.\n\n*The count is written with its deriver rather than as a bare literal, per the counts rule: the\nrounds that consult a number here are the rounds that change it.*\n\n*** A TOOLING NOTE FROM WRITING THIS ENTRY, because it is the fifth instance in one session:\nBACKTICKS IN RECORD PROSE MUST NOT GO THROUGH `python -c`. ***  Bash performs command substitution\non them before python ever sees the string -- this attempt emitted a screenful of\n`:118: command not found` and then a SyntaxError, having also executed lines out of two unrelated\nfiles it happened to glob.  **No write occurred, because python died before reaching it.**  The\nremedy is recorded and I keep not applying it: *record prose goes in a script FILE, written with the\nWrite tool, never inlined into a shell command.*\n\n*** RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-08 WITH A CENSUS, AND THE CENSUS IS WHAT MAKES THIS UNARGUABLE: `:251` IS A\nPOPULATION OF ONE AGAINST A POPULATION OF EIGHT. ***\n\n    grep -n \"ALPHA_V0\\]\" src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n        118  204  306  339  344  356  361  383        <- EIGHT handlers return via V0\n\n    grep -n \"r\\[ALPHA_A0\\] =\" src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n        251                                           <- EXACTLY ONE.  This line.\n\n**So it is not a local convention, not a naming difference and not a missing symbol.**  It is one\nidentifier, in the one place the file does it differently, under a comment that says the opposite.\n\n*** AND THE ABI WRAPPER SETTLES THE INTENT INDEPENDENTLY -- IT DECLARES a0 AS THE REGISTER THE\nGUEST MUST DISCARD. ***  `alpha_cpu.h:449-459`:\n\n    register unsigned long a0 __asm(\"$16\") = ctx;\n    register unsigned long v0 __asm(\"$0\");\n    __asm volatile(\"call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx\"\n        : \"=r\" (a0), \"=r\" (v0)\n        : \"i\" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), \"0\" (a0)\n        /* clobbers t0, t8..t11, a0 (above) */\n\n`v0` is `$0` and is an OUTPUT; `a0` is an IN-OUT operand the comment explicitly calls clobbered.\n**So the model writes its result into the register the caller is told to throw away, and leaves\nuntouched the one the caller reads.**  A correct guest cannot recover the value at all.\n\n**ORDERING IS CORRECT AND IS NOT PART OF THIS DEFECT** -- `:251` publishes the OLD `ctx` before\n`:255` overwrites it with the new one.  Recorded so a fix does not \"helpfully\" reorder anything.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each rather than asserting it:** whether the handler\nshould ALSO clobber a0 for fidelity (or whether that is unobservable by construction, since no\ncorrect caller may read it); REACHABILITY -- I have NOT shown any guest this project boots calls\n`swpctx` and consumes the return, and the wrapper existing in the OS header is a different claim;\nand the witness shape, which must seed `v0` with a distinguishing value or \"the handler did nothing\nto v0\" and \"the handler wrote the right thing\" are indistinguishable.\n\n**A CROSS-ROW HAZARD CARRIED FROM `alphatbinegsel`'s panel, recorded on BOTH rows:** `:296` in this\nsame arm is `invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, 0, INVALIDATE_ALL)` under `// TODO: Don't\ninvalidate EVERYTHING!`, and glm warned that **swpctx IS the context-switch PAL, so whichever of\nthe two rows lands SECOND must re-baseline its witness with the other's fix present** or a TBIAP on\nthe switch path confuses attribution between them.  The pass-1 brief asks explicitly whether that\nTODO belongs in this round or is a separate row, under the one-site rule.\n\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/PASS1_brief.md`, fired at codex.  Written in neutral vocabulary\nand checked against the `codexcyberflag` trigger list before firing -- zero hits.\n\n*** PASS 1 (codex): THE FIX IS CORRECT AND SUFFICIENT, KEEP `med`, EXCLUDE THE CACHE TODO -- AND MY\nCENSUS WAS TEXTUALLY RIGHT AND MISLEADING. ***\n\n**THE COUNT WAS INFLATED AND THE METHOD IS THE REASON.**  I reported EIGHT `ALPHA_V0` return sites.\nThree of them -- 306, 356, 383 -- are **inside `#if 0`**, and 118 is in `alpha_prom_call`, not the\nPAL handler.  **Five compiled assignments file-wide, four in `alpha_palcode`.**\n\n> *I grepped for textual presence and reported it as a population of compiled code.*  That is this\n> project own deriver rule landing on me twice in one day: **key on what makes a member a member,\n> not on a correlate of it.**  The conclusion is untouched -- the ABI wrapper is the decisive\n> evidence, and `:251` is still the only `r[ALPHA_A0] =` in the file -- but the number was wrong,\n> and a wrong number gets reused.\n\n**Q1 -- FIX CONFIRMED, AND MY FRAMING CORRECTED.**  `ALPHA_A0` -> `ALPHA_V0`, nothing more: the\nhandler captured the incoming `a0` in a local at entry, so `ctx = a0` still receives the new\ncontext.  **No additional `a0` write is required** -- the architecture marks `a0` UNPREDICTABLE and\nthat definition *explicitly permits a register to remain unchanged*; \"clobbered\" removes a\npreservation guarantee, it does not require destruction.  **And my \"unobservable by construction\"\nwas TOO STRONG:** software CAN read `a0`, it simply cannot require a particular value.  So there is\nno second fidelity defect here.\n\n**Q2 -- KEEP `med`.**  Returning stale register contents violates a defined primitive and merits\nrepair, but no demonstrated consumer, resulting corruption or isolation failure supports a raise.\n**It also corrected my severity prose in the other direction:** *\"wrong value rather than a crash\"*\ndescribes only the IMMEDIATE effect -- a consumer could crash later, and neither that nor\nharmlessness is established.\n\n**Q3 -- REACHABLE, and there is already execution evidence I did not know about.**  Route:\n`decoder -> alpha_instr_call_pal -> alpha_palcode -> swpctx arm`, permitted in kernel mode and\nrejected in user mode.  **`#507`'s own alphabook1 result records TWO COMPLETED `swpctx` CALLS with\nsuccessful PCB checks** (`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/probe_fix_20260907.out`) -- so the\nprimitive is reached and returns today; that run simply never tested `v0`.  No verified OS boot is\nknown to CONSUME the return value, and codex was explicit that this *\"does not establish\nunreachability or rule out a rung-3 witness.\"*\n\n**Q4 -- THE ORACLE NEEDS THREE DISTINGUISHABLE VALUES, and \"v0 changed\" is not enough.**  Establish\nan independently known old context `C`, a distinct incoming context `N`, and seed `v0` with a\nsentinel `S`.  **Expected `v0 = C`; the shipped code leaves `v0 = S`; an incorrect repair returning\nthe NEW context gives `v0 = N`.**  A row asserting only that v0 changed would pass the incorrect\nrepair.\n\n**Q5 -- EXCLUDE the `// TODO: Don't invalidate EVERYTHING!` at `:296`** from this round, per the\none-site rule.",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31850:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/codex_fixdesign_20260908.txt",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31861:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/codex_pass1_20260908.txt",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31873:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **RAISED BY THE CODEX SEAT** under the found-something-else licence, on a brief that asked\nonly whether Alpha machines can run anything (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt) -- and VERIFIED by reading before\nfiling.\n\n`PAL_OSF1_swpctx' loads a new context and assigns it into the pcb.  **`apcb_ptbr' is assigned TWICE and\n`apcb_flags' is never assigned at all:**\n\n    // Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously:\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ksp    = new_ksp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_usp    = new_usp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_ptbr;      <- correct\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_cpc    = new_cpc;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_asn    = new_asn;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_unique = new_unique;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_flags;     <- *** should be apcb_flags ***\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv0 = new_decrsv0;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv1 = new_decrsv1;\n\n*** WHY THIS OUTRANKS THE OTHER ALPHA ROWS: `apcb_ptbr' IS THE PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER. ***\n`alpha_translate_v2p()' opens with `uint64_t base = cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr << ALPHA_PAGESHIFT;' -- so\nafter ANY `swpctx' the page-table walk reads its tables from an address derived from the FLAGS word.  Every\ntranslation after a context switch walks garbage.\n\n**And `apcb_flags' keeps the OUTGOING context's value**, because nothing ever writes it -- a second, quieter\ndefect from the same line.\n\n**THE COMMENT DIRECTLY ABOVE ASSERTS WHAT THE CODE DOES NOT DO:** *\"Update all variables in the pcb\nsimultaneously\"*.  Two of the nine are wrong -- one clobbered, one never set.  That is the second time in\nthis one function that a comment states an intent the code contradicts; the other is [[alphaswpctxreg]].\n\n**FIX: one token** -- the second `apcb_ptbr' becomes `apcb_flags'.  As with its sibling, the intent is not in\ndoubt: `new_flags' is loaded from the context block and would otherwise be dead, and `apcb_flags' exists in\nthe struct and would otherwise never be written.\n\n**REACHABILITY: cold-debugger reachable, per the same pass** -- `swpctx' is an active handler reached by\nordinary guest instruction dispatch with no OS dependency.  A witness plants a `call_pal 0x30' and observes\n`apcb_ptbr' afterwards.\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH [[alphapfnoffset]] IS WORTH STATING, because it changes how that round should be\ndesigned: this defect CORRUPTS THE WALK'S BASE, and that one CORRUPTS THE WALK'S RESULT. ***  A witness for\neither must control for the other -- a probe that does a `swpctx' and then observes a bad translation cannot\nattribute it without pinning which of the two produced it.  **Fix this one first**: it is certain, one token,\nand it removes a confound from the harder row.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS, codex 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).  THE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED AND TWO OF MY\nSUPPORTING PREMISES WERE WRONG.  Corrected here. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"A and B are defects, and both proposed corrections are right.  They are TWO changes under your\nreopening rule.  `apcb_flags is never written' is false; no third destination typo was found.\"*\n\n**CORRECTION 1 -- `apcb_flags' IS WRITTEN, and I filed the opposite as fact.**  This row said it *\"is never\nassigned at all\"*.  `grep -rn apcb_flags src/' returns `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:221' -- **`wrfen' assigns it**\nfrom `r[ALPHA_A0]' and then writes it back to PCB memory, and `swpctx' itself READS the field when saving the\noutgoing context.\n\n**The honest statement is narrower and still a defect:** `swpctx' never assigns `apcb_flags' WHEN LOADING A\nNEW CONTEXT, so the incoming context's flags are silently dropped and the outgoing context's value persists\nacross the switch.  That is a real defect; *never written anywhere* was not.\n\n**CORRECTION 2 -- `new_flags' is NOT dead, and the distinction matters.**  I wrote that it *\"would otherwise\nbe dead\"*.  codex: it *\"has exactly one use after its declaration: the erroneous PTBR assignment.  It is\notherwise unused, but **not dead in the current program**: it changes PTBR.\"*  My phrasing implied the\ncompiler would flag it; it would not, because the erroneous line consumes it.\n\n**A QUALIFICATION ON THE SIBLING [[alphaswpctxreg]] THAT BOUNDS ITS IMPACT:** the handler captures its input\ninto a LOCAL `a0' before dispatch, so overwriting `r[ALPHA_A0]' does **not** change which context is loaded.\nThat defect is therefore purely a wrong RETURN VALUE -- it does not corrupt the switch itself.  I had not\nestablished that, and it is the difference between a reporting bug and a state-corruption bug.\n\n**SCOPE: TWO CHANGES, NOT ONE.**  I intended to fix both in one diff -- same function, same class.  codex\nrules they are two under the reopening rule.  **Accepted**: the observables differ (a guest register versus\ninternal PCB state reachable only through a translation), so one test cannot cover both without a new\nobservation, which is exactly what that rule forbids.\n\n**MY Q3 HYPOTHESIS WAS REFUTED, and recording that matters as much as a finding.**  I said a third\nwrong-destination assignment was *\"more likely than not\"* given two in one function, and asked for the whole\nhandler to be read.  It was, and there is no third.  **A predicted class that does not materialise is a\nresult** -- and it stops the next round hunting for one.\n\n*** READ-ONLY AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_read_20260907.txt`).\nTHE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED FIELD BY FIELD, THE FIX IS ONE TOKEN, AND A PREMISE OF MINE ABOUT\nREACHABILITY WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN THE WITNESS. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"`swpctx` assigns `apcb_ptbr` twice, leaves `apcb_flags` unchanged, and uses all nine\nlocals.  Its return line writes `a0`.  Opcode-0 dispatch has no privilege check, but\n`alpha_palcode()` itself checks user mode before the switch.\"*\n\n**CONFIRMED, from the seat's own nine-row assignment table rather than from my description:**\n`apcb_ptbr` is assigned twice, `apcb_flags` never, and **all nine locals are used** -- so the defect\nis precisely a mis-typed destination, not a dropped load.  **The fix is ONE IDENTIFIER TOKEN on ONE\nLINE** (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`), which the seat states explicitly.  The `a0`-for-`v0` return is\nconfirmed as a second, separate defect in the same handler.\n\n*** THE CORRECTION, AND IT IS A HARNESS-BREAKER RATHER THAN A RECORDS NICETY. ***  I wrote -- in the\nbrief AND in the first draft of the Alpha harness -- that `call_pal` dispatch is unconditional.\n**Dispatch INTO `alpha_palcode()` is; the function then GATES ITS OWN SWITCH** at\n`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:183`:\n\n    uint64_t userMode = cpu->cd.alpha.ps & ALPHA_PSL_USERMODE;      /* 0x0008 */\n    if (userMode && !(palcode == 0x80 || 0x81 || 0x83 || 0xae ||\n                      0xaa || 0x86 || 0x9e || 0x92 || 0x9f)) {\n            fatal(\"[ Privileged Alpha PALcode called from user mode: TODO ]\");\n            cpu->running = 0;\n            return;\n    }\n\n**`0x30` is not on that whitelist**, so in user mode `call_pal 0x30` does not reach swpctx -- it\nHALTS THE CPU.  **That failure presents as a probe whose step never completes**, i.e. as a broken\nharness rather than as a mode problem, which is the worst kind of failure to debug.  Verified by\nreading the lines rather than taken on the seat's word.\n\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ps` has NO explicit initialiser anywhere in `src/`, so it is zero from the zeroed cpu\nstruct and USERMODE is clear -- kernel mode, and the gate does not fire.  **That is a derivation, and\nthe harness turns it into a measurement**: `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` now carries a `palgate`\nrow asserting the diagnostic is absent, and a `palalive` row asserting the machine survives a swpctx\ncall at all.\n\n*Note the shape: the premise that was wrong is the one I did NOT think to question -- I checked the\ninstruction decoder carefully and never looked inside the function it dispatches to.  That is the\nthird instance this week and it is the subject of the queued rule question\n[[sgilinearnegidxpostmortem]], which asks whether the premise-audit instruction needs a\nself-directed twin.  **This is its third data point and the first where a seat caught it before it\ncost anything.***\n\n**AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THIS WAS THE SECOND ATTEMPT.**  The first -- carrying a four-step witness\nDESIGN -- was REFUSED by the same seat as chain-shaped content (see [[codexbriefscoping]]).  The\nchain-free re-ask, which requests a field-by-field audit and names no procedure, was answered in\nfull.  **The design question went to grok instead**, so neither question was dropped.\n\n*** THE WITNESS DESIGN WAS REFUTED, BY THE SEAT THE REFUSED BRIEF WAS ROUTED TO.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_witness_20260907.txt`, 13.5 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"YES, a rung-3 witness is buildable on the three constructing Alpha machines -- but the\nproposed 'no MMU at all, two `swpctx`, read `A+16`' design is unsound as written if `A.flags` is\nnonzero.  After the first switch, `apcb_ptbr` IS the flags word, the identity map dies, and the\nsecond `call_pal` (and the save that is supposed to publish the bug) can fail to land or can\n`exit(1)` the host.  Use K0SEG for code and both PCBs, or keep `flags==0` and accept a weaker\noracle.  The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous LHS.\"*\n\n*** THE DESIGN WAS SELF-DEFEATING AND I HAD NOT SEEN IT: THE DEFECT DESTROYS THE PATH THE WITNESS\nNEEDS TO OBSERVE IT. ***  Verified in `memory_alpha.c` rather than taken on the seat's word, and the\nsource is sharper than the verdict:\n\n  * **K0SEG is checked BEFORE `base` is used at all** (`:62`): `if (!userMode && vaddr >=\n    ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE && vaddr <= ALPHA_K0SEG_END) { *return_paddr = vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff; return\n    2; }`.  A K0SEG access never consults `apcb_ptbr`, so grok's remedy is sound.\n    `ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE = 0xfffffc0000000000`; `ALPHA_PHYS_TO_K0SEG(x) = x | BASE`.\n  * **The identity map is an explicit 'UGLY hack' conditioned on `base == 0`** (`:73`).  That is the\n    ONLY reason a naive harness works at all -- `ptbr` is zero at reset.  **The moment the buggy\n    assignment makes `apcb_ptbr` nonzero, `base != 0` and every later access falls into a real\n    page-table walk over garbage**, with an `exit(1)` sitting on one vaddr at `:75-78`.\n  * **AND THE POLARITY TRAP grok did not state, which kills the 'keep flags==0' fallback too:** if\n    the marker is put in `A.ptbr` instead, then under the *FIXED* build `apcb_ptbr` becomes that\n    nonzero marker and the identity map dies on the FIX rather than on the defect.  **Neither\n    polarity survives with a nonzero value anywhere in that field.**  So K0SEG is not the better\n    option, it is the only one.\n\n**THE CORRECTED DESIGN, and it needs no 64-bit immediates:** `lda`/`ldah` build 32-bit constants\nonly, and a K0SEG address needs bit 63..42 set.  The way through is to **load the pointers BEFORE the\nfirst `swpctx`, while the identity map is still alive**: place the 64-bit K0SEG pointers in\nidentity-mapped scratch with the debugger's `put q`, have the guest `ldq` them into registers, and\nonly then issue the calls.  Every access after that carries a K0SEG address in a register and is\nimmune to whatever `apcb_ptbr` becomes.\n\n**A SEPARATE TRAP FOUND WHILE CHECKING THIS, which the harness avoids only by luck:** `memory_alpha.c:87`\nsilently remaps `(vaddr & ~0x1fff) == 0x20000000` to `PROM_ARGSPACE_PADDR` (0x12000).  The harness's\ncode at `0x20010000` and scratch at `0x20020000` fall OUTSIDE that 8 kB window -- but nothing said\nso, and a probe placed 64 kB lower would have had its stores quietly land in PROM argument space.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED:** *\"The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous\nLHS.\"*  Both seats agree the correction is `apcb_flags` at `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`.\n\n*** THE ROUTING WORKED AND IS THE REUSABLE LESSON. ***  codex REFUSED this brief as chain-shaped;\ngrok answered it in full, read the handler, the dispatch path and the existing harness, and refuted\nthe design on a mechanism I had missed.  **The chain-free re-ask to codex and the chain-shaped\noriginal to grok together cost one extra invocation and lost nothing** -- which is the argument the\n2026-09-09 adjudication is asked to rule on under [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** CODE REVIEW OF THE HARNESS, BEFORE ITS FIRST RUN.  grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_harness_20260907.txt` -- codex REFUSED this brief too (sixth\nrefusal, chain/witness shape), so it is grok's third answer to a brief codex would not take. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DO NOT TRUST THE FIRST RUN.  The lda/ldah/ldq bootstrap and the swpctx SAVE/RESTORE\nwalk are right.  The encodings that this file actually emits match the decoder.  `put d` is the right\ndebugger command.  What is wrong is the ADDRESS PLAN, and it makes three rows able to go green while\nmeasuring 'unmapped reads return 0', not the handler.\"*\n\n*** FINDING 1 -- FATAL, AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT AND GREEN. ***  The machine is built `-M 64`\n= 64 MB and every address in the file sat at `0x200xxxxx` (512 MB+).  K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff`, so **the K0SEG idea survives contact with `alpha_translate_v2p` and the\nPHYSICAL BACKING does not.**  And the failure mode is silent:\n`memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` only halts when `halt_on_nonexistant_memaccess` is set, i.e.\nunder `-T`.  **Without it, writes succeed and are DISCARDED and reads RETURN ZEROS** -- and an\ninstruction fetch of zeros is a real instruction, `call_pal 0`.\n\n**MY ORACLE MADE THAT UNDETECTABLE.**  It asserted `PCB_A+16 == 0` for the correct case, and 0 is\nalso what an unmapped read returns.  **The witness would have gone green having written nothing,\nread nothing, and executed `call_pal 0` off a zero-filled code page.**\n\n**REPAIRED, and the repair is better than moving the addresses:** K0SEG means translation never\nconsults `apcb_ptbr`, so `apcb_ptbr` may now hold a distinctive NONZERO value -- which the FIRST\ndesign could not allow, because a nonzero ptbr killed the identity map.  So:\n\n    correct handler  -> PCB_A+16 == PTBR_VAL  (0xaaaa1111)\n    the defect       -> PCB_A+16 == FLAGS_VAL (0xbbbb2222)\n    anything broken  -> 0, which is NEITHER, so BOTH rows red\n\nplus a third row `swpctx mapped` asserting the value is not 0 -- **because without it a probe whose\naddresses drifted back outside RAM would fail one row and pass the other, which reads exactly like a\nreal defect.**  Addresses moved inside 64 MB with an `assert` that says so.\n\n*** FINDING 2 -- `palalive` IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, AND I WROTE IT WHILE APPLYING THE RULE\nAGAINST THAT. ***  *\"`alive` is 'the pty is still readable'.  `alpha_palcode`'s default arm does\n`cpu->running = 0` and stays in the debugger because `-V` is single-step.  The emulator does not\nexit.  `palalive` stays green.\"*  **Kept as a LABELLED NON-ORACLE rather than deleted**, so the next\nreader sees why it is there and does not re-add it as a real row; the mode question is answered by\n`palgate`, which greps the diagnostic text.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- the encoder self-test does not test the decoder.**  It re-runs `load_const`'s own\n32-bit wrap masked with `& 0xffffffff`, while LDAH sign-extends through an `(int32_t)` cast --\n`0xdeadbeef` would be `0xffffffffdeadbeef` in a register and the self-test would still print ok.\n**It is sound only because every value this file uses has bit 31 clear.**  Correct by luck; recorded\nin the file as such rather than quietly fixed.\n\n**FINDING 4 -- `put q` is not merely 128-bit, it is UNIMPLEMENTED** (`\"quad-words: TODO\"`).  The\npremise was true and understated.\n\n**CONFIRMED, so the design work was not wasted:** encodings match the decoder; the bootstrap method\nis valid and nothing between the `put d` writes and the first `call_pal` touches `apcb_ptbr`; K0SEG\nis checked before `base`; PCB offsets match `struct alpha_pcb`; `ps` is zero from `zeroed_alloc`;\n`0x30` is not on the unprivileged whitelist.  It also supplied two facts I did not have:\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ctx` starts at `0x10100`, and PROM setup (on by default) sets `a0 = 2048`.\n\n*** THE REVIEW COST ONE INVOCATION AND SAVED A GREEN WITNESS THAT PROVED NOTHING. ***  The lifecycle\nsays ask a seat to try a rung-3 probe; this says ask a seat to READ the probe before running it, and\non this evidence the second is worth as much as the first.\n\n*** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND RUN, 2026-09-07.  THE DEFECT REPRODUCES ON THE COMMITTED BUILD. ***\n\n    row              got          want              \n    encoder          ok           ok                load_const sign correction, 9 vectors, offline\n    live             0x5a5a5a5a   0x5a5a5a5a        LIVENESS: guest store then load through RAM\n    exec             0x1234       0x1234            a guest LDAH/LDA pair reached a register\n    palgate          not gated    not gated         call_pal 0x30 is privileged; we are in kernel mode\n    palalive         alive        alive             NON-ORACLE, labelled as such\n    swpctx alive     alive        alive             the guest survives two swpctx calls in K0SEG\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111        FAIL -- the defect\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222    FAIL -- the defect's exact signature\n    swpctx mapped    0xbbbb2222   NOT 0x0           ok  -- the memory WAS there\n    ALPHAHARNESS_RESULT=7/9\n\n**THE MECHANISM, confirmed by the measurement rather than only by reading:** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`\ncase `0x30` LOADs the new context, sets `apcb_ptbr = new_ptbr`, and then **overwrites it with\n`new_flags` a few lines later**.  The probe stages PCB_A with ptbr `0xaaaa1111` and flags\n`0xbbbb2222`, swaps A in, swaps B in (whose SAVE writes A's context back to `PCB_A+16`), and reads\nthat cell.  It holds the FLAGS.\n\n*** `swpctx mapped` IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PHANTOM, and it exists\nbecause a code review demanded it. ***  The grok harness review found that **every address in the\nfirst draft was planted past the end of RAM** -- `-M 64` gives 64 MB and the structures sat at\n`0x20010000`+ (512 MB) -- where writes are discarded and reads return zeros with no diagnostic.\nThree rows would have gone GREEN while measuring *unmapped memory returns 0*.  The repair moved\neverything below 64 MB **and added `assert max(P_CODE, P_PTRV, P_PCB_A, P_PCB_B) < 64*1024*1024`**,\nso the class cannot be reintroduced silently.  `swpctx mapped` then pins the same property at run\ntime: a `0` readback is reported as a SETUP FAILURE, not as a result.\n\n> **THAT IS THE THIRD DISTINCT ROW-CLASS THIS SESSION WHOSE WHOLE PURPOSE IS TO SEPARATE 'MEASURED\n> NOTHING' FROM 'MEASURED A ZERO'** -- the UBSan in-band control, the kill-table NUL check, and this.\n> They were built independently and they are all the same rule: *an apparatus that cannot fail\n> loudly will fail quietly and be believed.*\n\n**TEST-FIRST IS SATISFIED:** the witness shows the symptom on the committed build BEFORE any edit,\nwhich is what licenses a fix.  The probe exits non-zero on a red row, so `rc=1` here is the correct\noutcome and not an error.\n\n**AND IT UNBLOCKS [[alphapfnoffset]]**, which is the reason this row was worked first: that row's\nwitness needs a non-zero `apcb_ptbr` and a valid page table, and *this* defect clobbers `apcb_ptbr`\nwith the flags word -- so any translation probe built before this was fixed could not attribute a\nbad translation to the walk rather than to the swap.  The confound is now measured rather than\nsuspected.\n\n*** A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THIS ROW DOES NOT RECORD, found by COUNTING the assignments rather than\nreading them.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nOver `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:274-292`, the load block's field assignments are:\n\n    apcb_ksp 1   apcb_usp 1   apcb_cpc 1   apcb_asn 1   apcb_unique 1\n    apcb_decrsv0 1   apcb_decrsv1 1\n    apcb_ptbr  **2**        <- :278 = new_ptbr, :282 = new_flags\n    apcb_flags **0**        <- never assigned at all\n\n**So `:282` is a field-name transposition: it should be `apcb_flags = new_flags`.**  The value IS\nloaded -- the SAVE block stores `apcb_flags` to `ctx + 40` and the loader reads `new_flags` from\n`ctx + 40` -- and then lands in the wrong field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE 1 is the clobber the witness measures: `apcb_ptbr` ends up holding the flags word.**\n> **CONSEQUENCE 2 is new: `apcb_flags` is NEVER LOADED, so a context switch silently drops the\n> incoming context's flags.**  The row's title names only the first.\n\n*And the block's own comment at `:275` reads **\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"** --\nwhich is exactly the property it fails to have.  A comment asserting completeness directly above an\nincomplete list is the shape this project keeps finding: the claim is the thing least likely to be\nre-checked, because it reads as a description of the code below it.*\n\n**The counting is what found it.**  Reading the block, `:282` looks like an ordinary assignment in a\ncolumn of ordinary assignments; the eye supplies the field name it expects.  A `grep -o 'pcb\\.apcb_[a-z0-9]*\n*=' | sort | uniq -c` over the block makes the 2-and-0 unmissable -- **a census of a nine-line block,\nwhich is not where anyone thinks to run one.**\n\n*Fix design out to grok (`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/FIX_brief.md`) before any edit: whether\none token repairs both consequences, what reads `apcb_flags` today and might rely on it being stale,\nand whether the witness then goes 9/9 or only 8/9.*\n\n*** THE GROK FIX REVIEW RATIFIED THE ONE-TOKEN EDIT AND REFUSED TO RATIFY IT AS THE ROUND --\nbecause the witness was BLIND to the second consequence.  2026-09-07. ***\n\n*\"Both red rows read the same `got': A+16.  A build that only stops clobbering PTBR -- delete\n`:282', or assign new_flags to any other field -- also scores 9/9.  Consequence 2 is unpinned.\"*\n\n**That is a clause-(d) gap in my own witness**, and the repair it named is one readback on a page the\nprobe had already proved was backed.  Added as `swpctx flags' (PCB_A + 40) and MEASURED:\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111       FAIL   the clobber\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222   FAIL   the clobber's signature\n    swpctx flags     0x0          0xbbbb2222       FAIL   apcb_flags NEVER LOADED\n    HEAD 7/9 -> 7/10\n\n**Three red rows now pin three distinct consequences instead of one cell read twice.**\n\n**TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED, and a THIRD consequence found:**\n  * `apcb_flags` IS written globally -- by `wrfen` at `:221`.  My \"never assigned at all\" is true\n    only inside the load block, and I had stated it without that scope.\n  * *\"whatever the previous context left\"* is wrong: `swpctx` has never loaded that field from any\n    PCB, so the in-cpu value is **last `wrfen`, or 0 from the zeroed cpu struct**.\n  * **THIRD CONSEQUENCE:** today the SAVE at `:243-244` publishes that never-loaded word into the\n    OUTGOING PCB -- so a context switch *wipes the flags the guest planted*.  The bug propagates\n    outward into guest memory, not just inward into the cpu struct.\n\n**Every `apcb_flags` site in `src/` was enumerated and none relies on the stale value** (`wrfen`\nwrites without reading; the SAVE publishes; the header constants and `ALPHA_IF_CODE_FEN` are unused;\n`memory_alpha.c:46` reads `apcb_ptbr`, not flags).  *Sticky-FEN-across-`swpctx` is a PAL bug, not a\nbehaviour a reader is entitled to.*  **A separate finding volunteered in passing: `PAL_clrfen`\n(`0xae`) is named and user-mode-whitelisted but has NO `case`, so it reaches the default arm and\nstops the CPU.**\n\n*** `#507` APPLIED.  THE CENSUS THAT FOUND THE DEFECT IS THE CENSUS THAT CONFIRMS THE FIX. ***\n\n    before   apcb_ptbr 2   apcb_flags 0   (every other field 1)\n    after    apcb_ptbr 1   apcb_flags 1   (every field exactly 1)\n\nPropagated to all four trees; `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt, two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH on each, `est/` part 2 N/A.  *The witness re-run is in flight; its result is\nNOT recorded here until it lands, because a number written before the run that produces it cannot be\ntold from a number fitted to the answer.*\n\n*** `#507` MEASURED: 7/10 -> 10/10, SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx notflags  0xbbbb2222 -> 0xaaaa1111\n    swpctx flags     0x0        -> 0xbbbb2222     <- consequence 2, pinned by the row grok demanded\n\nSix controls green on both runs.  Clause (i) discharged by the pair.  **Nothing committed** -- the\nFable hold stands and this waits as finished work rather than as a proposal.\n\n*** COMMIT ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n    git show 7a41b3f -- src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n                                   contains the `#507' round tag 6 times\n\n**ALL FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31884:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the `swpctx' design pass (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt), raised under the\nfound-something-else licence.\n\ncodex enumerated the comparison rather than reasoning about it: *\"Local `a0' is unsigned.  Enumerating the\ncomparison gives `-2 -> VADDR', `-1 -> VADDR', `0 -> ALL', `1 -> VADDR', `2 -> VADDR', `3 -> VADDR'.  The\nheader's independent definitions require all-entry and all-per-process invalidation for the negative\nselectors.\"*\n\nSo `tbi(-1)' and `tbi(-2)' -- the OS's way of saying *invalidate everything* / *invalidate all\nper-process entries* -- are treated as *invalidate one virtual address*.  **A TLB flush that silently\nflushes almost nothing is the dangerous direction**: the guest believes stale translations are gone.\n\nNOT INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED BY ME.  The citation is given and the check is one comparison plus the header's\nselector definitions.  Filed rather than confirmed because the Alpha queue is now six rows deep and this\none needs the header read alongside the code.\n\n*** VERIFIED IN PART, 2026-09-08, BY codex -- AND IT REFUSED TO VERIFY THE HALF MY BRIEF HAD MADE\nUNANSWERABLE. ***\n\n**THE DISPATCH IS CONFIRMED.**  `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`, `case 0x33 /* PAL_OSF1_tbi */`:\n\n    if (a0 >= 1)\n            cpu->invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, a1, INVALIDATE_VADDR);\n    else\n            cpu->invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, 0, INVALIDATE_ALL);\n\n`a0` is a `uint64_t` local copied from `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0]`, itself `uint64_t`\n(`src/include/cpu_alpha.h`).  **There is no active signed cast** -- the only cast is inside a\ncommented-out `debug()`, which has no effect.  So `-2` is `0xff..fe`, `a0 >= 1` is TRUE, and both\nnegative selectors take the VADDR branch.  All six enumerated: `-2, -1, 1, 2, 3 -> VADDR`, `0 -> ALL`.\n\n**THE HEADER DEFINITIONS ARE REAL AND THEY ARE THE POINT** -- `src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h`:\n\n    #define ALPHA_TBIA()     alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)     /* all TB entries */\n    #define ALPHA_TBIAP()    alpha_pal_tbi(-1, 0)     /* all per-process */\n    #define ALPHA_TBISI(va)  alpha_pal_tbi(1, (va))\n    #define ALPHA_TBISD(va)  alpha_pal_tbi(2, (va))\n    #define ALPHA_TBIS(va)   alpha_pal_tbi(3, (va))\n\nSo the two selectors meaning *invalidate broadly* are exactly the two the comparison misroutes.\nSelector `0` is NOT defined by the header, so its fallback to INVALIDATE_ALL is not a requirement.\n\n*** WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, AND THE SEAT WAS RIGHT TO SAY SO: WHETHER THE TWO BRANCHES\nDIFFER IN EFFECT. ***  *\"The handler proves that different callback arguments are selected.  It does\nnot prove what the callback does with them ... I would not promote this to a verified functional\ninvalidation defect yet.\"*\n\n**THAT GAP IS MY BRIEF'S FAULT.**  I scoped its reading to the PALcode file and the header -- a habit\nadopted to keep the codex content filter from refusing -- and that scope EXCLUDES\n`invalidate_translation_caches`, which is the only thing that can answer the question.\n\n> *** THE TENSION IS WORTH NAMING BECAUSE IT WILL RECUR: NARROWING codex's READING SCOPE TO AVOID\n> THE CONTENT FILTER CAN STARVE THE QUESTION. ***  The scoping remedy in [[codexsubjectrefusal]] is\n> about which CORPUS trips the filter; it is not free, and here it cost the answer.  The seat named\n> the missing scope precisely rather than guessing, which is the behaviour to want.  Re-fired with\n> the widened scope as `codex_q3_20260908.txt`.\n\n**PROPOSED REPAIR (conditional on that answer):** `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)`, which sends both\nnegative selectors to the existing INVALIDATE_ALL path.  **And the seat flagged the disposition\nrisk:** if the callback ignores the flags or full-flushes either way, the source is still wrong but\nthe defect is UNOBSERVABLE -- documented-only, not a behavioural round.  A dispatch oracle (assert\nthe callback's `(address, flags)` for the six selectors) is decisive about DISPATCH and says nothing\nabout guest effect.\n\n*** Q3 IS SETTLED AND THE ANSWER IS (a): THE TWO BRANCHES DIFFER, AND `ALPHA_TBIA()` -- THE OS's\n\"INVALIDATE EVERY TLB ENTRY\" PRIMITIVE -- CURRENTLY INVALIDATES **VIRTUAL PAGE ZERO** AND NOTHING\nELSE.  codex, 2026-09-08, with the widened reading scope it asked for. ***\n\n**THE TRACE, end to end, each hop quoted in the answer:** `cpu_alpha.c` binds the callback to\n`alpha_invalidate_translation_caches`; `tmp_alpha_tail.c` generates it via\n`#define DYNTRANS_INVALIDATE_TC ... #include \"cpu_dyntrans.c\"`; and the two branches in\n`cpu_dyntrans.c` are genuinely different code:\n\n    VADDR   for (r=0; r<...; r++)\n                if (entry[r].valid &&\n                    (entry[r].vaddr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask) == (addr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask))\n                        INVALIDATE_TLB_ENTRY(...)          <- ADDRESS PREDICATE\n\n    ALL     if (flags & INVALIDATE_ALL) {\n                for (r=0; r<...; r++)\n                    if (entry[r].valid)\n                        INVALIDATE_TLB_ENTRY(..., 0);      <- NO ADDRESS PREDICATE\n                return;\n            }\n\n*** THE ARITHMETIC THAT MAKES IT BITE. ***  `ALPHA_TBIA()` is `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)` -- selector\n`-2`, **vaddr `0`**.  The unsigned comparison sends it to the VADDR branch, which then matches on\n`a1 == 0`.  Alpha uses 8 KiB pages and ordinary CPU construction leaves `vaddr_mask` ALL-ONES, so\nthe predicate matches **cached virtual page zero only -- and nothing at all if that page is not\ncached.  Every other mapping survives a flush the guest OS believes cleared the whole TLB.**\n\n**THE GUEST-VISIBLE CONSEQUENCE IS ESTABLISHED, not inferred:** a previously cached, subsequently\nremapped nonzero virtual page can still reach its OLD physical backing, because the Alpha load/store\nfast path consumes the surviving `host_load`/`host_store` pointers that\n`DYNTRANS_INVALIDATE_TLB_ENTRY` would have NULLed.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED THE ESCAPE HATCH I OFFERED. ***  My brief invited *\"if Alpha has no bootable\nin-tree rig that exercises paging, say so -- documented-only is a real answer.\"*  The seat declined\nit and gave three reasons: `memory_alpha.c` carries an ACTIVE three-level page-table walker (the\n\"Not yet implemented\" diagnostic is inside a specific zero-PTBR bootstrap case, not the walker);\nAlphaBook 1 IS registered in `machine_alpha.c`; and `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` and\n`alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py` already drive the ordinary Alpha machine with liveness and PAL-dispatch\ncontrols.  **\"Neither inherent unobservability nor absent paging support justifies closing it as\ndocumented-only.\"**\n\n**HONEST STATUS, in its words: \"source-confirmed functional defect; witness unvalidated.\"**  It ran\nno before/after comparison and found no dedicated test.  So the round still owes the rung-3\ndifferential -- but the basis for building one exists and is named.\n\n*** SEVERITY RAISED med -> high. ***  A documented OS primitive for *invalidate everything* clears\none page or none, silently, while the guest proceeds on the assumption that it worked -- and the\nconsequence is a guest reaching stale physical backing through the fast path.  That is the same\nclass as the other HIGH rows here (silent wrong data reaching the guest), not a cosmetic one.\n**This is a judgment and it is flagged for the adjudication rather than treated as settled**: the\nunderlying facts are codex's, verified by citation; the ranking is mine.\n\n> *** THE CLUSTER ORDERING NEEDS REVISITING, AND codex'S OWN CAVEAT SAYS WHY. ***  It ranked\n> `alphaswpctxreg` FIRST and labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the\n> greatest potential severity\"* -- explicitly because `alphatbinegsel` had \"confirmed dispatch\n> behavior but unverified functional consequences.\"  **Those consequences are now verified.**  The\n> ordering input has changed since the ranking was given, so the ranking should not be quoted as\n> though it still holds.\n\n**REPAIR, unchanged and now on a settled basis:** `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)`, sending both defined\nnegative selectors to INVALIDATE_ALL.  codex supplied the accurate CHANGELOG claim and its limit:\n\n> *\"Correct Alpha PAL `tbi` handling of negative selectors so broad invalidation clears all cached\n> address mappings.\"*\n\nIt **retains coarse full flushing for `-1`** and does NOT implement `ALPHA_TBIAP()`'s selective\npreservation of global (ASM=0) entries -- so the round must claim the coarse repair and disclose\nthat, rather than claiming the architectural semantics.\n\n*One stale in-tree comment fell out of the same trace and is worth a separate look: a comment\nclaiming the VADDR path \"clears only the quick array\" is stale for this implementation -- the\n64-bit entry helper NULLs `host_load`, `host_store`, `phys_addr` and `phys_page` and clears the TLB\nentry's `valid` flag.*\n\n*** WITNESS DESIGN: codex REFUSED IT AT THE PROMPT (2026-09-08), and the refusal is informative\nrather than merely inconvenient. ***\n\n`codex_witness_20260908.txt` is 0 bytes; its `.err` is 6,291 bytes with **zero file-read lines** --\nso it refused before opening anything, which makes this the CHAIN-SHAPE trigger recorded in\n[[codexsubjectrefusal]] rather than the archive-reading one.  The same seat answered three other\nbriefs on this very defect tonight, including the callback trace that settled Q3.\n\n**The distinction is between ANALYSING existing code and CONSTRUCTING a guest sequence that reaches\nthe defect.  Witness design is inherently the second**, so codex is the wrong seat for it -- not a\ndegraded one.  Re-fired the identical brief, byte-for-byte, at grok.\n\n*** THE WITNESS IS CONSTRUCTIBLE, AND grok SUPPLIED IT.  2026-09-08, on the brief codex refused at\nthe prompt -- reused BYTE-FOR-BYTE, so this is a seat-capability result and not a rewrite. ***\n\n**RUNG 3, unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.**  `machine_alpha.c` already `device_add`s LCA for\n`ST_ALPHABOOK1`, so nothing is added to reach PAL 0x33 -- which is the ladder's clause (iii).\n\n**THE OBSERVABLE IS A STALE DYNTRANS HIT AFTER A PTE REMAP, not a dispatch spy.**  That is the\nguest-visible channel codex could not name from its narrower scope: fill a mapping, remap the PTE\nbehind it, `ALPHA_TBIA()`, then load and see the OLD value.\n\n*** THE CONSTRAINT THAT DECIDES WHETHER THE ROW IS REAL OR VACUOUS, and it is a probe-design trap I\nwould have walked into: THE DEBUGGER PROMPT ALL-FLUSHES BETWEEN INTERACTIONS. ***  `debugger.c`,\nwhen `debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction` reaches 0, calls `cpu_create_or_reset_tc` and\n`invalidate_translation_caches(..., INVALIDATE_ALL)`.\n\n> **So the FILL, the REMAP, the `tbi` and the OBSERVING LOAD must all be inside ONE `step N`.  Split\n> them across two `step`s and BOTH builds re-walk after the remap -- the row goes GREEN ON HEAD and\n> the witness proves nothing.**  Every existing probe in this tree is written as a sequence of\n> debugger interactions, so the obvious construction is the broken one.\n\n**AND THE OBVIOUS SUBSTRATE CANNOT WITNESS IT EITHER:** an identity map (`ptbr == 0`) has no PTE to\nchange, and **K0SEG is checked BEFORE `ptbr`** in `memory_alpha.c` -- so a K0SEG address never\nconsults the page table at all.  The witness needs a real three-level walk, which is why it reuses\n`alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py`'s page-table substrate rather than starting from scratch.\n\n**THE SEQUENCE** (one session, one `step N`), with the walk planted through K0SEG while `ptbr` is\nstill 0:\n\n    ldq  a0, k0(PCB)\n    call_pal 0x30              # swpctx: arm the walk, and it ALL-flushes\n    lda  t0, 0x2000            # a NON-ZERO VPN -- see the page-0 constraint\n    ldq  t1, 0(t0)             # FILL: cold walk, host_load -> P_OLD\n    ldq  t4, planted PTE(P_NEW)\n    stq  t4, 0(k0(L3+8))       # remap via K0SEG, NOT via the walked VPN\n    lda  a0, -2(zero)          # a0 = 0xfffffffffffffffe\n    lda  a1, 0(zero)           # TBIA's a1 -- must NOT be the test VPN\n    call_pal 0x33              # PAL_OSF1_tbi\n    ldq  t2, 0(t0)             # OBSERVE: VAL_OLD on HEAD, VAL_NEW after the fix\n\n**Q3 (page-0) answered and extended:** the buggy arm invalidates virtual page 0, so a test VPN of 0\nwould pass on BOTH builds -- vacuous by address choice.  grok adds a second constraint I had not\nasked for: **`a1` must not be the test VPN either**, or the VADDR arm matches it and the row goes\ngreen for the wrong reason.\n\n**AN INSTRUMENT CONTROL IT VOLUNTEERED:** `lda a0, -2(zero)` really does sign-extend (`lda_0` does\n`(int64_t)(int32_t)arg[2]`, and LDA sign-extends its int16), **but print `a0` and `a1` after the\nprogram anyway** -- a truncated `0xfffe` would ALSO take the VADDR arm on HEAD and the ALL arm under\nthe fix, so the row would still move while no longer testing `TBIA`.  *That is a control against the\ninstrument rather than against the emulator, and it is exactly the hand-assembled-encoding trap this\nproject has recorded four times.*\n\n**IT ALSO CONFIRMED ALL SIX PREMISES AND CORRECTED A STALE IN-TREE COMMENT** -- the comment claiming\nthe VADDR path \"only clears the quick array, not `vph_tlb_entry[]`\" is stale: the 64-bit helper does\nNULL `host_load`/`host_store`/`phys_addr`/`phys_page` and clear `valid`.  **The bug is WHICH entries\nare selected, not that the helper is inert.**  It adds that the ALL arm does not implement ASM=0\neither, *\"extra, not a contradiction\"* -- consistent with codex's caveat that the proposed repair is\ncoarse and does not give `ALPHA_TBIAP()` its selective semantics.\n\n**REMAINING: build it.**  The design is complete enough to implement; nothing here has been run, so\nthe row's status stays *source-confirmed functional defect; witness DESIGNED, not yet measured*.\n\n*** THE DEBUGGER ALL-FLUSH IS VERIFIED BY ME, not taken from the seat.  The anchor is\n`grep -n debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction src/debugger/debugger.c` -- note the path is\n`src/debugger/`, NOT `src/core/`, which is where my first grep looked and found nothing. ***\n\n    :781  if (debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction > 0) {\n    :782          debugger_n_steps_left_before_interaction --;\n          } else {\n    :793          cpu_create_or_reset_tc(debugger_machine->cpus[i]);\n    :795          invalidate_translation_caches(\n\nSo the flush fires on the branch taken when the counter is NOT positive -- i.e. whenever the\ndebugger is about to interact.  **The one-`step N` constraint is real.**\n\n*A file-not-found from a wrong path is the safe direction: it is loud.  Had I grepped a path that\nEXISTS but is the wrong copy, I would have gotten a confident empty result instead -- which is the\npristine-`src/` trap this project has recorded, and the reason the citation-form rule prefers a\nverified grep anchor over a bare line number.*\n\n*** THE ASSESS STAGE HERE IS NOT A PANEL, AND THE GATE IS RIGHT TO SAY SO.  2/9. ***\n\n`check_stage_panels` reports this stage as having moved on without the full panel -- missing\nfable5, opus5, agy, kimi, glm, deepseek and minimax.  **That is accurate.**  What ran was TWO\nTARGETED BRIEFS, not a panel: codex on the dispatch and the callback trace, then grok on the\nwitness design after codex refused it.\n\n**I am recording this rather than backfilling.**  The owner's rule is FULL PANEL ON EVERY STAGE and\nSTOP AND ASK if a seat cannot be run -- *not degrade quietly* -- and quietly is exactly how a\ntwo-seat investigation becomes a \"panel\" in the record.\n\n*** THE THREE SEATS THAT COULD NOT HAVE RUN, by name and reason: opus5 and fable5 are PRESERVED for\nthe 2026-09-09 adjudication by standing owner directive, and agy is the MEASUREMENT RESERVE by the\n2026-08-30 ruling.  The four packet-fed transports COULD have run and were not fired -- that is a\nchoice I made, not a constraint. ***\n\n**So this row's assess stage owes a real panel before it is treated as adjudicated**, and the\nfindings it produced -- the dispatch defect, the callback trace, the witness design -- stand on\ntheir own as two seats' work, correctly attributed.\n\n*** THE ASSESS PANEL FIRED (four packet-fed seats) AND THREE OF FOUR FOUND THE SAME DEFECT -- IN MY\nBRIEF, NOT IN THE CODE. ***\n\n**THE SEVERITY RAISE AND THE COARSE REPAIR ARE CONFIRMED 4/4.**  glm, kimi, minimax and deepseek all\nsustain med -> high and all accept shipping `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)` with the ASM=0 limit\ndisclosed.\n\n*** DEFECT 1, FOUND INDEPENDENTLY BY glm, kimi AND minimax: MY POLARITY WAS INVERTED. ***  The brief\nsaid *\"VADDR keeps an entry only when `(vaddr_page & vaddr_mask) == (addr_page & vaddr_mask)`\"*.\n**The VADDR branch INVALIDATES the matching entry; it does not keep it.**  Verified by opening\n`cpu_dyntrans.c`: `if (flags & INVALIDATE_VADDR)` runs a loop whose own comment reads *\"they must be\ninvalidated based on the bits that matter\"*.\n\n**The CONCLUSION is unaffected** -- with `a1 = 0` the arm invalidates page zero and leaves every\nother mapping, which is the defect as filed -- **but the sentence stating the mechanism contradicted\nits own THEREFORE**, and glm said so in exactly those terms.  *A packet-fed seat can only reason\nfrom the packet, so an inverted premise there is the one error they cannot route around; that three\nof four caught it anyway is the premise-audit instruction working.*\n\n*** DEFECT 2, FOUND BY glm AND minimax: \"THE CONSEQUENCES ARE NOW VERIFIED\" OVERCLAIMS. ***  codex's\nown status line was *\"source-confirmed functional defect; witness UNVALIDATED\"*, and I compressed\nthat to \"verified\" when asking whether the ordering should change.  **What is verified is the\nDISPATCH.  The witness is DESIGNED, NOT BUILT.**  glm: *\"'witness constructible, shape known' is not\nverification -- do not promote this row to first on the packet as written.\"*\n\n**SO THE ORDERING QUESTION IS NOT SETTLED AND MUST NOT BE RECORDED AS IF IT WERE.**  kimi says this\nrow goes first; glm says not on the evidence as stated.  **Both are answering honestly and they\ndisagree because my premise was wrong.**  It goes to the adjudication as a live question with the\noverclaim corrected.\n\n**RESIDUALS WORTH KEEPING:**\n* **kimi's adversarial re-break: `a0 + 2 <= 5`** -- an unsigned shifted-window test that re-admits\n  the negative selectors while looking like a range check.  deepseek's is\n  `if (a0 != 0 && a0 != (uint64_t)-1)`.  **Both belong in the round's mutant list.**\n* **the designed witness is BLIND to stale STORES** (a same-VA store then load reads back green on a\n  broken build) **and to permission-only remaps** -- kimi.  A round building it owes those channels\n  or a disclosure.\n* deepseek, Q4: the witness is vacuous when the old and new data happen to match -- so the remap\n  must change the observed bytes, not merely the mapping.\n\n*** THE agy SEAT ANSWERED AND IT IS THE STRONGEST ANSWER ON THIS ROW: A FILE-READING SEAT THAT\nRE-DERIVED EVERY PREMISE FROM SOURCE RATHER THAN RATIFYING THE PACKET. ***\n\n**It confirms Q1 high and Q2 the coarse repair, and it re-verified the six inlined premises with\nfile and line** -- the uint64_t declaration, the two OS wrappers, the two dyntrans branches,\nvaddr_mask, the 8 KiB page size, the debugger ALL-FLUSH, the K0SEG early return, and the\nMEM_MIPS/MEM_M88K-only device-failure arms with ALPHA_KENTRY_MM having zero consumers.\n\n*** IT IS THE FOURTH INDEPENDENT STATEMENT OF THE CORRECT POLARITY, and it did not have to be\ntold. ***  Unprompted, it writes that INVALIDATE_VADDR *\"invalidates only entries matching\n(vaddr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask) == (addr_page & cpu->vaddr_mask)\"* -- which is the opposite of my\nbrief's *\"keeps an entry only when\"*.  glm, kimi and minimax caught the inversion from the packet\nalone; agy simply states the truth from the file.\n\n**Q2 gained a REASON the packet-fed seats could not supply**, and it is the reason that makes the\ncoarse repair right rather than merely acceptable: *\"GXemul's translation cache does not track ASM\nbits or ASN tags per entry in vph_tlb_entry / l1_64 at all\"*, so ASM=0 selectivity is not a finer\nsemantics this round declined to implement -- **it has no representation to be implemented in.**\nFlushing everything on ALPHA_TBIAP() is conservative and correct against that structure.\n\n*** FOUR ADVERSARIAL RE-BREAKS FOR Q3, AND THE FOURTH IS A CLAUSE-(d) NULL-REPAIR. ***\n\n    (uint32_t)a0 >= 1        looks like the 32-bit PALcode argument convention; 0xfffffffe >= 1\n    a0 > 0                   looks like a clean equivalent of a0 >= 1; -2 as uint64_t is > 0\n    a0 >= 1 || a0 <= 3       a one-character typo that is true for every 64-bit value\n    else -> INVALIDATE_VADDR the GUARD is correctly fixed and the ELSE ARM still says VADDR\n\n**The fourth is the one a mutant table would most likely miss**, because every row aimed at the\nGUARD sees a correct guard.  It is clause (d) exactly: keep the repair, null its effect -- and it\nbelongs in the round's mutant list beside kimi's `a0 + 2 <= 5` and deepseek's\n`a0 != 0 && a0 != (uint64_t)-1`.\n\n*** FIVE WITNESS BLIND CHANNELS, AND TWO ARE NEW AND SHARPER THAN THE THREE ALREADY FILED. ***\n\n* **TEST VPN = PAGE 0 IS SELF-DEFEATING.**  HEAD's defective arm flushes page zero, so a witness\n  that happens to pick VPN 0 goes GREEN ON THE DEFECTIVE BUILD.  *The defect is that only page 0\n  is flushed, so page 0 is the one page that cannot witness it.*\n* **A POLLUTED a1 MASKS IT.**  If a1 is not explicitly zeroed before `call_pal 0x33`, HEAD flushes\n  whatever a1 holds -- so if a1 happens to carry the test VPN the broken build looks correct.\n* the other three restate what kimi and deepseek already gave: value collision, split debugger\n  steps, and K0SEG or ptbr == 0 bypassing the page table.\n\n**Q5 ORDERING: agy says THIS ROW FIRST, and it is careful about why.**  It does NOT repeat my\nwithdrawn overclaim -- it writes *\"witness FEASIBILITY\"*, which is what is actually established,\nand it grounds the ranking on a severity comparison it made itself: `alphaswpctxreg` is *\"medium\nseverity (writing the return value to a0 rather than v0)\"*, and swpctx's own context arming via\napcb_ptbr works regardless of which register receives the old context address.\n\n*** I WROTE \"THE ORDERING NOW READS 3-1\" AND IT READS 2-3 THE OTHER WAY.  Corrected the same\nhour, from the answers, before this record shipped. ***\n\nI tallied from my own summary instead of from the five files, and got both numbers wrong --\nincluding deepseek, whose closing sentence is *\"if this row is high and verified, it may go\nfirst, but that is not established from the packet.\"*  **That is a tally of seats written from\nmemory, which is the exact class this project keeps paying for.**  Read from the artifacts:\n\n    kimi        THIS ROW FIRST    \"the ranking variable was uncertainty; it is now zero\"\n    agy         THIS ROW FIRST    on a severity comparison it made itself from source\n    glm         NOT YET           \"constructibility is not verification; no executed differential\"\n    deepseek    NOT ESTABLISHED   cannot rank without alphaswpctxreg's severity\n    minimax     NOT YET           \"mark ready to elevate once the witness actually runs\"\n\n> **BUT THE COUNT IS THE LEAST INFORMATIVE THING HERE, AND THE ARGUMENTS CONVERGE COMPLETELY.**\n> All three NOT-YET seats give ONE reason and it is the same reason: **the witness has not been\n> RUN.**  None of them disputes the severity or the mechanism.  So this is not a 2-3 split to be\n> resolved by weight -- it is a unanimous statement of the CONDITION under which the ordering\n> flips, and that condition is exactly what opus5's hold is for.  *Count the arguments, not the\n> seats.*\n\n**minimax put the trap in the sharpest form:** *\"The previous ranking was honest.  Flipping it on\nthe strength of 'constructible in principle' is the same shape of error in reverse.\"*\n\n*** FOUR FINDINGS THAT WERE SITTING BELOW THE VERDICT LINES, AND I HAD READ ONLY THE TAILS. ***\n\n* **glm: THE THEREFORE NAMES ONLY `ALPHA_TBIA()`, AND `ALPHA_TBIAP()` IS EQUALLY BROKEN.**\n  `-1` misdispatches identically, so **the CONTEXT-SWITCH primitive is broken too** -- which glm\n  says *\"strengthens Q1 and matters for Q5\"*.  agy's verdict line independently names both\n  wrappers.  My brief's conclusion sentence understated the defect's own reach.\n* **glm: MY SEVERITY ARGUMENT HAS AN UNESTABLISHED PREMISE -- \"during ordinary operation\".**\n  Nothing shown demonstrates a supported guest executing `tbi` with a negative operand during\n  normal running; the wrappers existing in the OS header is not the same claim.  **Settling fact\n  named: a trace of PAL 0x33 invocations with their `a0` values during an ordinary boot.**  glm\n  confirms high on per-invocation consequence regardless, but flags that if the rubric wants\n  demonstrated in-guest incidence, that trace is the missing artifact.  *That is a premise audit\n  landing on the one judgment in this row that is mine.*\n* **glm: A CROSS-ROW ATTRIBUTION HAZARD.**  This repair changes context-switch flush behaviour and\n  `swpctx` IS the context-switch PAL -- so **whichever of the two lands SECOND must re-baseline its\n  witness with the other's fix present**, or a TBIAP on the switch path confuses attribution\n  between the two rows.  Neither row records this today.\n* **kimi's Q2 open question was CLOSED BY agy, and neither seat knew.**  kimi: *\"the one fact that\n  would change this answer: do tc entries carry an ASN/ASM field?  Not stated; if they do not, the\n  fine semantics are not even implementable here.\"*  agy read the source: **they do not** --\n  `vph_tlb_entry` and `l1_64` carry no ASM/ASN.  So Q2's answer is not *coarse repair accepted with\n  a disclosed limit* but the stronger **the finer semantics have no representation to be\n  implemented in**, and the disclosure should say that instead.\n\n**AND kimi's Q3 CARRIES A REVIEWER INVARIANT WORTH MORE THAN THE MUTANT:** its `a0 + 2 <= 3 + 2`\nadmits exactly {-2,-1,0,1,2,3} by mod-2^64 wrap, so **every one of the five documented wrappers\ntakes the identical branch it took on the broken build** and any test authored against the\ndefective behaviour stays green.  The honest look-alike `(a0 - 1) <= 2` is CORRECT -- which is\nprecisely why the `+2` form survives review.  kimi's invariant: *\"the true-branch preimage over\nall 2^64 inputs must be exactly {1,2,3}; any test expressed as addition or subtraction on the\nunsigned a0 is a red flag.\"*\n\n**A SECOND ROW GAINS AN UNSOLICITED SEVERITY READ:** `alphaswpctxreg` medium, argued from the\nregister semantics.  It is carried to that row rather than acted on here.\n\n*** Q2 IS NOW SETTLED FROM PRIMARY SOURCE PLUS A GREP, AND THE ANSWER IS STRONGER THAN THE\nQUESTION I ASKED. ***  The 21066 manual arrived on a DIFFERENT row (`lcaconfbus1kill`, fetched at\ncodex's citation) and `machine_alpha.c:61` does `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`, so it is the right\nmanual for this machine's chipset.\n\n**WHAT THE HARDWARE DOES** -- `_scratchpad/21066_66a_hrm.txt:1484-1486` (ITB) and `:1684-1686`\n(DTB), read locally, not taken from a seat:\n\n> *\"The ITBs support a single address space number (ASN) through the PTE ASM bit.  Each PTE entry\n> in the ITB contains an ASM bit.  **Write operations to the ITBASM register invalidate all entries\n> that do not have the ASM bit set.**\"*\n\nThat is exactly `ALPHA_TBIAP()`'s selectivity, documented, and it confirms the round was right to\ncall the repair COARSE rather than complete.\n\n**WHAT THE TREE CAN REPRESENT** -- and this is the half that decides the disposition:\n\n    grep -rn \"ALPHA_PTE_ASM\" src/     ->  ONE hit: the #define itself, alpha_cpu.h:223\n    grep -rn \"apcb_asn\"      src/     ->  written by swpctx, read by a debug print, and\n                                          consulted by NO translation lookup\n\n**`ALPHA_PTE_ASM` IS DECLARED AND HAS ZERO CONSUMERS** -- the same shape as `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM`,\nwhich the round already noted is defined with nothing using it.\n\n> **SO THE HONEST DISCLOSURE IS NOT \"WE SHIPPED A COARSE REPAIR AND DEFERRED THE FINER\n> SEMANTICS\".  IT IS: THE FINER SEMANTICS HAVE NO REPRESENTATION IN THIS EMULATOR'S TRANSLATION\n> CACHE, AND IMPLEMENTING THEM IS A SEPARATE ROUND THAT MUST FIRST ADD THE PLUMBING.**  Over-\n> invalidation is conservative in the safe direction: it costs re-walks, never stale data -- the\n> exact opposite of the defect being fixed.\n\n*This is kimi's open question closed from the other side.  kimi named the deciding fact -- \"do tc\nentries carry an ASN/ASM field?  If they do not, the fine semantics are not even implementable\nhere\" -- agy answered it from the source, and the manual now supplies what the hardware would have\ndone.  **Three seats and a primary source, none of which saw the others.***\n\n**AND A CAUTION ON MY OWN CHECK, because the first grep I would have written is wrong:** the ASM\nbit IS present in the tree as a PTE constant, so a census keyed on *\"does the tree know about\nASM?\"* answers YES and would have contradicted agy.  The right question is *\"does anything CONSUME\nit?\"* -- which is the carrier's own deriver rule: **key on what makes a member a member, not on a\ncorrelate of it.**",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31928:     "hold_question": "`alphatbinegsel` is the one row I RAISED IN SEVERITY MYSELF this session (med -> high), and that ranking is the single judgment in it that is not a measurement.  THE FACTS ARE TWO SEATS' AND WERE RE-VERIFIED BY ME: `ALPHA_TBIA()` is `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)`, `a0` is a uint64_t with no signed cast, so `a0 >= 1` is TRUE for -2 and the call takes the VADDR arm with a1 = 0 -- **the OS primitive meaning 'invalidate every TLB entry' invalidates cached virtual page ZERO and nothing else.**  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) SEVERITY: is high right?  A documented primitive silently under-flushing during ordinary operation, with the fast path then consuming surviving host_load/host_store pointers into stale backing -- I put that with the other HIGH rows; confirm or correct, and note the raise is MINE, not a seat's.  (2) CLAIM SCOPE: the proposed repair `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)` is COARSE -- it fixes the misdispatch and does NOT give `ALPHA_TBIAP()` its ASM=0 selectivity.  Ship the coarse repair with the limit disclosed, or does the round owe the finer semantics?  (3) ORDERING: a file-reading seat ranked `alphaswpctxreg` FIRST and labelled that 'a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential severity' -- explicitly because THIS row's consequences were then unverified.  **They are now verified.**  Does the input to that ranking having changed move this row first?",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:31999:   "hold_question": "TWO SEATS ARE HELD ON THIS STAGE AND THEY ARE HELD FOR DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.\n\nfable5 -- ADJUDICATE: `alphatbinegsel` is the one row I RAISED IN SEVERITY MYSELF this session (med -> high), and that ranking is the single judgment in it that is not a measurement.  THE FACTS ARE TWO SEATS' AND WERE RE-VERIFIED BY ME: `ALPHA_TBIA()` is `alpha_pal_tbi(-2, 0)`, `a0` is a uint64_t with no signed cast, so `a0 >= 1` is TRUE for -2 and the call takes the VADDR arm with a1 = 0 -- **the OS primitive meaning 'invalidate every TLB entry' invalidates cached virtual page ZERO and nothing else.**  THREE QUESTIONS.  (1) SEVERITY: is high right?  A documented primitive silently under-flushing during ordinary operation, with the fast path then consuming surviving host_load/host_store pointers into stale backing -- I put that with the other HIGH rows; confirm or correct, and note the raise is MINE, not a seat's.  (2) CLAIM SCOPE: the proposed repair `if (a0 >= 1 && a0 <= 3)` is COARSE -- it fixes the misdispatch and does NOT give `ALPHA_TBIAP()` its ASM=0 selectivity.  Ship the coarse repair with the limit disclosed, or does the round owe the finer semantics?  (3) ORDERING: a file-reading seat ranked `alphaswpctxreg` FIRST and labelled that 'a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential severity' -- explicitly because THIS row's consequences were then unverified.  **They are now verified.**  Does the input to that ranking having changed move this row first?\n\nopus5 -- MEASURE: BUILD THE WITNESS.  You are the compile-and-measure seat and this row's single unvalidated element is its witness -- codex's own status line was 'source-confirmed functional defect; witness UNVALIDATED', and an earlier draft of my packet compressed that to 'verified', which the assess panel caught.  The design: on unmodified -E alpha -e alphabook1, fill a mapping, remap the PTE behind it, call ALPHA_TBIA(), then load and observe the OLD value.  *** THE TRAP: the debugger ALL-FLUSHES between interactions, so the fill, remap, tbi and observing load must be inside ONE step N or both builds re-walk and the row is GREEN on the defective build.  And K0SEG is checked BEFORE ptbr, so a K0SEG address never consults the page table and cannot witness this at all. ***  THREE CHANNELS THE ASSESS PANEL SAID IT MAY MISS, each owed a row or a disclosure: (a) kimi -- a same-VA STORE then load reads back green on a broken build; (b) kimi -- permission-only remaps; (c) deepseek -- the row is VACUOUS when the old and new data happen to match, so the remap must change the observed BYTES and not merely the mapping.  Report pre-fix RED and post-fix GREEN with the same script, or say plainly that you could not build it and what stopped you.  *** TWO MORE VACUITY TRAPS, from the agy seat, and the FIRST is the one this row's own design walks into: (d) DO NOT USE VPN 0 AS THE TEST PAGE.  HEAD's defective arm flushes page zero, so a witness on page 0 goes GREEN ON THE DEFECTIVE BUILD -- the defect is that only page 0 is flushed, so page 0 is the one page that cannot witness it.  (e) ZERO a1 EXPLICITLY before call_pal 0x33: if a1 carries the test VPN, HEAD flushes exactly the entry under observation and the broken build looks correct. ***"
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32008:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the `swpctx' design pass (regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).\n\ncodex: *\"SAVE discards store return values; the load helpers discard read status.  `ctx' changes before\nloading finishes, with no failure check before committing the PCB.  In this fork, failed translations zero\nread buffers, so ignored failure can become committed zero-valued state.  'Simultaneously' does not\nestablish failure atomicity.\"*\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH `#95' IS THE SHARP PART, AND IT IS THE THIRD TIME TONIGHT THAT FIX HAS TURNED UP\nWITH AN UNCONSIDERED EDGE. ***  `#95' zeroes the caller's buffer on a device READ so a partial fill cannot\nleak host memory -- unambiguously right.  Here that same zeroing means **a failed load returns a\nwell-formed zero instead of an error**, and the caller, which checks nothing, commits it to the PCB.  A\nsafety fix converting a detectable failure into a plausible value is worth knowing about generally.\n\nThe comment above the assignments says *\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"* -- and as codex\nnotes, simultaneity of ASSIGNMENT is not atomicity under FAILURE.  That comment now has three separate\nthings wrong with it across this row, [[alphaswpctxptbr]] and this one.\n\nNOT INDEPENDENTLY VERIFIED BY ME -- two further concerns from the same trace are recorded with it and are\nalso unverified: the helpers request translated accesses without `PHYSICAL', and `store_64bit_word'\nsign-extends the address where the matching loads and 32-bit stores do not.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : propagate context-memory failures through a defined failed-switch outcome\n    one oracle     : an injected memory failure produces that outcome without reporting a successful switch\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** codex ASSESS, 2026-09-08.  THE CLAIM SPLITS ACROSS TWO LAYERS, AND ONLY ONE HALF IS THIS\nHANDLER'S FAULT. ***\n\n**THE STORE HALF IS TRUE.**  `store_64bit_word` returns `int` -- it is `return cpu->memory_rw(...)`\n-- and `swpctx` discards that status at every save site.\n\n*** THE LOAD HALF IS NOT THIS HANDLER'S TO IGNORE. ***  `load_64bit_word` returns `uint64_t` DATA\nand has **no failure indication at all**: it calls `memory_rw`, discards the result, byte-swaps, and\nreturns the assembled word.  **A caller cannot check what the helper does not report.**  So \"the\nload ignores every memory-access failure\" mis-assigns responsibility -- the defect there is the\nhelper's ABI, and `load_64bit_word` **erases** the failure information rather than the caller\ndropping it.\n\n**Neither helper passes `NO_EXCEPTIONS`** -- both use `CACHE_DATA`, so my Q2 premise was wrong.\n**The consequence codex states: failed translations can become committed zero-valued PCB state.**\n\n## *** MY OPTION SET WAS INCOMPLETE, AND THE MISSING MEMBER IS THE RIGHT ONE ***\n\nI offered three dispositions: real defect with an observable consequence; fidelity gap with no\nchannel; or not a defect at all.\n\n> *\"Your three possibilities omit the present combination: **observable behavior is proved, but\n> architectural incorrectness is not yet proved.**\"*\n\n*\"This is not 'nothing to ignore': stores expose failure, and loads erase underlying failure\ninformation.  It is also not 'no observable channel': PCB saveback and PTBR supply one.  But\nchanging behavior for invalid input requires establishing what that input permits or requires.  **I\nwould not invent a fault or transactional switch rule and use that invention as the oracle.**\"*\n\n**Provisional severity: LOW, affecting guest state integrity; no host compromise, no unprivileged\nroute.**  If promoted, the claim must narrow to *reported failures being discarded or hidden*.\n\n*This is the option-set-completeness rule landing on me, on a brief where I thought I had enumerated\nexhaustively -- the same failure the three-membered de-fatalisation set records.*\n\n## THE IN-TREE IDIOM -- and a SIBLING SITE WITH THE SAME DEFECT\n\n* **`wrfen` (`cpu_alpha_palcode.c`) ALSO discards `store_64bit_word`'s status.**  So this is a small\n  CLASS inside the PAL layer, not a single site.  *Filed as part of this row rather than separately:\n  same file, same helper, same fix design.*\n* **`debugger_cmds.c` DOES check it** -- assigns to `res` and tests `!res`, for both widths.  **That\n  establishes the status is meaningful**, which is what makes discarding it a defect rather than a\n  convention.\n* **`cpu_alpha_instr_loadstore.c` checks direct `memory_rw` calls** and returns on failure before\n  consuming read data or reporting store-conditional success -- *\"the strongest nearby\n  implementation idiom, though it is not a PAL context switch.\"*\n* **NO checking PAL context-switch sibling exists**, which is why the fix has no exact precedent.\n\n## *** A THIRD INDEPENDENT CONFIRMATION OF [[alphafaultclaim]], UNASKED ***\n\n*\"The latter's comment saying the translator already signalled a fault is also unsupported by the\ninspected Alpha failure path.\"*  **Three separate briefs tonight -- alphatbinegsel Q3, alphapteprot,\nand this one -- have now reached that same conclusion from three different directions**, none of\nthem asked about it.\n\n## SCOPE: NO MERGER with [[alphaswpctxreg]], despite being lines apart\n\n    row              fix concern                          oracle concern\n    ---------------  -----------------------------------  ---------------------------------------\n    alphaswpctxreg   correct result-register assignment   the switch's return value appears in\n                                                          the required register\n    alphactxfail     preserve access status and apply a   a failed access produces the required\n                     VERIFIED failure policy              state and control flow\n\n*\"Either correction can exist while the other problem remains.\"*  **Proximity is not the test** --\nwhich is the round-scope rule stated exactly.\n\n**BLOCKED, like `alphapteprot`, on an Alpha architecture manual**: none of the sixteen PDFs in\n`_scratchpad/` is an Alpha reference, and the seat declined to certify fault, restartability or\nrollback semantics without one.  **That is now THREE rows blocked on absent primary sources**\n(`armbfcdecode` on DDI 0406; `alphapteprot` and this one on an Alpha ARM).",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32144:   "state_note": "Volunteered by the codex seat under the found-something-else licence on the `sgirandomcolour` design\npass, 2026-09-07: *\"The sibling's `lin_b diag once` row does not assert `(once)`.  It counts\n`\"unimplemented dst_mode\"` occurrences.  Removing the required marker alone would leave that row\ngreen.\"*\n\n**This is the CLAIM->ROW check failing on SHIPPED material.**  The row's NAME asserts the latch\nmarker; its ORACLE counts a different string.  So the one row whose whole purpose is to pin the\nsuppression marker is blind to that marker's removal -- vacuous with respect to its own name.\n\n*The house idiom is `(once)`, which a census this fork ran on 2026-08-30 put at 24 occurrences in 4\nfiles against 4 and 5 for the two rival spellings -- so the marker is load-bearing convention, and a\nrow named for it that cannot see it is exactly the check-that-cannot-fail shape.*\n\n**NOT fixed in-round and not a floor breach of `sgirandomcolour`:** it is a row in a DIFFERENT,\nalready-shipped detector, above the floor, so it is coverage debt under the stopping rule.  It\nbecomes L3 the moment a round relies on that row as the sole carrier of a latch obligation.\n\nCheap repair when a round touches that probe: assert the marker text itself, not the message body."
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32180:   "id": "gateworktree",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32195:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/gateworktree/codex_assess_20260908.txt",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32478:   "state_note": "Filed 2026-09-07.  Full record in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md under the same id.\n\n*** THIS ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE LEDGER<->BUGFILE SYNC IS ONE-DIRECTIONAL AND ITS WARRANT HAS DECAYED -- see [[bugfilereverse]].  It was filed into OUTSTANDING_BUGS and given no ledger row, which is precisely the failure check_bugfile_sync.py was written to stop, in the direction it does not check. ***\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : return/cache permissions according to the actual translation contract\n    one oracle     : observed access disposition matches independently specified page permissions\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** codex ASSESS, 2026-09-08: THE MECHANISM IS CONFIRMED AND THIS ROW'S FRAMING IS REFUTED.  The\ndefect is bigger than the one filed. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED:** every successful walk returns `2`.  `memory_alpha.c` ends the three-level walk with\n`// TODO.` then `return 2;`, and the kernel direct-map and zero-PTBR bootstrap arms return `2` as\nwell.  **The only PTE checks are `ALPHA_PTE_VALID`; `flags` is unused.**  There is no\nprotection-dependent successful return anywhere.\n\n*** BUT \"SO `memory_rw` CACHES EVERY WALK\" IS THE WRONG DIAGNOSIS, AND THE CONTRACT IS DOCUMENTED\nRATHER THAN UNWRITTEN. ***  `src/include/memory.h`:\n\n    0    MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED       denied or failed\n    1    MEMORY_ACCESS_OK           success, READ-ONLY\n    2    MEMORY_ACCESS_OK_WRITE     success, READ/WRITE\n    256  MEMORY_NOT_FULL_PAGE       suppresses whole-page caching\n\nwith the prose authority spelled out in `memory_arm.c`.  **Both 1 and 2 are cacheable** -- the\nconsumer passes `ok - 1` as the cache's write permission, so `1 -> host_store = NULL` and\n`2 -> host_store = host_page`.  *Caching a read-only mapping is legitimate; the defect is claiming\nREAD/WRITE for every page.*\n\n## *** AND CHANGING THE RETURN TO 1 WOULD NOT BE ENOUGH -- BOTH PATHS ACCEPT THE PROHIBITED STORE ***\n\n* **fast path:** `host_store` is installed, so the store never reaches a check;\n* **slow path:** `memory_rw` supplies `FLAG_WRITEFLAG` but **rejects only a ZERO translation\n  result**.  Alpha returns 2, so the RAM path performs the write; and the physical backing helper in\n  `core/memory.c` has no guest-PTE protection check either.\n\n> **So the repair is request-time ENFORCEMENT plus permission-dependent cache metadata -- not a\n> one-line return change.**  A round that only edited the return value would ship a fix that\n> measures clean on the fast path and still writes through the slow one.\n\n**THE IN-TREE IDIOM EXISTS AND WAS FOUND BY GREPPING, as the option-set rule requires:**\n\n    M88K   rejects writes when accumulated permissions contain PG_RO, then\n           return (accumulated_flags & PG_RO)? 1 : 2;          memory_m88k.c\n    ARM    computes permission from the descriptors and mode, requires access > writeflag,\n           returns that permission, else takes the permission-fault path.  memory_arm.c\n\n*** IT CROSS-CONFIRMED A DIFFERENT OPEN ROW ON THE WAY: [[alphafaultclaim]]. ***  *\"There is no\nimplemented Alpha MM-fault dispatch here.  `ALPHA_KENTRY_MM` and `ALPHA_MMCSR_*` have definitions\nbut no consumers; `kentry` entries are initialized and assigned, never used to dispatch.  The\ncomments claiming the translator or caller 'already signalled' a fault are unsupported by the\nimplementation.\"*  **That is `alphafaultclaim`'s claim, established independently by a seat that was\nnot asked about it.**\n\n**Q5 -- IT IS OBSERVABLE, so the documented-only escape does NOT apply:** the channel is guest RAM\nCONTENTS -- a prohibited write changes bytes that should not change.  *\"'Protection is unmodelled,\nso nothing can observe the error' does not follow: memory contents remain observable.\"*\n\n**Q6 -- ITS OWN SEVERITY WORDING, which is careful and which I am adopting rather than\nparaphrasing:** an *\"Alpha MMU protection-fidelity gap with observable guest-memory correctness\nconsequences\"*, substantial for software relying on page permissions -- and explicitly **NOT** host\nmemory corruption and **NOT** a host security-boundary failure.\n\n*** TWO CAUTIONS ON ITS OWN EVIDENCE, both of which I am recording rather than glossing: ***\n* **It corrected a premise I had inlined from another seat.**  The `DM_EMULATED_RAM` registration is\n  the RAM-MIRROR branch in `dev_ram.c`; base RAM is created through `memory_new` in `core/emul.c`.\n  The conclusion (2 is cacheable) is unaffected, but the premise as I stated it was wrong.\n* **IT CITED THE ALPHA ARCHITECTURE MANUAL BY URL, AND THAT MANUAL IS NOT IN `_scratchpad/`.**  The\n  fifteen PDFs there include no Alpha reference.  So the Table 17-3 citation is **NOT locally\n  verifiable**, and under this project's rule -- born from `#357`/`#362`/`#365` shipping page numbers\n  that came from model output -- it must not be quoted as an established architectural fact.  *The\n  SOURCE findings above stand on their own; only the architectural authority is unverified.*\n\n**A ROUND TAKING THIS OWES AN ALPHA ARCHITECTURE MANUAL**, exactly as `armbfcdecode` owes DDI 0406.\nThat is now TWO rows blocked on absent primary sources."
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32611:   "state_note": "*** WITHDRAWN 2026-09-07, SAME DAY IT WAS FILED: THIS IS A DUPLICATE OF [[queueunrated]], WHICH WAS OPENED EARLIER ON THE SAME DATE AND IS THE BETTER ROW. ***\n\nI filed it after a severity sort of the ledger died on a KeyError, wrote it up with a deriver and a proposed fix, filed it in BOTH OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md and here -- and never checked whether the class was already open.  It was.  `queueunrated` had the same finding with sharper numbers and a better diagnosis.\n\n**WHAT THE ORIGINAL HAS THAT MINE DID NOT:** the count that actually matters is not rows-missing-a-field, it is **73 rows that are UNRATED AND ALREADY WITNESSED** -- rows that could be worked today and that nothing has ranked.  And it names the trap I was walking into: a keyword scan over titles is a CORRELATE, because what makes a row high here is *a guest can reach it and something bad happens*, and no keyword tests reachability.  That is the deriver rule -- key on what DEFINES the class -- applied to triage, and I had not thought of it.\n\n**WHAT MINE ADDED, folded into the original rather than kept here:** the gap is not a closed legacy block.  Rows are STILL being created without the field today, so a backfill alone refills -- which is why the writer-side check must land BEFORE the rating pass, not after.\n\n*** THE REAL FINDING IS THE PROCESS ONE, AND IT IS THE FOURTH INSTANCE IN ONE SESSION. *** Four times running I re-derived something already recorded: the twelve-of-sixteen colour census and the depth-8 uninitialised-byte disclosure (both in COMMITTED codex artifacts under regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/), the `lin_b diag once' marker gap (filed as [[randompixdiagonce]]), and now this.  **This project's unread-review class has always been about PANEL answers nobody opened; these were committed files and open queue rows.**  The cheap defence is one command before filing anything -- grep the ledger ids and OUTSTANDING_BUGS for the subject -- and I did not run it once."
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32622:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 by the main loop, about the main loop.  **FIVE instances in ONE session**, every one\nof them work that was already recorded, and I found none of them by checking -- I found them by\naccidentally tripping over the record afterwards.\n\n    1  \"twelve of sixteen colour encodings reach the random arm\"   already in a COMMITTED artifact,\n                                                                   regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/\n                                                                   codex_design_20260907.txt, with a full table\n    2  \"depth 8 transmits uninitialised stack bytes\"               already in codex_pass1_20260907.txt AND\n                                                                   already an open row, [[sgirebufuninit]]\n    3  \"`lin_b diag once' does not assert the (once) marker\"       already an open row, [[randompixdiagonce]]\n    4  \"half the queue carries no severity\"                        already an open row, [[queueunrated]] --\n                                                                   and I FILED A DUPLICATE, [[ledgersevmissing]],\n                                                                   into the ledger AND OUTSTANDING_BUGS before\n                                                                   noticing.  Withdrawn same day.\n    5  \"the GET half consumes more bytes than it reads\"            already in [[sgirebufuninit]]'s own note,\n                                                                   almost verbatim\n\n**THIS IS THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, BUT NOT THE VERSION THE CARRIER DESCRIBES.**  That class is about\nPANEL ANSWERS NOBODY OPENED -- a seat file sitting in a panel dir.  Every instance here is different and\nworse: the knowledge was in **committed files** and **open queue rows**, i.e. in the two places this\nproject treats as its own memory.  Instance 4 is the sharpest, because the duplicate row PASSED THE\nPRECOMMIT GATE and would have sat in the queue indefinitely as a second entry for one defect.\n\n**THE COST IS NOT ONLY TIME.**  Instance 1 and 2 consumed a codex invocation on questions a committed\nartifact had already answered -- against a standing directive to spend codex on the queue.  And a\nduplicate row inflates the backlog the owner is trying to prioritise, which is the opposite of what\n[[queueunrated]] is for.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES IT A CLASS RATHER THAN CARELESSNESS: THE INVESTIGATION IS WHAT HIDES THE RECORD. ***\nIn every instance I reached the finding by READING SOURCE, and a source reading gives no signal that a\nrow exists.  The record lives under a row id I would only search for if I already suspected the answer.\nInstance 3 is the clean demonstration: I derived the `(once)` gap from the probe, wrote it up as new,\nand the row `randompixdiagonce` had been filed EARLIER THE SAME DAY under a name I would never have\nguessed from the code.\n\n**PROPOSED STRUCTURAL FIX, and it is deliberately two things because they catch different halves:**\n\n* **(a) WRITER-SIDE, mechanical -- a duplicate check on ADDED ledger rows.**  When a commit adds a row,\n  compare its title's content words against every open/in-work row's title and REPORT the nearest\n  matches for a human to dismiss.  It cannot auto-reject (titles are prose and near-misses are real\n  rows), so it warns; the value is that it fires at the moment of filing.  Forward-only from a cutoff,\n  the `check_cited_artifacts` idiom.  **Owes two negative controls -- a genuine near-miss pair must NOT\n  be reported as a duplicate, and an actual duplicate MUST be -- so it ships as its own harness round,\n  not as a rider.**\n* **(b) READER-SIDE, a habit with a command -- before investigating a device or a probe, list what is\n  already open against that FILE.**  This is the half that would have caught instances 1, 2 and 5,\n  which were never going to be caught at filing time because I never filed them.  The command is one\n  line and the artifacts directory is the other half of it:\n\n      grep -l \"<file>\" regress/artifacts/*/*.txt\n      python -c \"...print rows whose title or note names <file>...\"\n\n**HONEST LIMIT: (b) IS PROSE, AND THIS FILE'S OWN FINDING IS THAT PROSE DOES NOT FIRE.**  I am not\nclaiming it will.  It is recorded because (a) genuinely cannot catch instances 1, 2 and 5 -- there is no\nfiling event to hook -- and saying \"no structure reaches this half\" is more useful than pretending a\nhabit is a control.  If a later session finds a mechanical trigger for the reader side, that supersedes\nthis.\n\n*Severity med, not high: nothing shipped wrong.  Every instance was caught, four of them within the\nhour, and the one that reached the ledger was withdrawn the same day.  The cost is wasted effort and an\ninflated backlog, not a defect in the emulator.*\n\n*** A NEIGHBOURING RECORDS CLASS, MEASURED 2026-09-08: A MULTI-ROUND COMMIT LEFT ALL FOUR OF ITS\nROWS WITHOUT COMMIT ATTRIBUTION, AND ONE OF THEM ALSO KEPT A STALE STATE. ***\n\n`7a41b3f` shipped `#505 #506 #507 #508`.  Afterwards:\n\n    sgirergbroundtrip  #505   state STILL `open', commits None, witness None   <- worst\n    lcaconfwidth       #506   commits None\n    alphaswpctxptbr    #507   commits None\n    alphapfnoffset     #508   commits None\n\n**The `commits` gap is a records defect with no measured decision cost yet.**  The `state` gap DID\ncost one: I read `sgirergbroundtrip` as an open med defect while scoping the `sgirandomcolour`\nround in the same file, and began designing around a defect `#505` had already fixed.  *Those are\ndifferent fields and should not be conflated into one severity.*\n\n**HOW BOTH WERE FOUND, and the order matters:** the state gap by ACCIDENT (opening the source while\nscoping something else); the three commit gaps by then asking the general question --\n*\"which non-closed rows are named by a CHANGELOG round-block heading?\"* -- which is a precise query\nbecause the heading format is fixed (`# \\`#NNN\\` <rowid>: <title>`).  It returned **7 candidates,\nof which 4 were legitimate `in work` rows and 3 were real gaps**, so the query needs\nrow-by-row verification and is not a population claim.\n\n**NO CHECKER IS PROPOSED.**  The carrier's escalation discipline applies: the commit-attribution\ngap has consumed zero decisions, and a check that fires on legitimate `in work` rows would be\nswitched off.  *If a stale STATE ever consumes a second decision, that is the trigger to revisit --\nand this note exists so the count is findable.*"
tools\pipeline\ledger.json:32971:   "state_note": "*** A SEAT-FAILURE CLASS NOT ON THE CARRIER'S LIST, MEASURED BY CENSUS 2026-09-08. ***\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.  If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request.  To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program\n\n**It is a PLATFORM content filter, not a model refusal**, and it is the only codex failure mode\nhere that can leave a ZERO-BYTE answer behind a large `.err`.  The carrier lists codex refusal\ntriggers as vocabulary, archive-reading, chain shape and the evasion-construction question; this\nis a different mechanism and it is not listed.\n\n*** THE CENSUS, and it corrects my own first reading of it. ***\n\n    21   .err files carry the flag\n     8   of those left a ZERO-BYTE .txt\n     5   of the 21 produced a FULL answer anyway (14,102 / 18,687 / 12,952 / 8,682 / 6,868 b)\n\n**So the flag is NOT terminal and its PRESENCE proves nothing** -- exactly like the refusal-line\nrule already on record.  `gateworktree`'s 14,102 b assess carries the flag twice and answered in\nfull.  **The `.txt` SIZE is the only discriminator**, which is the rule this project already\nlearned once for refusal lines and had not connected to this filter.\n\n*** THE TRIGGER IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN VOCABULARY, WHICH IS WHY IT KEEPS RECURRING. ***  The row\nids are `sgirekills`, `lcaconfbus1kill`, `lcairqstubkill`, `pcc2c040kill`, `alphavmkill`,\n`asicoffsetkill`; the probe rows are literally named `tlb_a ld kill`, and briefs speak of *death\nrows* and *host kills*.  **A brief that merely NAMES its row carries the trigger.**  The graded\nevidence is in `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/`:\n\n    attempt1.err                       5,906 b   flagged early\n    attempt2_neutralised.err           6,572 b   flagged early\n    attempt3_scoping.err               6,829 b   flagged early\n    attempt4_neutral_late_flag.err   382,773 b   did the WHOLE analysis, flagged at the END\n\n*** THE RECOVERY ALREADY WORKS AND HAS BEEN USED THREE TIMES, AND IT IS WRITTEN DOWN NOWHERE. ***\n\n    sgirekills      codex_rounddesign_20260908       0 b  ->  _RETRY_  6,868 b\n    armbfiwidth     codex_fixdesign_20260907         0 b  ->  20260908 5,091 b, v2 7,308 b\n    sgirebufuninit  codex_pass1_20260907             0 b  ->  _reworded_ 10,557 b\n\n**The filename `codex_reworded_...` IS the technique**, applied by hand three times by whoever hit\nthe wall, and never promoted to a rule -- so the next round rediscovers it.  *That is the carrier's\nown trigger-versus-memory finding: the knowledge existed, on disk, at the moment of the decision,\nand did not fire.*\n\n**STILL UNRECOVERED (codex lost; another seat covered the topic in all but the last):**\n`lcaconfwidth` census (grok 25,281 b), `lcaconfbus1kill` witness (grok 10,679 b), `alphatbinegsel`\nwitness (grok 10,679 b -- grok took it after codex was flagged), and `m88kexcdeliver/pass1/\ncodex_p1_refire` which has a 0-byte `.txt` and **NO `.err` at all**, i.e. by the redirect-target\nrule it never started rather than being flagged.\n\n**A RECORDS GAP FOUND WHILE MEASURING THIS:** `lcaconfwidth` has no brief file on disk at all --\nthe brief was passed inline and not preserved -- so **a flagged job there cannot be re-fired or\naudited without reconstructing its brief.**  A brief that is not preserved is not re-runnable.\n\n**PROPOSED, NOT DONE:** a neutral-vocabulary convention for codex briefs (*process-exit arm*,\n*unreachable-path termination*, *abort site* for *kill*; describe the row rather than naming its\nid), plus preserving every brief beside its answer.  It is a convention change affecting every\nfuture brief, so it goes to the owner rather than being adopted by a round.\n\n*** THE TRIGGER IS NOW MEASURED, NOT INFERRED: A ONE-VARIABLE EXPERIMENT, TWO BRIEFS DIFFERING IN\nONE QUESTION. ***\n\n    IMPLEMENT_brief.md     Q4 = \"what is the smallest edit that would make this look\n                                implemented while still being wrong?\"        ->  0 b, flag x2\n    IMPLEMENT_brief_v2.md  Q4 = \"what observable behaviour distinguishes a correct\n                                implementation from an incomplete one?\"      ->  7,026 b, flag x0\n\nEverything else in the two files is byte-identical -- the diff is that question and nothing else.\n**The answer went from nothing to a full review, and the flag count from two to zero.**\n\n> **SO THE EVASION-CONSTRUCTION FORM IS A TRIGGER AND THE OBSERVABLE-DIFFERENCE FORM IS NOT, AND\n> THE TWO ASK FOR THE SAME ENGINEERING CONTENT.**  The carrier already listed \"the ask-again /\n> evasion-construction question\" among codex's refusal triggers; this measures it, and gives the\n> rewrite rather than only the warning.\n\n**THE REWRITE, which costs nothing and preserves the adversarial value:** ask what OBSERVABLE\ndistinguishes correct from incomplete, and what a regression row must assert so a later partial\nimplementation does not read as complete.  That still forces the seat to think like an adversary\nabout the detector, without asking it to author a disguised defect.\n\n**AND THE NON-TERMINAL HALF GAINED A SIXTH INSTANCE THE SAME DAY:** the `lcaconfbus1kill`\nself-review carried **five** flag hits and still returned **9,710 bytes** of full answer -- the\nlargest flag count on record with a complete answer behind it.  *The flag's presence remains\nevidence of nothing; the `.txt` size is the discriminator.*\n\n**Four briefs written in neutral vocabulary and checked against the trigger list before firing\n(`lcabadval/MANUAL`, `lcabadval/PACKET`, `alphaswpctxreg/PASS1`, `lcaconfbus1kill/SELFREVIEW`)\nreturned 8,657 / n-a / 4,841 / 9,710 bytes.  Zero lost seats since the rule was applied.**"

