ROW lcabadval
{"id": "lcabadval", "state": "open", "sev": "high", "opened": "2026-08-31", "commits": "", "title": "dev_lca.c: SIX chipset registers in DEVICE_ACCESS(lca_ioc) kill the host on a value outside a small accepted set (CONF, TB_ENA, W_BASE0/1, W_MASK0/1) -- plus a seventh exit in the default arm, which this round does NOT close", "note": "*** THE #443 BADVAL CLASS AT SCALE -- THE SAME SHAPE AS `gdromcondlies`, WHICH WAS RATED HIGH AND HAS SINCE BEEN FIXED, AND AS `sgideprimop`, FILED HIGH TONIGHT. ***  **Four registers accept exactly ONE 64-bit value and end the host process on every other**, and two more accept only a single-bit mask.  The author documented the decision rather than hiding it: each `fatal()` reads *\"from the only implemented value\"*.  That is an honest TODO expressed as a host kill.  **A guest here is firmware** -- SRM or NetBSD bringing up the chipset -- so the reaching value is whatever that firmware writes, not an attacker's choice.  A model that implements one value and dies on the rest converts every unmodelled firmware revision into a host death.  *** SCOPE, STATED SO IT IS NOT OVER-CLAIMED: the two RANGE guards (`dev >= 21`, `ofs > len`) are a DIFFERENT question from the four VALUE guards and are not analysed here. ***  A bounds guard that kills may be protecting a real array, in which case the disposition is decline-not-delete, and deciding that needs the array geometry -- which is exactly the check that withdrew `sgideoob` as a false OOB.  Named, not judged.  **SINGLE CONSUMER: `machine_alpha.c:61`, `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.**  There is no alpha rig in this tree, which is why the file has escaped attention -- the same reason `dev_pcc2.c` did.  *No rig means no coverage rather than no bug.*  **COMPANION TO `pcc2widthkills`, AND DELIBERATELY EXAMINED SEPARATELY.**  Both files surfaced from one question -- is `dev_sgi_re.c` uniquely bad or uniquely looked at? -- but they are different classes: pcc2 is WIDTH kills (12 sites, all `len != 1`), this is VALUE kills.  Filing them as one row would have merged two fix designs, and the reopening rule already says a round takes one site unless the SAME fix design covers both.  *A census that finds two files at once is exactly where over-claiming starts.*  *** THIS ROW HAS NO RUNG-3 ATTEMPT RECORDED AT ALL, AND THERE IS A PLAUSIBLE TARGET IT NEVER NAMES.  Found 2026-08-31 by grepping the row for the attempt the 2026-08-27 lifecycle ruling requires. ***  The row reasons from *\"No rig means no coverage rather than no bug\"*, which is true about COVERAGE and is not the same claim as *no machine instantiates this device*.  **`src/machines/machine_alpha.c` calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")` under `case ST_ALPHABOOK1:`, and that subtype is registered with the invocable alias `\"alphabook1\"`** (anchors: `grep -n 'device_add(machine, \"lca\")' src/machines/machine_alpha.c` and `grep -n 'alphabook1' src/machines/machine_alpha.c`).  So the prerequisite a rung-3 probe needs -- a real, committed, UNMODIFIED in-tree machine description that maps the device -- **exists**, and the invocation is `-E alpha -e alphabook1`.  *What is NOT established, and must not be assumed: whether that machine constructs far enough to accept a cold-debugger probe, and whether the four registers are reachable through its decode.  Those are exactly what the ATTEMPT is for.*  **The ruling's point is that the ask costs one invocation and nobody had made it** -- `witness: none` must mean *tried and could not*, never *did not think to*, and those two states must not render the same.  A seat that reports it cannot build one has given a real answer and the row proceeds as documented-only, with the attempt recorded.  *Filed as the next action on this row rather than done here: firing a probe contends with a measuring seat that was running when this was found, and a probe measured under load is the load-sensitive-oracle failure this project has already paid for once.*  *** THE STATED BLOCKER -- \"there is no rig\" -- IS NOT A REASON A RUNG-3 WITNESS IS IMPOSSIBLE, AND THIS PROJECT'S OWN MOST RECENT WITNESS REFUTES IT.  Measured 2026-08-31. ***  `#498`'s rung-3 witness for `sgidewidth` execs `gxemul -V -x -E sgi -e ip32 -M 64 0x80001000:<DUMMY>` and **`DUMMY` falls back to `/dev/null`** when `regress/dummy_kernel.bin` is absent (anchor: `grep -n 'DUMMY = ' regress/sgi_de_width_probe.py`).  **No disk image, no kernel, no boot -- a cold-debugger probe needs a machine that CONSTRUCTS and an empty load file, and that is all.**  Verified by running all four candidates under WSL with `<addr>:/dev/null`: `-E sgi -e ip32`, `-E alpha -e alphabook1`, `-E mvme88k -e mvme187` and `-e mvme188` **all reach `machine:` and print a cpu line**.  *A rig is what a rung-4 BOOT needs.  Conflating the two is what left both of these rows ranked as witness-blocked when neither had been tried.*  **WHAT IS STILL NOT ESTABLISHED, and the attempt is exactly for this:** whether the debugger reaches these registers through the machine's real decode, and whether the observable differs pre- and post-fix.  Constructing is necessary, not sufficient.  *My first run of this test reported all three machines failing -- because I ran the WSL-built ELF from git-bash and got `cannot execute binary file`, while my own grep for `GXemul|machine:` matched the word `gxemul` INSIDE that error and scored it a healthy banner.  A broken apparatus plus a check that matched the error text nearly filed a confident \"these machines do not construct\".*  For this row the target is **`-E alpha -e alphabook1`**, which is the only subtype whose arm calls `device_add(machine, \"lca\")`.  *** PASS-1 PACKET-FED PANEL, 2026-08-31 (`_scratchpad/panel_20260831_053346`).  THIS ROW'S OWN TITLE WAS WRONG, MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF, AND BOTH ARE NOW SETTLED FROM THE SOURCE. ***  **(1) THE CENSUS.  The title said each register *accepts exactly ONE value*; that is true of ONE arm out of four.**  Derived, with the last constant looked up after glm named it as the settling fact:  `W_MASK0` accepts exactly `{0x700000}` -- one.  `W_BASE0` accepts `{0, 0x300800000}` -- **two**.  `IOC_CONF` kills iff `idata & ~1`, so it accepts `{0, 1}` -- **two**, and the predicate is *bit 0 only*, not a constant.  `TB_ENA` kills iff `idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN`, and `alpha_lcareg.h` defines `IOC_TB_ENA_TEN 0x0000000000000080ULL` -- a SINGLE bit, so it accepts `{0, 0x80}` -- **two**.  Title corrected.  *This matters beyond tidiness: the de-escalation rule owes an accept row per predicate, and a detector built from `exactly one value` would pin the wrong accept side on three of the four arms.*  **glm's sharpest point is about WHICH value to pin:** for `W_BASE0` the accepted `0` is the *maximally ordinary* write -- zeroing a window -- so an accept row pinned to the exotic magic constant *misfires on the most legal write rather than an unusual one*.  **(2) MY BRIEF CONTRADICTED ITSELF AND THE SOURCE SETTLES IT AGAINST ME.**  Q2 asserted these fields *feed address-window decoding*; Q3's note said the emulator *does not otherwise model them*.  glm caught that both cannot be true and asked which.  Measured (`grep -n 'window_base_0|window_mask_0|tlb_enable|ioc_conf' src/devices/dev_lca.c`): `window_base_0`, `window_mask_0` and `tlb_enable` are **written and read back and nothing else** -- each appears exactly twice, as `odata = d->X` and `d->X = idata`.  **Only `ioc_conf` is CONSUMED**, at `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1`.  *** SO THE FIX DESIGN SPLITS, AND IT WOULD NOT HAVE WITHOUT THIS CHECK. ***  Three of the four arms are pure BACKED ACCEPT storage -- a decline that keeps the store harms nothing, because no emulator code reads the field.  **The fourth is different**: `ioc_conf`'s low bit selects the bus, and its guard is `idata & ~1`, so a rejected value's bit 0 still reaches the consumer.  glm's *\"a decline that keeps the store is an accept with a complaint, so validate BEFORE storing and leave the previous value\"* is right **for that arm specifically**, and is over-general for the other three.  **(3) `idata` IS `uint64_t`** (`memory_readmax64`), so `0x300800000` is representable and glm's width worry is answered -- the comparison is reachable, not dead code.  **(4) Reachability stays a READING, correctly.**  All three seats declined to say whether these are the only values REAL HARDWARE accepts or merely the only ones this emulator was developed against, and read the code's own wording (`TODO`, *\"differs from the expected values\"*) as supporting the second.  There is no LCA datasheet in `_scratchpad/`.  *That is the honest form and it is flagged as a reading rather than a fact.*\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP MEASUREMENT 2026-08-31 (not a seat answer; recorded here rather than in `entries` so it cannot be counted toward the panel). ***  evidence: dev_lca.c :203 :222 :237 :252 :276 :291 (six guards); :129 inside a comment block\n\n*** THREE WRONG RECORDS ON THIS ROW, found by the rung-3 seat and RE-VERIFIED BY READING dev_lca.c before being written here. ***\n\n(1) SCOPE: there are SIX value-guarded arms, not four.  Verified at dev_lca.c :203 (idata & ~1, IOC_CONF), :222 (idata & ~IOC_TB_ENA_TEN, TB_ENA), :237 (W_BASE0), :252 (W_MASK0), :276 (W_BASE1), :291 (W_MASK1).  OUTSTANDING_BUGS scoped four-plus-two-masks (= the correct six); the LEDGER's pass-1 census picked a DIFFERENT four and silently dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1.  A detector built from the ledger's four ships NO ACCEPT ROW for those two, which the de-escalation clause requires per predicate.\n\n(2) THE EARLIER TITLE CORRECTION IS ITSELF WRONG.  It says the one-value claim is `true of ONE arm out of four'.  Against all six, THREE arms accept exactly one value: W_MASK0 {0x700000}, W_BASE1 {0x240000000}, W_MASK1 {0x3ff00000}.  W_BASE0 accepts two, IOC_CONF {0,1}, TB_ENA {0,0x80}.  **The original title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it** -- this project's own rule that a correction carries more authority than the error and therefore earns a SECOND reading.\n\n(3) hold_question says TWELVE live exit(1).  It is ELEVEN: `grep -c` returns 12, but dev_lca.c:129 is inside a /* ... */ block.  The row's own `witness' field already said eleven, so the row contradicted itself.\n\nCONSUMPTION, confirmed and stronger than filed: five of the six guarded arms are PURE STORAGE (each field appears exactly twice -- odata = d->X and d->X = idata).  `ioc_conf' is the sole consumer, at dev_lca.c:121 `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1'."}
hold_question: The rung-3 seat says a witness IS constructible and specified a full sequence, and it refuted three of this row's own records (six value-guarded arms not four; eleven live exit(1) not twelve; three arms of six accept exactly one value, not one of four -- so the ORIGINAL title was closer to right than the correction that overturned it).  It also named a CONFOUND that would make the probe lie: lca_interrupt_assert and lca_interrupt_deassert are bare fatal()+exit(1) with the whole ISA PIC chain wired to them, so ANY interrupt ends the host with the SAME status as the value guards under test.  THE QUESTION FOR THE FLAGSHIP SEAT: given that every row must therefore match the fatal() TEXT rather than the exit status, and given that lcaconfwidth (filed today) means the IOC_CONF arm's text can PRINT THE WRONG VALUE, is the text-matching oracle sound at THAT arm -- or must lcaconfwidth's printf fix land FIRST, making it a prerequisite of this row rather than a sibling?  Second: the de-escalation clause owes an accept row PER PREDICATE, and the ledger's earlier census dropped W_BASE1 and W_MASK1 -- confirm the accept-row set is six, not four.
