ROW lcaconfwidth
{"id": "lcaconfwidth", "sev": "med", "state": "in work", "opened": "2026-08-31", "title": "a 64-bit guarded value printed through (int) in a KILL diagnostic, so the message can name a value that would NOT have killed -- and at one site names the value the code calls ACCEPTABLE.  A CLASS, censused; derive the population, do not quote it", "note": "*** FILED AS ONE SITE IN dev_lca.c; CENSUSED THE SAME DAY AND IT IS A CLASS.  Widened low -> med. ***\n\nTHE DERIVERS -- run them, do not trust these numbers, and note they answer DIFFERENT questions:\n\n    # (1) the whole cast population -- mostly HARMLESS and NOT the defect\n    grep -rc '(int)idata\\|(int) idata' src/devices/*.c | grep -v ':0$'\n\n    # (2) the sharp sub-class: a cast inside a diagnostic at a site that then EXITS\n    #     (a python window scan; see the round record for the exact form)\n\nOn 2026-08-31 (1) gave 309 casts across 70 device files and (2) gave 45 host-kill sites, about twenty of which carry an explicit full-width guard on idata.\n\n*** MOST OF THE 309 ARE NOT DEFECTS, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT -- an 8- or 16-bit register loses nothing through (int). ***  The defect needs BOTH halves:\n  * a guard that tests the FULL 64-bit value -- `idata & ~MASK' or `idata != CONST'; and\n  * a diagnostic that prints the TRUNCATED one.\nThen a guest write whose only distinguishing bits are above bit 31 dies, while the message reports a value that would have been accepted.\n\n*** THE SHARPEST INSTANCE, VERIFIED BY COMPUTATION AND BY READING THE ARM: dev_pvr.c's `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE && idata != 0x0cff0000)' paired with `(int) idata'. ***  A guest writing 0x1_0cff0000 fails the 64-bit compare and the host dies -- and the message prints 0x0cff0000, **EXACTLY THE VALUE THE CODE SAYS IS ACCEPTABLE**.  So the diagnostic tells a maintainer the guest wrote the correct value and died for it, which sends the investigation in precisely the wrong direction.  dev_lca.c's `idata & ~1' is the same shape, milder: it prints 0x0 for a value whose low half is genuinely zero.\n\nSEVERITY REASONING, stated rather than assumed: none of this changes WHETHER the host dies -- the guards are unchanged and the kills are the kills.  It changes only what the log says about WHY, at the one moment the log is all that survives.  That is why it is med and not high, and why it is a RECORDS-CORRECTNESS defect in product code rather than a control-flow one.\n\nNOT A ONE-LINE SWEEP.  Each site needs its own read: the fix is the format specifier plus a (long long) cast, but which sites are genuinely reachable with a >32-bit datum depends on the register width and the guest paths that reach it, and a blanket edit to 45 diagnostics is exactly the unlicensed addition this project refuses.  The honest first round is the ~20 sites whose guard is demonstrably full-width, ordered by whether a guest can deliver a >32-bit write at all.\n\nFound while checking whether the dev_lca.c site was a one-off, as parallel work during a gate run.", "commits": "7a41b3f"}
hold_question: 
state_note: 

*** CODEX REFUSAL #7, 2026-09-07, ON THE `lcaconfwidth` CENSUS BRIEF -- AND MY OWN LAUNCHER
REPORTED IT AS A CLEAN RUN.  Two defects, both mine, both of the check-that-cannot-fail family. ***

    codex rc=1        stdout 0 bytes        stderr 768,161 bytes
    stderr tail:  ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk.
    tokens used:  108,432

**The seat did 108k tokens of real work first** -- the stderr shows it reading `dev_osiop.c` and
enumerating diagnostic sites -- and the filter discarded the answer at the end.  So this is not a
seat that declined the task; it is a seat whose completed answer was destroyed on the way out.

**LAUNCHER DEFECT 1: the content check read the WRONG STREAM.**  It grepped the `.txt` for refusal
text.  A content-filter refusal writes NOTHING to stdout and puts the ERROR line on STDERR, so the
check printed `refusal hits: 0` **on a refusal**.  A check that looks in the wrong stream returns
the same answer whether or not the thing it tests is true -- which is the exact shape this project
named after `panel.sh`'s phantom re-fire.

**LAUNCHER DEFECT 2: the script exited 0 while holding `rc=1`.**  It captured codex's status into a
variable, printed it, and then ended on a successful command.  **The harness reported the task
COMPLETED.**  That is the failure-laundered-into-a-clean-completion class, and this project has
three prior instances (a `diff` under `set -e`, a Windows path to `wsl`, a truncated preserved log).

> **BOTH FIXED in `cxlaunch.sh`, which greps BOTH STREAMS and exits 7 on a refusal, 8 on a wall, 6
> on an e
