ROW fbfiqsilent
{"id": "fbfiqsilent", "state": "open", "sev": "med", "opened": "2026-08-30", "commits": "", "title": "dev_footbridge.c answers FIQEnable reads with a silent constant 0 while its IRQ twin kills the host -- the whole FIQ column is softer than the IRQ column", "note": "*** THE SIBLING-PARITY CHECK FIRING AT DESIGN TIME, WHICH IS WHERE IT IS SUPPOSED TO FIRE.  Found while adjudicating n6's ordering, 2026-08-30. ***  `n6` is about to ACCEPT the `+0x188` IRQEnable read on the warrant that the DC21285 prose makes it a documented read-only register.  **That same prose covers `188h` and `288h` JOINTLY** -- and `FIQ_ENABLE_SET` at `+0x288` has **no read arm at all**, so the model answers it with a silent constant 0.  A guest that sets FIQ bits and reads them back gets zero: **wrong value, no kill, no announce.**  **IT IS NOT IN THE EIGHT-KILL CENSUS AND THAT IS THE POINT** -- it is the same semantic class with the same one-line fix shape and the same row shape, differing only in that the IRQ column kills where the FIQ column lies quietly.  *Shipping the IRQ accept while leaving its FIQ twin silently wrong and unnamed is exactly the asymmetry the sibling-parity line exists to catch.*  **AND IT IS A COLUMN, NOT A SITE:** `FIQ_ENABLE_CLEAR`'s read side at `+0x28c` is also a silent constant 0 while its IRQ twin kills.  Worth ONE sentence in the decline round's `PRETABLE.out` rather than five separate discoveries later.  Under the reopening rule this becomes a candidate JOINER for a footbridge round once a row witnesses it, and a filed row otherwise.  *** THE DATASHEET SPLITS WHAT THIS ROW TREATS AS ONE CLASS.  Derived 2026-08-30 from `_scratchpad/dc21285.txt`, cross-confirmed in THREE independent places so no extraction stagger is possible: the register summary (`IRQEnable/IRQEnableSet 188h`, `IRQEnableClear 18Ch`), the offset table (`188h  IRQEnable  IRQEnableSet` / `18Ch  --  IRQEnableClear`), and the section heading `7.3.32 IRQEnableSet/FIQEnableSet Register--Offset 188h/288h`. ***  Constants confirmed in `src/include/thirdparty/dc21285reg.h`.  **(a) READ of 0x188 -> ACCEPT.**  *\"IRQEnable/FIQEnable Register--Offset 188h/288h.  This read-only register is used to mask the interrupt input sources... The value of this register can only be changed by writing to the EnableSet and EnableClear registers.  Reset value: 0.\"*  The emulator ALREADY does `odata = d->irq_enable;` -- the documented value -- and then calls `exit(1)` on it.  Delete the kill, KEEP THE VALUE, **no latch**: there is no event to announce, because the read is implemented and is not ignored.  A bounded announce here would ship a wrong record in product code.  **(b) READ of 0x18c -> DECLINE.**  The offset table gives `--` for its read function: there is NO defined read.  The emulator returns `d->irq_enable`, which is a GUESS, then exits.  **Different disposition from its immediate neighbour, and the code treats them identically.**  **(c) WRITE to FIQ_STATUS (0x280) -> DECLINE.**  *\"The Status and RawStatus are read-only.\"*  *** WHY THIS MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING TRIVIA: a single splice across all three would ACCEPT an undefined read at 0x18c and DECLINE a documented one at 0x188 -- getting both neighbours wrong, in OPPOSITE directions. ***  This is the option-set rule paying out again, with the ACCEPT member in play -- and that member is visible only from the datasheet, never from the source.  **AND IT SHARPENS THIS ROW SPECIFICALLY:** the IRQ/FIQ asymmetry recorded here is not merely an inconsistency to be normalised in either direction -- **the datasheet adjudicates which side is right.**  0x188/0x288 is IRQEnable/FIQEnable, a documented read-only register with reset value 0, so answering a FIQEnable read with a constant is closer to correct than killing the host on the IRQ side.  The fix direction is therefore ACCEPT on both, not decline on both.  *** THE TWO-LINE FIX, DATASHEET-BACKED, AND IT REPAIRS A HOST KILL AND A WRONG VALUE AT ONCE. ***  The datasheet says 188h/288h read back IRQEnable/FIQEnable -- the live enable mask, reset value 0.  So:  **IRQ side:** delete the kill and KEEP the value the model already computed (`odata = d->irq_enable`).  **FIQ side:** serve the read too -- `odata = d->fiq_enable` -- instead of falling through with `odata` at 0.  **The FIQ side is not merely 'silent', it is WRONG whenever `fiq_enable != 0`:** a guest that enables FIQ sources and reads the register back is told nothing is enabled.  That is the more insidious half, because unlike its twin it never announces anything.  *Both are member 4, ACCEPT -- no latch, because there is no event to announce: after the fix the reads ARE implemented and are NOT ignored.  A bounded announce would ship a wrong record in product code, which is exactly how the ACCEPT member was discovered.*  **RANKS AS AN EASY RESOLVABLE: two lines, three independent datasheet confirmations, no new latch bit, no struct change, and the accept rows read back through `odata` rather than through debug lines.**  Its only design question is whether 0x18c (IRQEnableClear, which the offset table gives NO read function) joins the same round -- it does not: different disposition, DECLINE not ACCEPT, per the split recorded above."}
hold_question: 
state_note: 

*** codex ASSESS 2026-09-08.  THE ASYMMETRY IS REAL, AND BOTH SIDES ARE ACCEPT. ***

**IRQ_ENABLE_SET read (`+0x188`):**

    odata = d->irq_enable;
    fatal("[ WARNING: footbridge read from ENABLE SET? ]");
    exit(1);

*** IT COMPUTES THE CORRECT VALUE AND THEN ENDS THE HOST. ***  That is member 4 ACCEPT in its purest
form -- delete the kill, keep the value -- and it is the SAME SHAPE this project already found at
`+0x188`'s neighbour, where the datasheet showed a documented read-only register whose answer the
model produced and discarded.

**FIQ_ENABLE_SET (`+0x288`)** returns a silent constant zero.  *"Zero is correct when disabled, but
not for every configured state"* -- so the silent half is a fidelity gap, not a non-defect.

**Both warrant member 4, in SEPARATE rounds.**

*** AND IT REFUTED MY BRIEF'S PREMISE, WHICH IS THE FINDING THAT TRAVELS FURTHEST. ***  I told it
that footbridge being ARM meant FAULT might be available.  **The ARM device-failure arm does not
exist**; `memory_rw.c` has `#ifdef MEM_MIPS` and `#ifdef MEM_M88K` and nothing else.  See the
generalised note now carried on the three `dev_lca.c` rows.

**This row is NOT blocked on a manual**, which is why it was chosen: `_scratchpad/dc21285.txt` is
present, and the same datasheet has already changed one footbridge disposition in this project.
ENTRIES TYPE list
