ROW lcaconfbus1kill SEV high STATE open COMMITS 41923ac
TITLE dev_lca.c: writing 0x1 to LCA_IOC_CONF is ACCEPTED by its guard and arms a host kill in lca_pci_conf -- `bus != 0` is a bare fatal()+exit(1) five lines below the file's own graceful-degradation precedent
state_note 'R.**  The Q2 question is a hardware-behaviour question the packet\ncannot settle, and `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA/21066 datasheet (the `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` list has\nDC21285 but no 21066), so it may not be settleable from this machine at all -- which would make it a\ncandidate for the 2026-09-09 adjudication rather than for'
state_note ', so the code comment and grok agree once the names are\nstraightened.  *The variable is simply misnamed: `bus = d->ioc_conf & 1;` stores a cycle type in\nsomething called `bus`.*\n\n**THE FIX IS THEREFORE TWO ASSIGNMENTS, NOT ONE:**\n\n    } else {\n            bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  type-1: bus is bits 23..16  */\n   '
state_note "E:** that `ioc_conf & 1` is the cycle type\nis a statement about the 21066/LCA, and **`_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet** -- the PDF list has\nDC21285 but nothing for this chipset.  It is consistent with the file's comment and with standard PCI\ntype-0/type-1 addressing, but it is not CHECKED here, and this project's rule"
state_note 'the cycle-type claim is *"a statement about the 21066 ... not\nCHECKED here"* because `_scratchpad/` holds no LCA datasheet.  **codex pointed at\n`_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` -- the REAL OpenBSD LCA driver, 4,925 bytes,\ndated 1997, on disk in this project.**  Verified and read:\n\n    :103   if (bp != NU'
state_note '-worded comment ("bus 0 vs bus non-0 selection bit") was groping at.  **The\nfork\'s variable is misnamed**, and its type-0 handling is correct -- `(1 << (device + 11))` is\nprecisely the IDSEL form the `0x800U << dev` loop scans for.\n\n*** THE ROW IS MASSIVELY STRENGTHENED, AND THIS IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. ***  My witness '
state_note 'real guest driver\n> takes the moment it probes a secondary bus.**  The row previously said "NOT CLAIMED: that any\n> shipped guest issues this sequence."  That caution can now be withdrawn: a shipped guest driver\n> issues exactly this sequence, by construction, for secondary-bus config cycles.\n\n**THE FIX, now backed by the'
state_note 'nely be available here, `memory_rw`\'s\ndevice-failure path has an ARM arm."  It replied that the claimed arm **is absent from this tree**,\nand opening the file confirms it.  **I was one brief away from spreading a false availability claim\nacross the ARM queue**, having just corrected three rows for the Alpha version of it.'
state_note ' EVERYTHING AROUND IT NEEDED CORRECTING --\nINCLUDING TWO PREMISES I STATED AS SETTLED. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, with a denominator.**  `dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f` is correct: `relative_addr >> 5`\nundoes the address expansion, so for a valid tag T and aligned register R,\n`A = (T << 5) | (R << 5) | 24` gives `((A >> 5) & ~0xff) == '
state_note " 6-1, \xa76.4.2, \xa76.2.18) with page anchors.  No 21066/LCA manual was\nin `_scratchpad/`, and there are several open LCA rows reasoning from source alone.  Fetched at the\ncitation, on the `vmesbcp2.pdf` precedent.\n\n*** AND THEN VERIFIED LOCALLY RATHER THAN TRUSTED, which is what the carrier's rule demands and\nwhich this project"
state_note 'ess bits <26:2>"\n                    -> THE SHIFT OF 5 that `relative_addr >> 5` implements.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9216  S6.2.9   "PCI address bits ad<1:0> come directly from configuration cycle type\n                     register (IOC_CONF, Section 6.4.2) bits <1:0>"\n                    -> IOC_CONF selects the CYCLE TYPE and d'
state_note 's <1:0>"\n                    -> IOC_CONF selects the CYCLE TYPE and does NOT supply the bus.  CONFIRMED.\n    :9486  S6.2.18  "the IOC returns arbitrary data with error status to the CPU, which causes\n                     an instruction exception" + internal interrupt + ERR/CODE=1/CMD/ADDR/LOST\n                    -> absen'
state_note '/CMD/ADDR/LOST\n                    -> absent-device reads are NOT all-ones on real hardware.  CONFIRMED.\n\n**One narrowing, stated because a wrong reason gets reused:** codex wrote *"bits 23..2 from CPU\naddress bits 28..7"*.  The manual\'s general sparse rule is the WIDER `<31:7> -> <26:2>` with the\nsame shift; codex\'s rang'
