  72: 
  73: R and X's conditional selftests have the same version problem as V: staged/unstaged/untracked names decide whether to run, but the filesystem checker is exercised.
  74: 
  75: For the original nine-of-ten plan, D would inspect the omitted tracked edit's additions rather than the nine staged diffs. A/B would still use the correct candidate source bytes **if their selected source files were fully staged and remained unchanged afterward**.
  76: 
  77: **Q3. J is affected, but your description of the two files needs correcting.** The project-root originals are outside the Git repository. Only their `GXEMUL-SEC/tools/` copies belong to this index.
  78: 
  79: [J's decisive line](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:728) is exactly:
  80: 
  81: ```bash
  82: elif cmp -s "$ROOT/$_c" "$SEC/tools/$_c"; then
  83: ```
  84: 
  85: This covers both `CLAUDE.md` and `PIPELINE.md`.
  86: 
  87: Updating the external original and its working copy, while omitting the copy's update from staging, lets J pass while the commit retains the old carrier. That is the failure you are concerned about.
  88: 
  89: Also, J's "tracked copy" existence test is merely `-f`; it does not verify index membership.
  90: 
  91: **Q4. A correct conversion needs an explicit choice of snapshot.** For a fix that continues accepting intentional partial staging:
  92: 
  93: - **Index mode:** repository membership, contents and checker selftests come from the candidate index. D uses `git diff --cached -U0`.
  94: - **Working-tree preview:** preserve the useful before-staging audit. D uses ordinary `git diff -U0`.
  95: - Keep deliberately external inputs-carrier originals, build trees, panels-and historical Git queries explicitly separate.
  96: 
  97: Choose and announce the mode **before the first pipeline invocation**, not at today's late `CHANGED` assignment.
  98: 
  99: "Nothing staged" normally means the index equals HEAD, not that the index contains no files. Blindly replacing reads with `git show ":$path"` would therefore inspect old versions during the common before-staging run. Preserve that run as a preview; commit approval requires checking the subsequently prepared index.
 100: 
 101: Given the helpers' filesystem interfaces, a complete isolated index snapshot is a practical implementation approach. It must include unchanged dependencies and preserve access to real Git history and external project paths. The helpers derive paths from `__file__`, so simply redirecting the shell's `ROOT` is insufficient.
 102: 
 103: Thus conversion costs more than changing one flag: enumeration, existence checks, helper paths, tested checker versions and failed blob reads all need consistent handling. Byte comparisons also require deliberate treatment of Git filters/newline conversion; locally, the inspected carrier copies use LF and `core.autocrlf` is false.
 104: 
 105: **Q5. The agreement guard is useful, but insufficient alone.** Its conservative core is:
 106: 
 107: ```bash
 108: git diff --quiet --exit-code --
 109: ```
 110: 
 111: In staged mode, run it early and treat differences or Git errors as a hard refusal. Checking only `$CHANGED` would miss unstaged dependencies that the gate nevertheless reads. A repository-wide tracked-file guard catches those conservatively.
 112: 
 113: The read-only check restricted to your excluded probe returned **exit status 1**. This guard has a live failure condition here; it would refuse that intentional exclusion once staged-mode enforcement applies.
 114: 
 115: Two distinctions matter:
 116: 
 117: 1. **Passing after staging everything is correct for an agreement check.** Agreement plus a valid content check can establish the indexed predicate. The content check must still reject invalid matching versions.
 118: 2. **D currently breaks that argument.** When the agreement guard passes, ordinary `git diff` is empty. Guard-only mitigation would leave D unable to inspect any staged additions. Changing D's diff source is mandatory.
 119: 
 120: A tracked-file guard also does not cover untracked inputs admitted by filesystem enumeration, guarantee literal byte equality under Git filters, or protect against inputs changing during the run.
 121: 
 122: Therefore: **guard plus the D correction is a useful conservative interim repair for tracked-content mismatch.** Full candidate validation additionally needs controlled input membership and stable inputs. To accept your intentional partial staging, use the indexed snapshot instead of refusing the discrepancy.
 123: 
 124: **Q6. High-priority gate-integrity defect; the workflow is ordinary, and a shipped inconsistency is documented.** The permitted [probe-checker source](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py:169) explicitly says:
 125: 
 126: > a tracked probe with no entry is UNCLASSIFIED . `2956c7e` shipped the second one - see `[[gateworktree]]`.
 127: 
 128: Read-only historical inspection confirmed that commit contains `regress/sgi_mte_fill_probe.py`, while its `check_probe_wiring.py` contains **zero manifest entries** for that file. That establishes an actual shipped invariant violation. I did not verify historical gate output.
 129: 
 130: The [main script's own history](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/tools/precommit_check.sh:868) also records selective staging during concurrent work as the reason for its staged-name selection.
 131: 
 132: The general defect produces a false green when an omitted or partially staged change supplies something a check needs. **Every subset commit is not necessarily invalid.** D's coverage failure is broader: fully staging tracked edits always leaves it no additions to inspect.
 133: 
 134: No gate/selftests were executed and no files were changed. The index changed during inspection, so this is not approval of its current contents. One malformed search accidentally returned excluded ledger text; the findings above rely on harness source and Git metadata.
