    #513 sgirekills: six host kills removed from dev_sgi_re read arms, and the
    detector that shipped with them survived four adversarial passes
    
    src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c: the six `fatal("TODO: read from CRIME_RE_*"); exit(1);`
    read arms are gone.  FIVE become ACCEPT (option-set member 4): the model already
    holds the answer in re_tlb_a/b/c and the shadow arrays, and the arms discarded it
    and killed the host.  Each now composes its halfwords into `odata` and falls
    through to the common tail -- NOT into `data[]`, which the tail
    `if (writeflag == MEM_READ) memory_writemax64(cpu, data, len, odata);` would
    overwrite.  TEX becomes a latched DECLINE (member 3) on a new SGIRE_W_TEX_RD
    latch bit, because nothing computes a TEX value to accept.
    
    regress/sgi_re_width_probe.py: 35 -> 46 rows.  Ten ANCHORED accept rows, two per
    window at its BASE and its LAST legal slot -- clause (f) plus the far-bound rule,
    since a row at the base tests the near bound twice and the far bound never.  Four
    TEX rows, two latch-independence rows (tex/unmap, unmap/tex), and a live64
    control.
    
    *** THE ROW THAT SCORED 46/46 WHILE MEASURING NOTHING. ***  `tex xbit` called
    store(A, DE_MODE_DST, MODE_LIN_B) against a signature of store(addr, op=, val=),
    so the VALUE was passed as the OPCODE -- six calls, all wrong, and the row was
    green under every mutant because it was green under everything.  No kill table
    could see it: a row that does nothing dies to nothing.  codex found it by
    READING.  The row is now SELF-CHECKING -- it counts how many of its own setup
    literals actually appeared and fails if the count is not the full set, so a
    future miswiring reds the row instead of silently passing.
    
    KILL TABLES (scratch clone, GXEMUL-SEC never written):
      regress/artifacts/sgirekills/killtable_20260908/
        TABLE1.txt        12 mutants
        L2VERIFY.txt      BASE 46/46, M13 44/46
        CLASSVERIFY.txt   CLASSVERIFY3.txt
        SWEEP11.txt       all 11 alias cells KILLED on ONE probe version
    
    The class-closure amendment is what ended the chain.  Passes 1-3 each found one
    more cell of the same class -- width, then component, then direction/position --
    and cell-by-cell repair converges linearly.  Closing the whole enumeration in one
    diff and VERIFYING the enumeration is what made pass 4 come back with zero.
    SWEEP11 is the closing measurement, and it honours kimi's sharpening: every cell
    killed on ONE probe version, not nine kills carried across a probe edit.
    
    The panel was right three times and I was wrong three times -- the six-row design,
    the "#392 unbounded" strawman, and carrying nine kills across a probe edit.
    
    L4 RECORD CORRECTIONS, in place: the enum next-free claim demoted (it named a
    value the enum no longer leaves free), and the LINEAR alignment attribution
    corrected at BOTH arms rather than one -- mechanical rule 2, grep for the
    siblings before calling it fixed.
    
    Also in this commit, and separable from the fix:
      * OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md gains `sgidefatalstale` -- the de-fatalisation rounds left
        prose DESCRIBING host kills that no longer exist.  LOW, filed, not fixed.
      * `alphatbinegsel`'s assess stage: seven seats answered, opus5 and fable5 are
        HELD.  One marker of mine was written WITHOUT its leading bracket, so
        check_stage_panels could not see it and the fable5 entry fell through into the
        seat tally as an ANSWER.  A hold counted as agreement is the shape this
        project exists to refuse; the marker is repaired and the population of that
        defect is measured at exactly one.
      * the same row's Q5 tally was written from memory as "3-1 in favour" and reads
        2-3 against.  Corrected from the five answers before shipping.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

