   2: 
   3: **Q1 - completeness.** REASONED: within the defined operator (one constant on one edited line), the cells are exactly the other used bits of that word, and the M_bit9/M_bit10 kills themselves corroborate that bits 6-10 live in the same word the TEX latch consults - firing the DE path suppressed the aliased TEX announce, which is only possible co-resident. That is good internal evidence. NOT CHECKABLE from the packet, and it is THE enumeration premise: **"twelve bits in use, 0 through 11."** Settling fact: one grep of the `SGIRE_W_*` values / all `d->warned` set-and-test sites. Also not checkable: whether some used bit is set only from a path no guest session drives (the exact 9/10 lesson). On Q1's "second field": READ - DE is a separate device (`DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`), but REASONED - a second *word* is not a cell for this operator, since aliasing is intra-word; the right worry is the used-set of this one word, above.
   4: 
   5: **Q2 - can `tex xbit` pass while the latch is wrong?** REASONED: inside the class, no. The clean BASE_fixed wanting "1/11" and scoring 46/46 is a *measured* proof that all eleven setup announces fire on the clean device; any alias i ? 0..10 then yields count 0  "0/11"  red, and the mutant being a single-line constant means setup-firing transfers. Outside the class, yes: xbit has exactly one TEX load, so once-ness is entirely `tex rd lat1`'s job; an announce-but-never-latch mutant on bits 0-10 passes xbit (presence, not count) - out of class, but say it. And an alias onto a used bit �12 passes everything and stays in-class. That is Hole A again - the row structure cannot compensate for an under-enumerated word.
