1871: ### 7g-6. (MED) *** THE GATED-ROW FORM, AFTER PASS 2: THREE SEATS SPLIT, AND THE ONLY IN-TREE
1872: ### PRECEDENT TURNS OUT TO GATE ON SOMETHING ELSE. ***
1873: 
1874: 7g-2 asked this from pass 1 with no evidence behind it. Pass 2 supplied the evidence and the
1875: split, so it is restated here with both, and 7g-2 should be read as superseded by this.
1876: 
1877: **THE FORM.** Three rows in `#509`'s detector -- `fill bot`, `copy 2nd`, `copy 4th` -- read bytes
1878: that are HOST STACK before the fix and DEFINED after it. Each is gated:
1879: 
1880:     fill_top_ok = (fill session's dest[0] == 0x11)
1881:     row("fill bot", byte_n(fullword, 3) if fill_top_ok else "n/a: ...", "0x44", ...)
1882: 
1883: Pre-fix the row reports `n/a` and **FAILS deterministically, naming its reason**, instead of
1884: scoring an undefined byte. The alternative is to drop all three -- which measurably re-opens two
1885: total escapes (`dst_mode |= RGB` and `src_mode |= RGB`, M5 and M8, both 11/13 and 12/13 only
1886: because these rows exist).
1887: 
1888: **THE SPLIT, and it is not a tally -- the arguments differ in kind:**
1889: 
1890: * **grok: SHIP `fill bot`, KEEP `copy 4th`, and write the coupling on the row.** Its reason is
1891:   precise: `fill bot` gates on **the same `lw` it then scores**, so its coupling is local. The
1892:   copy rows gate on a **different session**, which is a weaker thing, and it says so rather than
1893:   lumping them together. *"Fail-when-closed is a derived red, and that is acceptable HERE because
1894:   the n/a names the reason and the row\'s job is the RGB cell, not the pre-fix leak."*
1895: * **codex: KEEP all three, with truthful dependency diagnostics.** *"Their pre-fix failures need
1896:   not be independent defect witnesses to make useful post-fix assertions. A failed prerequisite
1897:   should still prevent an overall pass."* It adds that the gate must be renamed `fill_top_ok`,
1898:   because `dest[0] == 0x11` **admits RGB as well as RGBA** -- so the current name overclaims.
1899: * **deepseek: NOT RATIFY -- fix the gate per-path or drop the rows.** *** ITS REMEDY IS REJECTED
1900:   ON MEASUREMENT, NOT ON A VOTE: *** it proposes gating the copy rows on `copy dest[0] == 0x1a`.
1901:   grok's byte-walk shows that is VACUOUS -- `0x1a` is what **both** CI-CI and RGBA-RGBA produce,
1902:   so the gate would always be open, including pre-fix, and the rows would then score exactly the
1903:   host stack the gate exists to avoid. *A proposed edit is a sketch until it is run against the
1904:   real control flow.*
1905: 
1906: **WHAT I DID:** shipped all three, renamed the flag, corrected the message to name the FILL, and
1907: wrote both measured limits into the probe. **The disposition is mine and it is the one that costs
1908: least work, which is why it wants an adjudication.**
1909: 
1910: > #### THE PRECEDENT QUESTION, and pass 2 changed the answer to it
1911: >
1912: > Pass 1 recorded "no precedent found in this tree". **That was wrong in a way that cuts both
1913: > ways.** `pcc2_default_probe.py:174-178` DOES use the `n/a`-and-fail shape --
1914: > `t.count(MSG) if alive else "n/a"` -- so a row that refuses to score and names its reason is
1915: > already house style. **But it gates on SURVIVAL of its own session.** codex and grok both drew
1916: > the line in the same place: that establishes the *n/a idiom*, and it does **not** establish a
1917: > **cross-session dependency**, which is what `copy 2nd` and `copy 4th` actually do.
1918: >
1919: > So the genuinely new thing is narrower than pass 1 thought and still real.
1920: 
1921: **THE TWO MEASURED LIMITS, both from grok, both now written into the probe rather than glossed:**
