1963:     `1U' -> `1' in the mask ternary                     re-opens C99 6.5.7p4 at width 31
1964:     restore `topBitMask = 1 << (width-1)' above the if  re-opens the second UB
1965: 
1966: **Neither is killable by any row, and the reason is structural rather than a thin row set.** Both
1967: are DEFINEDNESS repairs, and definedness has no guest-visible value on a non-trapping host:
1968: `1 << 31` and `1U << 31` yield the same mask after the `- 1`, and with a correct 32-bit mask
1969: `x |= ~mask` is `x |= 0`. *This also means `s w32` pins the MASK and not `topBitMask` \u2014 the probe
1970: comment claimed otherwise and is corrected.*
1971: 
1972: > **BUT THE TREE ALREADY HAS THE VEHICLE.** `build-asan` carries `-fsanitize=address,undefined`,
1973: > and `regress/gate_asan_sweep.sh` exists. A UBSAN DIFFERENTIAL pins both \u2014 fixed source produces
1974: > no shift diagnostic, the `1U`->`1` mutant produces one \u2014 and it would cover the whole class:
1975: > `lcashiftub`, `sgirebufshiftub`, `m88k_rot:513`, `cpu_mips_instr.c:1757` and the two
1976: > `is_userpage` sites. **Eight sites across three CPUs, none visible to a value oracle.**
1977: 
1978: **I DID NOT BUILD IT, and the reason is the question.** It is a NEW MEASUREMENT LANE \u2014 a second
1979: binary and a STDERR oracle instead of a value oracle \u2014 and this round's pass 2 had already run.
1980: **A lane added after a pass is a lane nobody reviewed.** So I shipped with the escape disclosed in
1981: the probe header and filed the lane as `ubsanshiftlane`.
1982: 
1983: > **The question, stated so it generalises past this round: when a floor-relevant escape is
1984: > unkillable by the round's instrument BUT killable by a lane the tree already has, is disclosing
1985: > it sufficient, or does the round owe the lane in-round?**
1986: >
1987: > The pull is real in both directions. *Owing it in-round* means every round that touches UB
1988: > acquires a second measurement lane and a second pass \u2014 which is the `#392` shape. *Disclosing*
1989: > means a clause-(d) escape ships with a full score beside it, and this project's own vacuity
1990: > floor exists because a full score beside a live escape is exactly what it refuses.
1991: >
1992: > **My disposition was DISCLOSE, and it is the one that costs less work, which is why it wants
1993: > adjudication rather than my say-so.**
1994: 
1995: ---
1996: 
1997: ### 7h-2. (MED) IS LEAVING `bfi` A CORRECT ONE-SITE SCOPING, OR A PARTIAL REPAIR? \u2014 kimi's challenge
1998: 
1999: `#510` fixed `ubfx` and `sbfx` and **left `bfi` alone**, on the ground that its
2000: `width = msb - lsb + 1` spans **-30..32** (465 pairs strictly negative, 31 giving width 0) and
2001: that ARM calls `msb < lsb` UNPREDICTABLE, with the fork answering that class inconsistently.
2002: 
2003: **The kimi substitute refused that rationale as too broad, and it is right:** `bfi`'s
2004: **`msb = 31, lsb = 0` cell is architecturally DEFINED**, gives width 32, and is **the same
2005: `(1 << 32) - 1` defect this round just fixed twice** \u2014 the same ternary would fix it. So the
2006: UNPREDICTABLE argument covers `msb < lsb` and does **not** cover bfi's width-32 cell.
2007: 
2008: grok, independently, called leaving bfi *"a correct use of the one-site rule, not a partial repair
2009: sold as complete"* \u2014 while agreeing the legal cell is the same defect.
2010: 
2011: > **So: does the one-site rule permit leaving a KNOWN, DEFINED, IDENTICAL-EXPRESSION cell unfixed
2012: > because its NEIGHBOURING cells raise a design question?** Or does "same defect, same file, same
2013: > one-token fix" oblige the round to take it, and confine the scoping argument to the
2014: > UNPREDICTABLE half?
2015: >
2016: > *Note the witness does not cover bfi either way \u2014 there is no bfi row \u2014 so taking it would also
2017: > have owed a row.*
2018: 
2019: ---
2020: 
2021: ### 7h-3. (MED) WHAT SHOULD `bfi` DO WHEN `msb < lsb`?  *(the row's `hold_question`)*
2022: 
2023: 496 encodings reach it. ARM says UNPREDICTABLE. **No seat could settle it from the tree**, and a
2024: codex pass examined 13 comparable decisions to say so: no-op has bitfield precedent, conditional
2025: UND has decoder precedent, `cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:204` picks another defined result for an
2026: UNPREDICTABLE halfword load, and **`#312`'s own comment argues AGAINST halting**.
2027: 
2028: *** AND THE OBVIOUS ROUTE IS WRONG AS WRITTEN: `X(und)` expects `arg[0]` to hold a PC OFFSET, and
2029: `bfi`'s `arg[0]` is a REGISTER POINTER. *** Routing bfi's unchanged call record there would hand a
2030: pointer to code that reads an offset.
2031: 
2032: 
2033: ---

LINES 2155 2181
2155: ## 7k. `m88kexcdetectorl2` \u2014 a MEASURED L2, and the repair's cost is the question
2156: 
2157: *agy measurement: BASELINE 59/59, MUTANT `implemented = (ncpus < 2)` 59/59, zero rows reddened
2158: (`regress/artifacts/m88kexcdetectorl2/agy_measure_20260907.txt`).*
2159: 
2160: The clean sweep is **explained**: `m88k_exc_probe.py` never passes `-n`, so every row runs at one
2161: CPU and the mutant's condition is true throughout. The repair is one session at `-n 2` asserting the
2162: exception is still delivered \u2014 **de-risked by reading**: `-n` is validated to 1..256, the CPUs are
2163: created in the generic path (so bare `-E testm88k` gets them), and `bootstrap_cpu` is deterministic
2164: because `machine_test.c` never sets `use_random_bootstrap_cpu`. That last one would have been a
2165: `random() % 2` flake.
2166: 
2167: ### 7k-1. (MED) HOW MUCH RE-MEASUREMENT DOES ONE ADDED ROW OWE? *(the row's `hold_question`)*
2168: 
2169: This project's standing caution is that added rows perturb the scores of every mutant already
2170: measured. **Does adding one row to a 59-row detector oblige a full re-table, or is a targeted run \u2014
2171: baseline plus the mutants whose kill sets could plausibly move \u2014 sufficient?** The difference is a
2172: full table versus a few runs, and the answer governs every future L2 repair, not just this one.
2173: 
2174: *Also worth a line: the row said the mutant applies to SEVEN arms; agy derived ELEVEN and explained
2175: the gap \u2014 the "seven" counted only arms carrying a `/* m88kexcdeliver */` annotation, silently
2176: excluding four pre-existing vectors in the same switch. A count keyed on an annotation rather than
2177: on what defines the class.*
2178: 
2179: 
2180: ---
2181: 

LINES 2182 2237
2182: ## 7l. `lcaconfbus1kill` \u2014 the fix is SETTLED; the DETECTOR is not, and four seats agree it cannot be
2183: 
2184: *Pass-1 fix panel: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/pass1_panel/` \u2014 six seats (codex, grok, glm,
2185: deepseek, minimax, the kimi-k3:cloud substitute). No flagship seat, by the standing directive
2186: reserving opus5/fable5 for this adjudication.*
2187: 
2188: **THE DEFECT IS MEASURED** (`regress/lca_confbus_probe.py`, 3/4, the RED row being the defect): an
2189: **accepted** `stq 0x1` to `LCA_IOC_CONF` arms a bare `fatal(); exit(1)` that the next PCI config
2190: access reaches.
2191: 
2192: **THE FIX IS SETTLED AND CONFIRMED AGAINST A PRIMARY SOURCE.** Not from the fork's comment but from
2193: `_guest-src-openbsd22/sys/arch/alpha/pci/lca_pci.c` \u2014 the real OpenBSD LCA driver, on this machine:
2194: 
2195:     } else {
2196:             bus = (tag >> 16) & 0xff;    /*  lca_decompose_tag:103  */
2197:             dev = (tag >> 11) & 0x1f;    /*  lca_decompose_tag:106  */
2198:     }
2199: 
2200: and `lca_conf_read:127` writes `REGVAL(LCA_IOC_CONF) = 0x01` on the secondary path, proving the
2201: register selects the configuration **format**, not a bus number. **The same driver shows my witness's
2202: two accesses are its own secondary-bus sequence, line for line** \u2014 so the row's earlier caution
2203: ("not claimed that any shipped guest issues this") is withdrawn.
2204: 
2205: ### 7l-1. (HIGH) SHIP A FIX WHOSE DETECTOR PROVABLY CANNOT DISCRIMINATE IT?  *(the `hold_question`)*
2206: 
2207: Four seats reached this independently, by different routes:
2208: 
2209: | seat | how it put the wall |
2210: |---|---|
2211: | grok | *"the witness address is not a type-1 tag, and empty-slot data cannot pin the extract"* |
2212: | glm | it *"conflates IMPLEMENT with explicit-all-ones DECLINE, silent with latched, and can false-pass on stale all-ones unless the destination register is pre-seeded"* |
2213: | minimax, kimi(sub) | only a **populated bus-1 device** could separate them |
2214: 
2215: **An empty bus 1 answers all-ones whether the model implements type-1 or merely fakes the reply**,
2216: and nothing in this tree places a device on bus 1. So:
2217: 
2218: > **(a)** ship the fix and DISCLOSE the non-discriminating detector under clause (e); or
2219: > **(b)** ADD a synthetic bus-1 device to the apparatus so the row becomes possible.
2220: >
2221: > **(b) is an addition to the test apparatus made in order to observe a fix** \u2014 which is precisely
2222: > the shape the addition licence exists to police, and it is not obviously refused by it either,
2223: > since the licence governs PRODUCT additions. **That boundary is the question.**
2224: 
2225: *glm's pre-seeding point is the one improvement available under (a) and should be taken either way:
2226: seed the destination register so a stale all-ones cannot false-pass.*
2227: 
2228: ### 7l-2. (MED) A CARRIER LINE FOR `guestsrcunknown` \u2014 proposed, not done
2229: 
2230: `_guest-src-openbsd22/` holds **8,138 `.c` files** covering `alpha`, `arc`, `arm32`, `mvme88k`,
2231: `pmax` and `powerpc` \u2014 six of this fork's own targets \u2014 at the project root, untracked, and
2232: **mentioned nowhere in the carrier or the harness**. I declared a chipset question unanswerable from
2233: this machine one paragraph before a seat answered it from that tree.
2234: 
2235: The carrier already says to `ls _scratchpad/*.pdf` before reasoning from first principles about a
