regress/artifacts/sgirekills/codex_rounddesign_20260908.err:2451:tools/pipeline/ledger.json:30542:   "state_note": "*** MY OWN DIAGNOSIS IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE IT CHANGES\nTHE REMEDY. ***  I wrote that the trigger is 'the SUBJECT SHAPE, not a phrase', on the evidence of two\nrefusals of one brief.  **codex then answered a brief on THE SAME DEFECT, in the same file, at 167 KB.**\n\nThe difference is the FRAMING, and it is sharper than either earlier guess:\n  REFUSED  the WITNESS brief -- 'can you build a probe that REACHES this and shows the bad value?'\n  ANSWERED the DESIGN brief -- 'what should this code do instead, and what should the rows assert?'\nSame defect, same file, same session.  A request to CONSTRUCT A PATH TO A DEFECT reads as an attack recipe;\na request to DECIDE WHAT THE CODE SHOULD DO reads as ordinary review.  Neutralising the vocabulary did not\nhelp because the vocabulary was never the trigger.\n\n**SO THE REMEDY IS NOT ONLY RE-ROUTING: it is to ask codex the DESIGN question and let another seat answer\nthe REACHABILITY one.**  That is cheaper than it sounds -- grok answered the witness brief with a complete\nbuildable sketch, and codex then produced the best design answer of the panel.  Each seat was asked the\nquestion it would answer.\n\n*** THE SEAT-CHECK HAZARD IS UNCHANGED AND IS THE PART THAT WAS ACTUALLY FIXED. ***  The refusal writes a\nzero-byte stdout with the reason on stderr on a direct call -- indistinguishable from an agy quota wall --\nand through panel.sh it produced 150,372 bytes at 97% novel, which `seat_verdicts.py` scored as **answered,\nNO VERDICT LINE**.  A seat failure counted as an answer, in the tool built to refuse exactly that.  Every\nexisting test passed it: no quota keyword (not a wall), high novelty because the seat streams reasoning\nBEFORE the filter fires (not an echo), CLI seat (not a burn).  Now reported as **SEAT REFUSED**, counted with\nthe DOWN seats, keyed on the vendor's own sentence, and controlled in four directions: it fires on the\nrefusal, leaves a genuine quota wall as WALLED, leaves the mid-answer wall's READ-THE-BODY label intact, and\ndoes not touch answering seats.\n\nBoth refusal transcripts and the answered design brief are preserved beside the round's artifacts, so the\nbefore/after comparison is checkable rather than remembered.\n\n*** THIRD INSTANCE, 2026-09-07 -- AND IT REFUTES THE DIAGNOSIS THIS ROW CURRENTLY CARRIES. ***\n\nPreserved: `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/attempt3_scoping.err'.  Exact text, twice:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: https://chatgpt.com/cyber\n\n**stdout was 0 bytes; the banner, the echoed prompt and both ERROR lines went to stderr** -- so a\ncheck that measures the `.txt' alone sees an empty file and a check that measures SIZE sees 6829\nbytes of healthy-looking stderr.  `seat_verdicts.py' already reports this as SEAT REFUSED / DOWN\n(fixed earlier in this session); this instance confirms the detector on a fresh case.\n\n*** WHAT IS REFUTED: this row concluded the trigger is the reach-the-defect FRAMING -- *design\nbriefs are answered, witness briefs are refused*.  THE REFUSED BRIEF HERE IS NEITHER. ***  It is a\nread-only SCOPING task that explicitly says *\"Do not design the fix diff.  Do not write rows\"*.\n\n**AND THE CONTROL IS STRONG, because it ran the same evening on the same model at the same effort:**\nthe `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 brief -- which discusses uninitialised host stack reaching guest-visible\nmemory, i.e. a MEMORY-DISCLOSURE defect -- was ANSWERED at 317 KB with a GO-WITH-CHANGES verdict and\ntwo new findings.  So the trigger is not the subject matter, not the security-adjacent content, and\nnot the brief's purpose.\n\n**THE REMAINING HYPOTHESIS, stated as a hypothesis because it is not yet tested: VOCABULARY DENSITY.**\nThe refused brief is saturated with this fork's historical term for the defect -- *kills*, *THE HOST\nSTILL DIES*, *terminating calls*, *host kill* -- while the answered brief describes an equivalent\nseverity in ordinary engineering words.  A re-fire is in flight with the identical technical content\nrewritten in bounds-and-error-handling language (`RESEARCH_scoping_neutral_20260907.md'): same\nquestions, same code, same seven dispositions, no `kill'/`dies'/`host' vocabulary.\n\n**That is a real experiment with a clean control and it settles the hypothesis either way** -- if the\nneutral form answers, the trigger is vocabulary and the remedy is the benign-framing rule the carrier\nalready mandates for the Anthropic seats, applied to codex too; if it also refuses, vocabulary is out\nand the next candidate is brief LENGTH or the enumerate-the-sites structure.\n\n*Note the cost is real and recurring: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, and a refusal is\ninvisible to any size-based check.*\n\n*** SOLVED, WITH A CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT.  THE TRIGGER IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN ARCHIVES, NOT THE BRIEF'S\nSUBJECT AND NOT ITS FRAMING.  Measured 2026-09-07 across four runs. ***\n\n    attempt  brief vocabulary   reading scope        stderr     flag\n    1        kills / HOST DIES  unrestricted           6,829 b  IMMEDIATE, before any work\n    3        neutral            unrestricted         374,583 b  at byte 279,268 (~75% in)\n    4        neutral            src/ + regress/*.py  279,132 b  *** NONE -- full verdict delivered ***\n    (control) pass-2 panel      unrestricted         395,340 b  at the END of the answer\n\n**TWO SEPARATE FILTERS, and each attempt isolates one:**\n* **Attempt 1 -> 3 shows a PROMPT-level filter keyed on vocabulary.**  Rewriting *kills / the host dies /\n  terminating calls* into ordinary bounds-and-error-handling language converted an instant refusal into a run\n  that worked for minutes.  The technical content was IDENTICAL -- same twelve sites, same expectation design,\n  same seven dispositions.\n* **Attempt 3 -> 4 shows a CONTENT-level filter keyed on what the seat READS.**  Adding one paragraph -- *do\n  not read `regress/artifacts/' or `tools/pipeline/ledger.json'* -- carried the run past the exact point where\n  the unrestricted one died, to a clean finish.  **279,132 vs 279,268 bytes: the same distance, opposite\n  outcome.**\n\n**WHY THE ARCHIVES: they are this project's own record of a hardening campaign** -- kill tables, `host kill'\ncensuses, `exit(1)' enumerations, mutant names like `widthkillclass' -- accumulated over months.  Read in bulk\nthey look like exactly what the filter exists to catch.  *The irony is that the archive is the honest record\nof DEFENSIVE work, and its own vocabulary is what makes the seat refuse to read it.*\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE, and it costs two sentences in a brief: for any codex run, (a) write the brief in\n> ordinary engineering language, and (b) SCOPE ITS READING to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.**  The pass-2 control\n> shows why (b) matters even when (a) is satisfied: that brief was already neutral and was still flagged, at\n> the end, after reading the ledger and CHANGELOG.\n\nThis matters more than one lost answer: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, a refusal writes 0\nbytes to stdout while leaving healthy-looking stderr, and it is invisible to every size-based check.\n\n*** SIXTH RUN, AND IT REFUTES THE REMEDY I SHIPPED TWO HOURS AGO -- SCOPING IS NECESSARY, NOT\nSUFFICIENT.  Worse: it REHABILITATES the diagnosis I recorded as REFUTED, and my refutation was a\nmisreading of my own brief. ***\n\nA `swpctx' design brief scoped ENTIRELY to `src/' was flagged at 145 KB -- the flag fired while it was\ngrepping `debugger_cmds.c', which is INSIDE the allowed scope.  All six runs, sorted by what they asked:\n\n    WHAT IS TRUE                                    WHAT TO BUILD\n    analysis of a defect        317 KB clean        smallest EDIT that RE-OPENS     375 KB FLAGGED\n    count + classify            298 KB clean        smallest guest PROGRAM that\n    is this path reached?       294 KB clean          WITNESSES each defect         145 KB FLAGGED\n    CAN a guest run at all?     502 KB clean\n\n**The discriminator is whether the brief asks the seat to BUILD SOMETHING THAT TRIGGERS A DEFECT.**  The\n502 KB clean run asked at length about cold-debugger probes, breakpoints and planting instructions -- and\nwas fine, because it asked whether such a thing is POSSIBLE rather than for one aimed at a defect.\n\n*** AND I HAD THIS RIGHT THE FIRST TIME. ***  This row's original diagnosis was *the reach-the-defect\nFRAMING is the trigger*.  I recorded it REFUTED because a read-only SCOPING brief was also refused --\nbut that brief's Q3 asked *\"what is the smallest change to the model that would restore the old\nbehaviour ... while still satisfying a test built that way?\"*  **That is construction framing, and I did\nnot re-read my own brief before declaring the diagnosis dead.**  The premise-audit failure again, on my\nown record rather than on the code.\n\n**THE ARCHIVE FINDING IS NOT SUPERSEDED** -- it carried a run past the exact byte where an unscoped one\ndied, which is a measurement and stands.  There are TWO triggers; §C5 shipped implying one, and now says\nso.\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it costs something real:** codex cannot be asked for mutants or witness\nprograms.  It can CHECK a construction supplied to it, and it can say whether one is possible -- both\nmeasured clean.  **The construction itself goes to a measuring seat.**  That is a genuine capability\nloss, not a wording problem, and it should be weighed on the 9th alongside [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** SEVENTH RUN, AND A THIRD REFINEMENT: A BRIEF THAT ASKS `IS THIS TRUE' CAN STILL BE REFUSED IF ITS\nCONTENT IS AN EXPLOITATION CHAIN.  Immediate rejection at 8 KB. ***\n\nThe `sgilinearnegidx' verification brief obeyed both existing rules: neutral engineering language, reading\nscoped to `src/' and `regress/*.py', and it asked only for each link of a chain to be CHECKED -- explicitly\n*\"I want it attacked rather than confirmed\"*, with no construction requested anywhere.  **Refused on the\nprompt**, before any work, exactly like the first `kills'-vocabulary attempt.\n\n**What it contained that the six clean runs did not: a numbered seven-step guest-controlled chain ending in\na PHYSICAL write, plus questions about the reachable address range and about how far the guest can CONTROL\nthe value.**  The clean runs analysed defects, classified sites, traced reachability of a code path, and\nasked whether a probe is possible.  None of them laid out *guest does A, then B, then C, and thereby writes\nwhere it chooses*.\n\n> **So the discriminator is not the QUESTION FORM but the SHAPE OF THE CONTENT.  `What is true about this\n> code?' is clean; `here is a chain by which a guest reaches X -- how far does it go?' is not, however it is\n> phrased.**\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is the sharpest limit yet:** the seat cannot be used to review the very\nfindings that matter most in a hardening fork, once those findings have been assembled into a chain.  It can\ncheck the LINKS SEPARATELY -- *is `src_x' masked in the linear path?* is a clean question -- but not the\nchain as a chain.  **Split the chain into independent link questions, or verify it yourself.**  I verified\nthis one myself in three greps.\n\n*Recorded with the other two triggers rather than replacing them: vocabulary (prompt-level), archive reading\n(content-level, late), and now chain SHAPE (prompt-level).*\n\n*** THIRD RECORDED INSTANCE, 2026-09-07, AND IT CARRIES THE CLEANEST DISCRIMINATOR YET, because\nTWO briefs on the SAME SUBJECT MATTER passed on the SAME DAY and this one did not. ***\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/codex_witness_20260907.err`.  The `.txt` is **ZERO\nBYTES and the process exited 0** -- so an exit-status check scores it a success and a size check\nscores it a dead seat; only the `.err` names it:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: ...\n\n**THE CONTROLS ARE THE VALUABLE PART.  Same seat, same model, same effort, same day, same device\nfile, all three read-only:**\n\n    PASSED   brief_lcabadval_fixcheck.md      \"six value guards that END THE PROCESS\" -- inlined\n                                              fatal()+exit(1) verbatim, asked whether a proposed\n                                              DELETION was sound.  6,976-byte answer.\n    PASSED   brief_sgirandomcolour_scope.md   \"writes FOUR random() bytes to guest memory\",\n                                              \"uninitialised host stack reaches guest memory\",\n                                              asked for a reachability census.  7,946-byte answer.\n    REFUSED  brief_lcaconfbus1_witness.md     asked whether a proposed TEST CONSTRUCTION would\n                                              successfully REACH the termination, step by step.\n\n**So it is not the vocabulary and not the subject.**  \"Host kill\", \"exit(1)\", \"guest-drivable\",\n\"uninitialised stack into guest memory\" all pass.  What differs is the ASK: the first two ask *is\nthis analysis correct*; the third asks *will this sequence work*.  A numbered two-step recipe --\nwrite this value here, then touch that window, and the process exits -- reads as exploit validation\nhowever defensively it is framed, and mine was framed defensively (opened with \"ordinary regression-\ntest adequacy review\", no offensive-security wording anywhere).\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE: ask codex to VERIFY AN ANALYSIS, not to VALIDATE A SEQUENCE THAT REACHES A\n> TERMINATION.**  This is the carrier's existing C5 trigger -- *asking for a construction that\n> reaches a defect* -- with a measured negative control on both sides for the first time.  Where a\n> construction genuinely needs checking, ask about its CONTROLS in isolation (\"does a matched\n> surviving control at the same address attribute an outcome to the value?\") without narrating the\n> path to the kill.\n\n**RECOVERY, and it is the sixth-class rule applied: the seat refused, the QUESTION did not become\nunanswerable.**  grok reads the repo, takes its brief from a FILE (no argv cap), runs `--permission-\nmode plan` read-only, and has no such filter.  It was fired on the byte-identical brief.  A refused\nseat with a live alternate transport is ONE SEAT COVERED, not one lost -- but it is recorded as a\nREFUSAL by name, never as agreement or as a wall.\n\n*Cost note: 17,471 tokens spent before the flag fired, and 217 KB of tool-call reasoning in the\n`.err` showing it had already listed the tree and begun reading.  The refusal is not instant, so a\nzero-byte `.txt` after several minutes is NOT evidence of a hang.*",
