armbfxwidth32 state in work commits c0c7f55 hold_question "What should `bfi` do when msb < lsb?  Its width = msb - lsb + 1 spans -30..32, so 465 of the 1024 pairs give a strictly NEGATIVE shift count (496 is the msb < lsb population, i.e. width <= 0; the other 31 give width 0, which is defined) -- undefined by the same C99 clause as the too-large one.  ARM calls that encoding UNPREDICTABLE, and this fork's standing position is that an unpredictable GUEST encoding must not produce undefined behaviour in the HOST.  A codex pass examined 13 comparable decisions and found the tree answers this class INCONSISTENTLY: no-op has bitfield precedent, conditional UND has decoder precedent, cpu_arm_instr_loadstore.c:204 picks another defined result for an UNPREDICTABLE halfword load, and #312's own comment argues AGAINST halting.  It said plainly that nothing examined settles bfi uniquely.  AND THE OBVIOUS ROUTE IS WRONG AS WRITTEN: X(und) expects arg[0] to hold a PC OFFSET and bfi's holds a REGISTER POINTER.  #510 therefore fixes the three WITNESSED sites and leaves bfi alone rather than inventing a policy."
